\id JER EN_UST en_English_ltr Wed Sep 09 2020 10:57:58 GMT-0500 (Central Daylight Time) tc
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Jeremiah
\toc1 The Book of Jeremiah
\toc2 Jeremiah
\toc3 Jer
\mt1 Jeremiah
\c 1
\p
\v 1 {I,} Jeremiah son of Hilkiah, {wrote} these messages. {I am} a priest, from the town of Anathoth in the area where the tribe of Benjamin lives.
\v 2 Yahweh started to give me these messages when Josiah had been ruling Judah for almost thirteen years.
\v 3 Yahweh continued to give me these messages when Josiah’s son Jehoiakim was the king of Judah, and he continued to do that until Zedekiah, Josiah’s son, had been the king of Judah for eleven years. It was in the fifth month {of that year} that the people of Jerusalem were exiled {to Babylonia}.
\m
\v 4 {One day} Yahweh gave me this message. He said,
\q1 \v 5 “I chose you before I formed you in your mother’s womb.
\q2 I chose you to serve me in a special way before you were born.
\q2 I appointed you to be {my} prophet {whose messages would be} for people from {all} nations.”
\p
\v 6 I replied, “O, Yahweh my God, notice that I am not {a good public} speaker, because I am quite young!”
\m
\v 7 Yahweh replied, “Do not say that you are too young, because you must go to everyone to whom I will send you, and you must tell them everything that I tell you to say.
\v 8 You must not be afraid of the people to whom you will speak, because I will protect you so that they do not harm you.” This will surely happen because Yahweh has said it!
\m
\v 9 Then Yahweh touched my mouth with his hand and said to me, “Listen to me! I have put my message into your mouth.
\v 10 Understand this: today I am appointing you to warn nations and kingdoms. You will tell them that I will completely destroy and get rid of some of them and that I will establish others and cause them to be prosperous.”
\m
\v 11 Then Yahweh said to me, “Jeremiah, what do you see?”
\p I replied, “I see a branch from an almond tree.”
\p
\v 12 Yahweh said to me, “That is correct. And {because the word for ‘almond’ resembles the word for ‘watching’,} it means that I am watching {closely}, and I will make certain that what I have said to you will happen.”
\m
\v 13 Then Yahweh spoke to me again and said, “What do you see?”
\p I replied, “I see a pot full of boiling water. It is in the north, tipping {toward me}.”
\p
\v 14 Yahweh replied, “{It means that} from the north I will cause disastrous things to happen to all the people living in this land.
\q1 \v 15 Listen to what I say:
\q2 I am summoning the armies of the kingdoms that are north of Judah to come to Jerusalem.
\q1 Their kings will set up their thrones at the gates of this city {to indicate that they are now the kings of Judah}.
\q2 And {their armies} will attack and break down the walls of this city, and they will do the same thing to all the other cities in Judah.
\q1 \v 16 I will declare how I am going to punish my people for all the evil things that they have done;
\q1 they have abandoned me and have burned incense {as an offering} to false gods.
\q2 They have worshiped idols that they have made with their own hands!
\p
\v 17 So, you must get ready for action! Then go to the people of Judah and tell them everything that I tell you to say! Do not be afraid of them, because if you are afraid of them, I will punish you {as an example} right in front of them!
\v 18 But listen! Today I will cause you to be {strong, like} a city that has strong walls around it. You will be {as strong as} an iron pillar or a bronze wall. None of the kings of Judah or its officials, its priests, or any of its people will be able to defeat you.
\v 19 They will oppose you, but they will not be able to defeat you, because I will be with you in order to protect you.” {This will surely happen because} Yahweh, has said it!
\c 2
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me {another} message. He told me
\v 2 that I should go and proclaim in the hearing of the people of Jerusalem. He said that I should tell them this.
\q1 “I, Yahweh, declare this: I remember in your favor, that long ago you faithfully loved me.
\q2 You loved me {and tried to please me} like a bride loves {and tries to please} her husband.
\q1 You followed me in the desert,
\q1 where no crops were sown.
\v 3 At that time you Israelites were set apart to me;
\q2 {you belonged to me like} the first part of the harvests belong to me.
\q1 I promised to punish all those who harmed you, my people,
\q2 and truly I did send disasters on them.
\q2 {That happened because I,} Yahweh, said {that it would happen.}”
\q1 \v 4 Listen to what Yahweh says you descendants of Jacob, yes, listen you people from every clan of Israel.
\p
\v 5 Yahweh says,
\q2 “Your ancestors stopped worshiping and obeying me even though I did no wrong to them.
\q1 They worshiped worthless idols,
\q2 and they themselves became worthless.
\q1 \v 6 They did not say,
\q2 ‘We need Yahweh, who brought us {safely} out of Egypt
\q1 and led us through a desert where there were a lot of pits;
\q2 where there was no water and where it was very dangerous.
\q1 {He led us through a land} that no one travels through and where no one lives.’
\q1 \v 7 I brought you into a fruitful land,
\q2 in order that you could enjoy the fruit and other good things {that you would harvest}.
\q1 But when you came, your sinful behavior caused the land that I promised to give to you to be unfit for me
\q2 and to become detestable to me.
\q1 \v 8 Your priests did not say,
\q2 ‘Yahweh should be helping us now!’
\q1 Those who teach my laws do not understand or truly know me
\q2 and your leaders have rebelled against me.
\q1 Your prophets gave you messages they said were from {their god} Baal,
\q2 and they worshiped worthless idols.
\q1 \v 9 So I will argue my case with you.”
\q1 I, Yahweh, declare,
\q1 “and I will argue my case with your grand-children.
\q1 \v 10 If you go {west} to the island of Cyprus,
\q2 or if you go {east} to the land of Kedar,
\q1 and if you ask people in those places,
\q2 they will tell you that no people from their countries have ever done the things that you people have done!
\q1 \v 11 The people of no other nation have ever abandoned their gods (even though they are not gods at all)!
\q2 But my people have abandoned me, their glorious God,
\q2 and now worship gods that profit them nothing.
\q1 \v 12 {It is as though everything in} the sky is dismayed about what you have done;
\q2 it is as though they tremble because they are very horrified!”
\q1 I, Yahweh, declare this {to you}.
\v 13 “{You,} my people, have done two evil things:
\q1 You have rejected me, the one who is {like} a fountain {where you can obtain} fresh water,
\q2 and you are worshiping gods that are {like} pits in the ground,
\q2 that are cracked and which are not able to hold any water.
\q1 \v 14 You Israelite people were certainly not servants {when you were born}, or {slaves} born in the house {in which they serve}!
\q2 But now you have been captured {by your enemies}!
\q1 \v 15 {Your enemies} roared at you {like} lions,
\q2 and they destroyed your land.
\q1 Now your towns have been burned,
\q2 and no one lives in them.
\q1 \v 16 Soldiers from Memphis and Tahpenes{, cities in Egypt,} have defeated you
\q2 {and} shaved your heads {to show that you are their slaves}.
\q1 \v 17 It is because you abandoned {me,} Yahweh your God, as I was leading you in the way you should go,
\q2 that these disasters have happened to you!
\q1 \v 18 It certainly will not help you to make an alliance with the rulers of Egypt!
\q2 It certainly will not help you to make an alliance with the rulers of Assyria!
\q1 \v 19 It is because you have done wicked things that I will punish you.
\q2 It is because you have turned away from me that I will condemn you.
\q1 When I do that, you will realize that bitter and evil things will happen to you because you have forsaken me, Yahweh, your God,
\q2 and you no longer have an awesome respect for me.
\q1 That will certainly happen because I, the Lord, Yahweh, commander of the angel armies in heaven, have said it.
\q1 \v 20 Long ago, I delivered you from your slavery {in the land of Egypt}.
\q2 But you said, ‘I will not worship {Yahweh}!’
\q1 Instead, you worship idols that are under green trees on the top of every hill.
\q2 You {love and worship those idols instead of me like} a woman who is not faithful to her husband and instead becomes a prostitute.
\q1 \v 21 {It is as though} you were a grapevine,
\q2 that I planted when it was a cutting from a very good vine.
\q1 So now it is disgusting that you have changed your behavior toward me and have become {like} a rotten worthless vine!
\q1 \v 22 Your guilt from your sinful behavior is like very bad stains {on a cloth,}
\q2 and you cannot get rid of those stains even by using very strong soap.
\q1 {This is true because I,} Lord Yahweh, have said it.
\q1 \v 23 You should not say, ‘I am acceptable to God;
\q1 and have not worshiped the images of Baal.’
\q2 But think about the disgusting things that you do {very eagerly in the Hinnom} Valley {outside Jerusalem}.
\q2 {Know that you are like} fast female camels running aimlessly all about to find male camels to mate with.
\q1 \v 24 {You are like} a wild female donkey that is accustomed to the wilderness.
\q2 Because that wild donkey desires to mate she sniffs the air {to find where the male donkeys are}.
\q2 At the time that, that female donkey is in heat there is no one who can restrain her lust.
\q1 None of the male donkeys that desire to mate with her become tired searching for her,
\q2 because at mating time they find her.
\q1 \v 25 I told you to stop running here and there {to find idols to worship,} with the result that your sandals have become worn out,
\q2 and your throats have become dry.
\q1 {I told you to stop doing that} but you said,
\q2 ‘We cannot stop, because we love foreign gods,
\q2 and we must worship them!
\q1 \v 26 As a robber is disgraced when he is caught,
\q2 so all of you Israelites, including your kings, princes, priests and prophets, are similarly disgraced.
\q1 \v 27 You say to {a piece of} wood {that is carved to become an idol,} ‘You are our father!’
\q2 And you say to a stone {that you have set up}, ‘You are our mother!’
\q1 You have rejected me,
\q2 but when you experience troubles,
\q1 you say, ‘Take action and save us!’
\q1 \v 28 Take notice that the {false} gods that you made for yourself are not helping you.
\q2 Let them take action, if they are able, and rescue you,
\q2 when you need help.
\q1 For you, people of Judah, have as many {false} gods as you have cities!
\q1 \v 29 You should not complain that it was wrong for me {not to have rescued you,}
\q2 since you have all rebelled against me.
\q1 This is what, I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 30 I punished some of you,
\q2 but you did not learn anything from my doing that.
\q1 Instead you have killed many of the prophets {that I sent to you},
\q2 like fierce lions kill {other animals}.
\p
\v 31 You people of Israel, pay attention to what I, Yahweh, say!
\q1 I have certainly not been {like} a desert to you my people, Israel,
\q2 and I have certainly not been {like} a very dark land {to you}.
\q1 You, my people, should not say that you are free from my control,
\q2 and that you will not return to worship me anymore!
\q1 \v 32 A young, unmarried woman would certainly never forget to wear her jewelry,
\q2 and a bride would {never forget to wear} her wedding dress!
\q1 But you, my people, have forgotten me for many years.
\q1 \v 33 You know how to easily find {gods from other countries} whom you can love.
\q2 So, even prostitutes could learn from you.
\q1 \v 34 You also have the blood of poor, innocent people on your clothes whom you have murdered,
\q2 {even though} you did not catch them robbing you!
\q1 But in spite of all of these things,
\q1 \v 35 you still say, ‘I have done nothing wrong,
\q2 so I am sure that Yahweh will not be angry with me.’
\q1 But know that I will punish you
\q2 for claiming that you have not sinned.
\q1 \v 36 It is ridiculous how you go about constantly changing who you ask for help!
\q1 Previously you requested {the army of} Assyria to help you,
\q2 but they were not able to help you.
\q1 Now you have requested {the army of} Egypt to help you,
\q2 but they will not be able to help you, either.
\q1 \v 37 They will capture you, and you will be their prisoners,
\q2 {led to Egypt, very ashamed} with your hands on your heads.
\q1 That will happen because I, Yahweh, have rejected those nations that you are relying on,
\q2 and they will not be able to help you at all.”
\c 3
\q1 \v 1 “{Moses wrote} that if a man divorces his wife and then she marries another man,
\q2 her first husband certainly must not take her back again {to be his wife},
\q1 because that would certainly cause the whole nation to become unacceptable to me.
\q1 This nation is like this woman.
\q1 {Instead of being faithful to me} you have been {like} a prostitute who has had sex with many men.
\q2 {Because you have been like a prostitute} it makes me not want to accept you if you return to me.”
\q1 This is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\v 2 “Look up at the barren hilltops.
\q2 On every hilltop you have worshiped idols.
\q1 {It is as though} you have sat along the roadsides like a prostitute waiting to have sex with those who pass by.
\q2 {It is as though} you have sat along the roadsides like an Arab, waiting {to attack and steal things from} those who pass by.
\q1 Because of worshiping idols and all the other wicked things that you have done, you have caused the {entire} land to become unacceptable to me.
\q1 \v 3 That is why I have not sent you any rain at the times of the year when you needed it.
\q1 But you are like prostitutes
\q2 who are not at all ashamed for what they have done.
\q1 \v 4 Now each of you says to me, ‘You are my father!
\q2 You have loved me ever since I was young!
\q1 \v 5 So surely Yahweh will not be angry with me forever!’
\q1 This is what you have said,
\q2 but you keep on sinning as much as you can!”
\p
\v 6 {One day} when Josiah was the king of Judah, Yahweh said to me, “Have you seen what the people of Israel have done? They have turned away from me, like a woman who has abandoned her husband and sleeps with other men. They have gone up on every hilltop and under every green tree and worshiped idols there.
\v 7 I thought that after they did all these things they would return to me, but they did not. And the treacherous people of Judah, who also descended from their forefather Jacob, were watching all of this.
\v 8 I observed that it was because the unfaithful people of Israel worshiped idols that I sent them away {as captives,} as a man writes a note saying that he is divorcing his wife and then sends her away because she has committed adultery. The people of Judah, who also descended from Jacob, acted treacherously {towards me} like the people of Israel, and they did not respect me, and were unfaithful to me like a woman who abandons her husband and goes searching for other men to have sex with.
\v 9 They took the sin of worshiping idols lightly, so they have made the {entire} land unacceptable to me by worshiping idols made of stone and wood.
\v 10 In spite of all this, the treacherous people of Judah, who also descended from Jacob, have not sincerely returned to me, but they have only pretended to. {This is true because I,} Yahweh, have said it.”
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh said to me, “The people of Israel have turned away from me, but what the treacherous people of Judah have done is worse.
\v 12 Go and tell this to the people of Israel,
\q1 ‘Yahweh says this to you Israelite people who have turned away from him:
\q1 “I am merciful.
\q2 I will not continue to be angry with you forever.
\q2 So return to me.
\q1 \v 13 But you must admit that you are guilty,
\q2 and have rebelled against me, Yahweh, your God,
\q1 that you have worshiped idols under green trees everywhere,
\q2 and you have not obeyed me.’”
\q1 This is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\v 14 “Begin loving and obeying me again, my unfaithful people,
\q2 because you belong to me!
\q2 I will take you, some of you, {from the countries from which you were exiled}
\q2 and bring you back to Jerusalem.
\q1 \v 15 If you do that, I will appoint for you leaders with whom I am pleased,
\q2 leaders who will guide you well because they will know and understand {what pleases me}.
\q1 \v 16 And when you become very numerous in your land,
\q2 you will not need to talk about the Sacred Chest of Yahweh that contained the Ten Commandments.
\q1 You will not think about it or remember it,
\q2 and you will not want to make a new one.
\q1 \v 17 At that time people will say that I rule from Jerusalem.
\q1 People from all nations will come there to worship me,
\q2 and they will no longer stubbornly do the evil things that they desire in their inner beings.
\q1 \v 18 At that time the people of Judah and the people of Israel will return from their exile in lands to the northeast.
\q2 They will return to the land that I gave to their ancestors to posses.
\p
\v 19 I, Yahweh, wanted to make you people of Israel, like children to me.
\q1 I wanted to give you this delightful land.
\q2 It is a land more desirable than the land of any other nation!
\q1 I wanted you to call me your father,
\q2 and I wanted you to never stop obeying me.
\q1 \v 20 Surely you people of Israel have abandoned me like wives who have abandoned their husbands.’”
\q2 {That is what} Yahweh said.
\q1 \v 21 People will hear a noise on the barren hilltops.
\q2 It will be the noise made by the people of Israel weeping and pleading {for God to be merciful to them}.
\q1 They will be admitting that they stopped paying attention to Yahweh their God,
\q2 and that they stopped behaving as God wanted them to.
\q1 \v 22 {Yahweh will say to them,} “You Israelite people, start loving and obeying me again!
\q2 {If you do that,} I will cause you to no longer be unfaithful to me.”
\q1 {The people will reply,} “Yes indeed, we will begin loving and obeying you again,
\q2 because you are Yahweh, our God.
\q1 \v 23 Surely we did not get any help from {the idols we worshiped on} the hills;
\q2 we did not get any help from making all that noise on the mountains.
\q1 Surely, {only} Yahweh our God is the only one who can rescue us Israelite people!
\q1 \v 24 From the time when we were young, the shameful {god Baal} has taken away from us everything that our ancestors worked hard to acquire.
\q2 He has taken away their flocks of sheep and herds of cattle, their sons and their daughters.
\q1 \v 25 So, now we should lie down feeling very ashamed,
\q2 because we and our ancestors have sinned against Yahweh our God,
\q1 and we have not obeyed him at any time.”
\c 4
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh says, “If you Israelite people will again love and obey me,
\q2 and if you get rid of your detestable idols,
\q2 and if you do not act unfaithfully toward me,
\q1 \v 2 and {if}, when you make promises in my name by saying, ‘Just as surely as Yahweh lives,’
\q1 you make those promises truthfully, justly, and with righteous intentions,
\q2 then the {people in the other} nations will request that I bless them {as I have blessed you},
\q1 and they will praise me.”
\p
\v 3 This is what Yahweh says to the people of Jerusalem and the other cities in Judah,
\q1 “Prepare your inner beings to be ready to receive my messages
\q2 like farmers plow up hard ground so that they can plant seed in it.
\q1 Just as farmers do not waste good seed by sowing seeds among thorny plants,
\q2 so I do not want to waste my time telling you messages that you are not ready to receive.
\q1 \v 4 Make your thoughts and motives acceptable to me,
\q2 people of Judah and those who live in Jerusalem.
\q2 If you do not do that, my being angry with you
\q2 because of {all} the sins that you have committed
\q2 will be like a fire that will be impossible to extinguish.
\q1 \v 5 Declare this {to all the people} in Jerusalem and {the rest of} Judea;
\q2 blow the trumpets everywhere in the land to warn the people.
\q1 Tell them that they should flee to
\q2 the cities that have high walls around them.
\q1 \v 6 Shout to the people of Jerusalem,
\q1 ‘Flee to a safe place now! Do not delay,
\q2 because I am about to cause you to experience a terrible disaster
\q2 that will come from the north.
\q1 \v 7 An army that has destroyed many nations will attack you
\q2 {like} a lion that comes out of its den {to attack other animals}.
\q1 The soldiers of that army have taken down their tents
\q2 and they are already marching toward your land.
\q2 They will destroy your cities and leave them without any people still living in them.’
\p
\v 8 So, put on clothes that show that you are very sad.
\q1 Weep and wail
\q2 {to show that you are very sorry for what you have done},
\q2 because Yahweh is still very angry with us.
\q1 \v 9 Yahweh said that at the time when he punishes you, the king of Judah and all his officials will be very afraid,
\q2 and the priests and the prophets will be terrified.
\p
\v 10 Then I replied, “Oh, Yahweh {my} God, you completely deceived this people and the people of Jerusalem by {the false prophets} telling them that there would be peace in Jerusalem, but now our enemies are ready to slaughter us with their swords!”
\p
\v 11 When that happens, Yahweh will say to the people of Judah and Jerusalem,
\q1 “A huge army will come to attack you.
\q2 They will not be {like} a gentle breeze that separates the wheat from the chaff.
\q1 They will be {like} a very hot wind that blows in from the desert.
\q1 \v 12 They will be {like} a strong wind that I will send.
\q2 Now I am declaring that I will punish you.”
\q1 \v 13 {Our enemies} are about to advance {against us} like clouds and their chariots are like whirlwinds.
\q2 Their horses are faster than eagles.
\q1 It will be terrible for us!
\q1 \v 14 You people of Jerusalem, cleanse your inner beings of evil thoughts and intentions
\q2 in order that Yahweh will rescue you.
\q1 How long will you continue to think about wicked things?
\q1 \v 15 From the city of Dan {in the far north} to the hills of Ephraim {a few miles north of Jerusalem}, messengers are proclaiming that disasters are coming.
\q1 \v 16 Tell this to the people in other nations,
\q2 {but also} announce it in Jerusalem:
\q1 Yahweh says “An army is coming to Jerusalem from far away;
\q2 they will shout a battle cry against the cities in Judah.
\q1 \v 17 They will set up tents around Jerusalem like people set up temporary shelters around a field {at harvest time}.
\q2 That will happen because the people of Judah have rebelled against me.”
\q1 \v 18 Your own actions are causing those things to happen to you
\q2 because of the evil things that you have done.
\q1 How extremely unpleasant it is!
\q2 It will be so bad that it will effect your inner being!”
\q1 \v 19 Then I said to myself, “I am extremely anguished;
\q2 the pain in my inner being is very severe.
\q2 My heart beats wildly!
\q1 But I cannot remain silent
\q2 because I have heard {our enemies} blowing their trumpets
\q2 to announce that the battle {against Judah will start immediately}.
\q1 \v 20 Disasters will occur one after another
\q2 until the whole land is ruined.
\q1 Suddenly all our tents will be destroyed;
\q2 and the curtains inside the tents will be ripped apart.
\q1 \v 21 How long will this battle continue?
\q2 How long will I continue to see the enemy battle flags
\q2 and hear the sound of their trumpets being blown?”
\q1 \v 22 {Yahweh says:} “My people are foolish!
\q2 They do not have a relationship with me.
\q1 They are like stupid children
\q2 who do not understand anything.
\q1 They very cleverly do what is wrong,
\q2 but they do not know how to do what is good.”
\q1 \v 23 {God gave me a vision in which} I saw
\q2 that the earth was barren and without form.
\q1 I looked at the sky,
\q2 and there was no light there.
\q1 \v 24 I looked at the mountains and hills,
\q2 and they shook and moved from side to side.
\q1 \v 25 I looked and saw that there were no more people
\q2 and all the birds had flown away.
\q1 \v 26 I looked and saw that the fields that previously were fertile had become a desert.
\q1 The cities were all ruined;
\q2 they had all been destroyed by Yahweh because he was extremely angry.”
\p
\v 27 This is what Yahweh is saying,
\q1 “The entire land of Judah will be ruined,
\q2 but I will not destroy it completely.
\q1 \v 28 I will do to my people what I said that I would do,
\q2 and I will not change my mind.
\q1 {So when that happens, it will be as though} the earth will mourn
\q2 and the sky will become very dark.”
\q1 \v 29 When the people hear the sound of the calvary and the archers {from the enemy army},
\q2 they will flee from all of their cities.
\q1 Some of them will find places to hide in the bushes,
\q2 and others will run toward the hills {to escape being killed by their enemies}.
\q1 All the cities {in} Judah will be abandoned
\q2 and not one person will remain in them.
\q1 \v 30 So you who will surely be destroyed,
\q2 what will you do then?
\q2 Even though you have worn beautiful clothes and jewelry
\q2 and paint around your eyes,
\q1 those things will not help you,
\q2 because the people in other countries that you think love you really despise you,
\q2 and they will try to kill you.
\q1 \v 31 {It is as though} I already hear {the people in Jerusalem} crying very loudly,
\q2 as a woman cries when she is giving birth to her first child;
\q1 she gasps for breath and pleads for someone to help her.
\q2 {It is as though the people in Jerusalem} are saying, “Something terrible is happening to me! I am very afraid {because they are about to} murder me!”
\c 5
\q1 \v 1 {Yahweh said to the people of Jerusalem,} “Go up and down every street in Jerusalem.
\q2 Search in the city squares to find
\q2 people who do what is just and right,
\q1 If you can find {even} one person like that,
\q2 then I will forgive the people of Jerusalem {and not destroy their city}.
\q1 \v 2 But when the people there say, ‘I solemnly declare that as surely as Yahweh lives, {I will do what he says,’}
\q1 they are lying.”
\q1 \v 3 “Yahweh, you are certainly searching for people who are faithful and truthful.
\q2 You punished them, but they did not pay any attention.
\q1 You caused them to experience such difficult things that it is as if you were crushing them, but they ignored what you were trying to teach them.
\q1 They were extremely stubborn
\q2 and refused to change their attitude and begin obeying you.”
\q1 \v 4 Then I thought, “{We cannot expect these people to act righteously, because} they are poor;
\q2 they do not have any sense.
\q1 They do not know the way Yahweh wants them to conduct their lives;
\q2 they do not know what God requires them to do.
\q1 \v 5 So, I will go and talk to their leaders,
\q2 because they surely know how Yahweh their god wants them to conduct their lives.”
\q1 But they also have stopped obeying Yahweh,
\q2 and they will not allow him to lead them.
\q1 \v 6 Because of that, {their enemies} will kill them {like} lions coming out of the forests;
\q2 and will attack them {like} wolves from the desert;
\q2 {and will be like} leopards that lurk outside their cities that maul anyone who walks outside the cities.
\q1 {Those things will happen} because the people have sinned very much against God
\q2 and have been unfaithful to him many times.
\q1 \v 7 {Yahweh says,} “I cannot forgive these people!
\q2 Their children have abandoned me.
\q1 and when they solemnly declare something, they ask their false gods {to show that what they say is true.}
\q1 I gave my people everything that they needed,
\q2 but they often went to {the shrines of their idols} and committed adultery there.
\q1 \v 8 {Just like} well-fed male horses who neigh, wanting to have sex with female horses,
\q2 each of the men desires to have sex with his neighbor’s wife.
\q1 \v 9 I should surely punish them for this!
\q2 I will certainly get revenge on this nation whose people behave like that!
\q1 \v 10 {I will say to your enemies, ‘The people of Judah and Israel are like a vineyard.}
\q1 Go among them as you would go down rows in a vineyard
\q1 and get rid of most of the people,
\q2 but do not kill all of them.
\q1 These people do not belong to me,
\q1 so get rid of them,
\q2 like a gardener lops off branches from a vine.
\q1 \v 11 The people of Israel and Judah have been very unfaithful to me.”
\q1 This is what Yahweh declares.
\q1 \v 12 They have spoken falsely about him and said,
\q2 “He will not punish us!
\q2 He will not send disasters on us!
\q2 We will not experience wars or famines!
\q1 \v 13 What the prophets say is nothing but wind!
\q2 They do not have messages {from God}!
\q2 So may the disasters that they predict happen to them!”
\p
\v 14 So, this is what Yahweh, the commander of the armies of angels in heaven, has said to me:
\q1 “Because my people are saying those things,
\q2 I will give you a message to tell them that will be {like} a fire,
\q2 and these people {will be like} wood that the fire will burn up.”
\q1 \v 15 Listen to Yahweh’s declaration, you people of Israel:
\q1 “I will bring {the army of} a distant nation to attack you.
\q2 It is a very powerful nation that has existed for a long time.
\q1 They speak a language that you do not know,
\q2 and which you will not be able to understand.
\q1 \v 16 Their soldiers are all very strong,
\q2 and the arrows from their quivers will cause many Israelite people to die.
\q1 \v 17 They will eat the food that you have harvested from your fields, and eat your bread.
\q2 They will kill your sons and daughters,
\q1 and they will kill your flocks of sheep and herds of cattle.
\q2 They will eat your grapes and your figs.
\q1 Your cities, that have high walls around them, that you trusted in {to keep you safe}, they will destroy
\q2 and will kill the people in them with their swords.
\p
\v 18 But even when those things happen, I will not get rid of all of you.
\q1 \v 19 And when the survivors ask, ‘Why is Yahweh our God doing this to us?’
\q2 you will tell them, ‘Just as you rejected him and worshiped foreign gods in your land,
\q2 so {now} you will become slaves of foreigners in a land that is not your land.’”
\q1 \v 20 {Yahweh instructed me:} Proclaim this to the people of Israel and Judah:
\q1 \v 21 “Listen to this, you foolish and senseless people!
\q2 You have eyes, but {it is as though} you cannot see.
\q1 Yes, you have ears, but {it is as though} you cannot hear.
\q1 \v 22 I, Yahweh, declare, that you should revere me
\q2 and tremble when you are in my presence!
\q1 {You should do those things} because I, Yahweh, am the one who put a barrier along the shores
\q2 so that the waters of the ocean cannot cross it {and flood the land}.
\q1 The waves roll and roar, but they still cannot go past that barrier.
\q1 \v 23 But you people {are not like the waves that obey me}.
\q2 You people are very stubborn and rebellious,
\q2 and have constantly chosen not to obey me.
\q1 \v 24 You do not think to yourselves,
\q2 ‘Let us now greatly revere Yahweh our God,
\q2 the one who sends us rain at the times that we need it,
\q2 the one who causes the grain to become ripe at the harvest season.’
\q1 \v 25 It is because of the wrong things that you have done, that those good things have not happened.
\q2 It is because of the sins that you have committed that Yahweh has not given you those good things.
\q1 \v 26 Among my people are wicked people who hide along the roads to ambush people
\q2 like men who hunt birds set traps to catch them.
\q1 \v 27 Like {a hunter has} a cage full of birds {that he has captured},
\q2 so their homes are full of things that they have gotten by deceiving others.
\q1 So now they are rich and powerful.
\q1 \v 28 They are big and fat,
\q2 and there is no limit to the evil deeds that they have done.
\q2 They do not try to defend orphans in the courtrooms.
\q2 They themselves are rich but they do not help poor people to get what they have a right to receive.
\q1 \v 29 So I will certainly punish them for doing those things.
\q2 I, Yahweh, declare that I will certainly give their nation what they deserve for acting like they have.
\q1 \v 30 Very appalling things are happening in this country.
\q1 \v 31 Prophets speak {only} lies
\q1 and priests rule by their own authority,
\q2 and you people like that!
\q1 But when the time comes for Yahweh to judge you, you will have no one to help you!
\c 6
\q1 \v 1 You people in Jerusalem who are from the tribe of Benjamin,
\q2 flee {from this city}!
\q1 Blow the trumpets in the city of Tekoa {south of Jerusalem}!
\q1 Send up a smoke signal in the town of Beth Haccherem
\q2 {to warn the people of the coming danger}!
\q1 A powerful army will come from the north,
\q2 and they will cause great destruction.
\q1 \v 2 Jerusalem is like a beautiful and delicate woman.
\q1 \v 3 Enemy kings, {who are like} the shepherds {of foreign people groups}, will come to Jerusalem with their armies{, who are like their flocks} and set up their tents around the city.
\q2 Then each {king will choose a part of the city for his soldiers to destroy as} shepherds divide their pastures for their flocks of sheep to graze in.
\q1 \v 4 {The kings will tell their troops,}
\q2 ‘Get ready for the battle by making the necessary sacrifices.
\q2 We should attack them before noontime.
\q1 But {if we arrive there late in the afternoon,} when the shadows are becoming long,
\q1 \v 5 we will attack them at night
\q2 and tear down their fortresses.’”
\p
\v 6 Yahweh, the commander of the armies of angels, says this,
\q1 “{I will command those soldiers to} cut down the trees {outside Jerusalem}
\q2 and to build dirt ramps up to the top of the city walls {so that they can enter Jerusalem}.
\q1 This city must be punished
\q2 because the people who live there continually oppress others.
\q1 \v 7 Its people keep doing wicked deeds,
\q2 just as a well keeps producing water.
\q1 I hear within Jerusalem the noise {people doing} violent and oppressive actions.
\q2 I continually see {people who are} suffering and wounded.
\q1 \v 8 Pay attention to my warning, you people of Jerusalem,
\q2 because if you do not listen, I will reject you
\q1 and cause your land to become desolate,
\q2 a land where no one lives.”
\p
\v 9 Yahweh, the commander of the angel armies, also says this:
\q2 “{I will tell your enemies} to cause your country to become as desolate as a vineyard from which all the grapes have been completely stripped from the vines.
\q1 {Their soldiers will seize the possessions of those} who remain in Israel {after the others have been exiled}
\q2 like farmers go to the vines again to pick any grapes that were not picked.”
\q1 \v 10 {Then I said,} “If I speak {to the Israelite people} to warn them, no one would listen to me.
\q2 Certainly, the Israelite people are very stubborn and they refuse to pay attention!
\q1 Certainly, they scorn the message Yahweh gave me to tell them;
\q2 they would not be happy to receive it.
\q1 \v 11 {So} now I am extremely angry, like Yahweh is angry,
\q2 and I cannot restrain it any longer.”
\q1 Yahweh said to me,
\q2 “Tell everyone that you are very angry with them.
\q2 Tell the children in the streets and the young men who gather together.
\q1 Tell the men and their wives that Yahweh is angry with them also,
\q2 and tell the very old people.
\q1 \v 12 {Tell the men that} I will give their houses to their enemies,
\q2 and I will give their property and their wives to them, also,
\q1 when I punish the people who live in this land!
\q2 {This will surely happen because} Yahweh has said it.
\q1 \v 13 Everyone is trying to get money by tricking others,
\q2 from the least important people to the most important people.
\q1 Even the prophets and the priests are trying to deceive {people to get money}.
\q1 \v 14 They act as though the sins of my people are like small wounds that they do not need to put bandages on.
\q1 They tell the people that everything will go well with them, but that is not true; things will not go well for them.
\q1 \v 15 They should be ashamed about the disgusting things that they do,
\q1 but they are not ashamed at all.
\q2 They have become so accustomed to sinning that they are not remorseful.
\q1 So, they also will be among those who will be killed.
\q1 They will be destroyed when I punish them.”
\q1 This is what Yahweh says.
\p
\v 16 This is {also} what Yahweh said {to the Israelite people},
\q1 “Stand at the crossroads and look {at the people who pass by}.
\q2 Ask them what was the good behavior that their ancestors had long ago.
\q1 And {when they tell you how their ancestors behaved,} behave that way.
\q2 If you do that, you feel peaceful in your inner beings.”
\q1 But you replied, “{No} we do not want to do that!”
\q1 \v 17 I sent my prophets who were like watchmen.
\q2 I, Yahweh, told you to listen carefully to my warning which is like the warning city watchmen give when they blow trumpets {to warn the people of the city that enemies are approaching},
\q2 but you said, “{No,} we do not want to listen.”
\q1 \v 18 Therefore, you people in the other nations, listen to this:
\q2 “Pay attention to what is going to happen to the Israelite people.
\q1 \v 19 Listen, all of you people of the earth!
\q2 I am going to cause disasters to happen to the Israelite people.
\q2 I will do this to them because they have refused to obey what I commanded them.
\q2 They have refused to obey my laws.
\q1 \v 20 {You Israelite people,} when you burn frankincense that came from the {far away} land of Sheba
\q2 and {when you offer to me} sweet-smelling anointing oil that came from far away,
\q2 I will not be pleased with your sacrifices.
\q1 I will not accept the sacrifices that are completely burned on the altar;
\q2 I am not pleased with {any of} your sacrifices.
\p
\v 21 Therefore, I, Yahweh, will summon enemy armies to attack my people so that it will be as if I was putting obstacles {on the roads on which my people travel}.
\q2 These invading armies will strike men and their sons and neighbors and friends so that they all fall over and die as if they had stumbled over obstacles in their path.
\q1 Everyone will die.”
\p
\v 22 Yahweh {also} says this,
\q1 “You will see an army marching toward you from the north.
\q2 {The army of} a great nation very far away is preparing {to attack you}.
\q1 \v 23 They have bows {and arrows} and spears;
\q2 they are cruel, and do not act mercifully {to anyone}.
\q1 As they ride along on their horses,
\q2 the horses’ feet sound like the roaring of the ocean {waves};
\q1 they are on horses and are riding in battle formation
\q2 to attack you people of Jerusalem.”
\q1 \v 24 {The people of Jerusalem say,}
\q1 “We have heard reports about the enemy;
\q2 {so} we are very frightened, with the result that we feel weak.
\q1 We are very afraid, and worried,
\q2 like women who are about to give birth to babies.
\q1 \v 25 {So one person says to another,} ‘Do not go out into the fields! Do not go on the roads,
\q1 because the enemy {soldiers} will attack you with their swords, and they are everywhere;
\q2 they are coming from all directions, and we are extremely afraid.’”
\q1 \v 26 So I say,
\q1 “My dear people, put on rough textured clothes and sit in ashes
\q1 {to show how much you are grieving over the many sinful things you have done}.
\q1 Mourn loudly and cry very much,
\q2 {as parents would cry} if their only son died.
\q1 For destroying armies will attack you suddenly.”
\p
\v 27 {Then Yahweh said to me,
\q1 “Jeremiah,} I have caused you to become to my people {like} someone who heats metal very hot {to completely burn the impurities}.
\q2 You will examine my people’s behavior.
\q1 \v 28 You will find out that they are all stubborn rebels,
\q2 who slander others.
\q1 Each of them is so unyielding in their inner being that it is as if their inner beings are hard like bronze or iron.
\q2 They do corrupt things.
\q1 \v 29 {A metalworker causes} the bellows to blow very hard {to make the fire very hot and he adds} lead {in with the un-purified silver he wishes to purify so that} the lead will draw out and burn up with the impurities in the silver.
\q1 {However, the warnings I gave the Israelites through you} have been in vain. They have been like an unsuccessful attempt at purifying silver
\q2 because my words of warning have not caused them to stop doing evil things.
\q1 \v 30 I, Yahweh, have rejected them;
\q2 I say that they are like worthless silver.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said to me,
\v 2 “Go to the entrance of my temple and give this message {to the people} there: You people of Judah, who come here to worship Yahweh, listen to this message from Yahweh.
\v 3 This is what Yahweh, Israel’s God, the commander of the angel armies, says to you: ‘If you {stop doing evil things and} start doing what is right, I will allow you to remain living in your land.
\v 4 {But some people} are repeatedly saying to you, “The temple of Yahweh is here, {so we will be safe; he will not allow us and the temple to be destroyed.”} But do not believe what they say, because they are deceiving you.
\v 5 {I will act mercifully to you only} if you fully change your behavior and stop doing evil things, and if you {start} to act fairly toward others,
\v 6 and if you stop oppressing foreigners {who live in your country,} and orphans and widows, and if you stop killing innocent people in this land, and stop worshiping foreign gods which results in your own harm.
\v 7 If you do what I have told you, I will allow you to stay in this land that I promised to your ancestors long ago that it would belong to them {and their descendants} forever.
\p
\v 8 Pay attention! {People are repeatedly} telling you, {“The temple is here, so we are safe,”} and you are believing that what they are saying {is true}, but those people are deceiving you; {what they say is} worthless.
\v 9 You think that you can steal things, murder people, commit adultery, tell lies in court, and worship Baal and all those other gods that you did not know about previously.
\v 10 Then you think you can come here and stand before me in front of this temple, which is my temple, and say “Nothing bad will happen to us!”, so that you may {continue} to do all these disgusting things.
\v 11 I am appalled that you have made this temple, which is my temple, to become {like} a den where thieves hide! Know that I, Yahweh, see {all the evil things that you people do there}!’ This is what Yahweh has declared.
\p
\v 12 ‘Long ago I put my sacred tent in the city of Shiloh, to be a place where people would worship me. Think about how I destroyed it because my people, the Israelites, did many wicked things {there}.
\v 13 So now, because you did all those {evil} things, and I warned you about them many times, but you refused to listen and I called out to you, but you refused to answer me,
\v 14 therefore, just like I destroyed Shiloh, I will {now} destroy this temple that was built for people to worship me, this temple that you trust in, that is in this place that I gave to you and your ancestors.
\v 15 And I will expel you from my presence, just as I did to all your relatives, all the descendants of Ephraim.’
\p
\v 16 {Yahweh said to me, ‘Jeremiah,} do not pray for these people. Do not weep or pray on their behalf and do not plead for me {to help them}, because I will not respond to your request.
\v 17 Notice all the wicked things they are doing in the streets of Jerusalem and in the other cities in Judah!
\v 18 The children gather firewood and their fathers use it to make fires {on the altars to burn sacrifices}. The women knead dough to make cakes to offer to {their goddess Astarte who people call} the Queen of Heaven. And {on their altars} they pour out offerings of wine to their other idols. All of those things cause me to become extremely angry!
\v 19 But I am not the one whom they are hurting! They are really hurting themselves {by doing these things}, to their own shame!’
\v 20 So Yahweh the Lord says this: ‘Because I am extremely angry with {what happens at} this place, I will punish these people severely; my being very angry will be {like} a fire that will not be extinguished, and I will destroy the people and {their} animals and I will destroy {their} fruit trees and {their} crops.’
\p
\v 21 Therefore, this is what Yahweh, the commander of the armies of angels, the God of Israel, says: ‘Take away your offerings that you bring to burn completely on your altars and your {other} sacrifices; {do not give them to me;} eat them {yourselves}!
\v 22 When I led your ancestors out of Egypt, it was not offerings to be completely burned on the altar or {other} sacrifices that I wanted from them.
\v 23 But what I told them was, “Obey me; if you do that, I will be your God and you will be my people. If you do all the things that I command you to do, life will go well for you.”
\v 24 But your ancestors would not obey me or pay any attention to me. They continued to do the evil things that they wanted to do, everything that in their stubborn inner beings they desired to do. Instead of coming closer to me, they went further away from me.
\v 25 From the day that your ancestors left Egypt until now, I have continued to send my prophets to you.
\v 26 But you, my people, have not listened to me or paid attention to what I said; you have been stubborn, and you have done more sinful things than your ancestors did.’
\p
\v 27 {Then Yahweh said to me,} ‘You must tell all this to my people, but they will not obey {what} you {tell them}. You must call out {my warnings} to them, but they will not respond to you.
\v 28 You must say to them, “You people {of Judah} have not obeyed the instruction of Yahweh, your God; you have not accepted it when he tried to correct you. No one among you is truthful; you do not say anything that is true; {you speak only lies}.”
\q1 \v 29 {So, tell them to} cut off their hair and throw it away {to show that they are mourning}; tell them to go up into the barren hills and sing a mourning song,
\q1 because I have completely rejected this generation {of people} who have made me angry.’
\v 30 Yahweh says this: ‘The people of Judah have done many things that I say are evil. They have set up their disgusting idols in my temple and as a result they have defiled it.
\v 31 They have built altars at Topheth in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom {outside Jerusalem,} and they sacrifice their sons and daughters on those altars. I never commanded them to do that; it was not even in my thoughts!’
\v 32 Yahweh says, ‘So they should beware! There will be a time when that place will no longer be called Topheth or the Valley of the Son of Hinnom; instead, it will be called the Valley of Slaughter. There will be a huge number of people who will be buried there, with the result that there will be no space to bury more bodies.
\v 33 The corpses of my people that are {not buried and are left on the ground} will be eaten by vultures and wild animals, and there will be no one to chase them away.
\v 34 In the streets of Jerusalem and the cities of Judah I will cause there to be no one singing and laughing anymore; there will be no more joyful voices of bridegrooms and brides, because the land will be completely destroyed.’”
\c 8
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says, “At the time {that your corpses are scattered on the ground, your enemies will} break open the graves of your kings and other officials who lived in Judah and the graves of your priests and prophets and other people who lived in Jerusalem and bring out their bones.
\v 2 They will {dishonor those people by} scattering their bones on the ground under the sun and the moon and all the stars—those are the gods which my people loved, served, sought after, and worshiped. No one will gather up their bones and bury them {again}; they will remain scattered on the ground like dung.
\v 3 And all the people of this wicked nation {who are still alive and} whom I have exiled to other countries will prefer to die rather than to continue to stay alive in all those countries.” {That will happen because} Yahweh the commander of angel armies has said it.
\p
\v 4 {Yahweh said to me, “Jeremiah,} tell the people that this is what {I,} Yahweh, am saying to them: ‘You know that when people fall down, they get up again and
\q1 when people are going along a road and find out that they are walking on the wrong road, they go back {and find the correct road}!
\q1 \v 5 So why do these people of Jerusalem rebel against me and continue trusting in those idols?
\q1 They trust in those idols that have deceived them and refuse to change their behavior.
\q1 \v 6 I have listened carefully {to what they say,} but they do not say what they should say. Not one of them is sorry for having sinned.
\q1 No one says, “I have done wicked things.” They all are {sinning and} doing what they want to
\q1 as fast as a horse that is running into a battle.
\q1 \v 7 All the birds {that fly south for the cold season} know the time that they need to fly south,
\q1 and they all return at the right time the following year.
\q1 But my people {are not like those birds!} They do not know what {I,} Yahweh, require them to do.
\q1 \v 8 Your men who teach you the laws that Moses wrote have been saying false things {about those laws}.
\q1 So, they should not {continue} saying, “We are wise because we have the laws of Yahweh”!
\q1 \v 9 Those teachers, who think that they are wise, will be ashamed and dismayed when they are taken {to other countries by their enemies}.
\q1 Indeed, they rejected what {I,} Yahweh, told them, which shows that they are not wise!
\q1 \v 10 So, I will give their wives to other men; I will give their fields to {the enemy soldiers} who conquer them.
\q1 I will do this because all the people, the least important and those who are most important and everyone in between, are all trying to get money by tricking others.
\q1 Even the prophets and priests, they all do that.
\q1 \v 11 They act as though the sins of my people are not serious, like wounds that do not need to be cleaned and bandaged.
\q1 They tell the people that everything will go well with them, but that is not true; things will not go well with them.
\q1 \v 12 They should be ashamed when they do disgusting things, but they are not ashamed at all! They do not even know how to blush {about their sins}!
\q1 So, they will be killed, and their corpses also will lie among the corpses of others whose enemies have killed them. They will be killed when I, Yahweh, punish them.
\q1 \v 13 I will surely {allow their enemies to} take away the grapes and figs that the people would have harvested from their fields. Their fruit trees will all wither.
\q1 They will not receive {all} the blessings that I prepared for them.” {This will certainly happen because} Yahweh, has said it.
\q1 \v 14 {Then the people will say,} “We should not wait here {in these small towns}. We should go to the cities that have high walls around them, {but even if we do that} we will be killed there,
\q1 because Yahweh our God has decided that we must be destroyed; {it is as though} he has given us a cup of poisoned water to drink, because we sinned against him.
\q1 \v 15 We hoped that things would go well for us, but things have not gone well.
\q1 We hoped that bad things would not happen to us any more, but things that terrify us {are happening to us}.
\q1 \v 16 People {far north in Israel} in the city of Dan have already heard the snorting of the horses of our enemies {who are preparing to attack us.} Because of the sound of the neighing of their stallions {it is as though} the entire land {of Judah} is shaking.
\q1 Our enemies are coming to destroy our land and everything in it, the cities and the people who live in them.”
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh declares, “I will send those enemy soldiers to Judah, and they will be like poisonous snakes among you,
\q1 {like poisonous snakes} that no one will be able to stop them attacking you; they will attack you {like} snakes do{, and kill you}.”
\q1 \v 18 Then I, Jeremiah said, “I wish that in my grieving I could find something to comfort me! I am very sad in my inner being!
\q1 \v 19 Listen! Throughout our land, the people ask, ‘Has Yahweh abandoned Jerusalem?
\q1 Is {Yahweh,} our city’s king, no longer there?’” {Yahweh replies,} ‘My people have caused me to become very angry by {worshiping} idols and foreign gods!’
\q1 \v 20 {The people say,} “The harvest season is finished, the hot season has ended, but Yahweh has not rescued us {from our enemies}.”
\q1 \v 21 I, {Jeremiah} cry because {it is as if} my people have been crushed. I mourn, and I am completely dismayed.
\q1 \v 22 I ask, “Surely there is medicinal balm in the region of Gilead? Surely there are doctors there?”
\q1 But my people are very {spiritually} sick and their {spiritual} health is not restored.
\c 9
\q1 \v 1 I wish that my head was a spring of water, and that my eyes were a fountain producing tears.
\q1 Then I would cry day and night for all of my people who have been killed {by our enemies}.
\q1 \v 2 I wish that I could leave my people and go and live in a traveler’s lodging place in the desert {so that I could forget them}.
\q1 {I want to do this} because none of them have remained faithful {to Yahweh}; they are a company of people who deceive others.
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh says, “They bend their tongues to tell lies {like} people bend bows {to shoot arrows}.
\q1 They do not ensure that dealings are truthful in their land. They go about doing one wicked thing after another and they do not know me.”
\q1 \v 4 Nobody trust your neighbors or even your brothers,
\q1 because surely every brother lies and every neighbor slanders {others}.
\q1 \v 5 They deceive their friends and do not tell the truth.
\q1 They have taught themselves to be skillful liars; they wear themselves out with their frequent sinning.
\q1 \v 6 Jeremiah, everyone living around you is a deceiver. By deceiving themselves, they refuse to obey me.
\q1 \v 7 Therefore, Yahweh, commander of the angel armies, says this:
\q1 “Listen carefully to what I say: I will test my people, {like a metalworker} puts metal in a hot fire to completely burn out the impure bits.
\q1 For there is nothing else that I can do because of all {the evil things} that my people have done.
\q1 \v 8 The deceitful things they say harm people {like} poisoned arrows do.
\q1 They say to their neighbors, ‘I hope things will go well for you,’ while they are planning to kill them.
\q1 \v 9 I should surely punish them for doing those things!
\q1 Yes, I should certainly get revenge on the people of a nation that does things like that!”
\q1 \v 10 So, I will weep and wail for the mountains and lament for the places where the livestock eat the grass,
\q1 because those areas will be scorched so that no one will travel through them.
\q1 There will be no cattle there,
\q1 and all the birds and wild animals will have fled {to other places}.
\q1 \v 11 {Yahweh says,} “I will cause Jerusalem to become a heap of ruins,
\q1 and jackals will live there.
\q1 I will destroy the cities of Judah, with the result that they will be completely deserted;
\q1 no one will live there.”
\q1 \v 12 I said, “Only people who are very wise can understand these things.
\q1 Only those who have been taught by Yahweh can explain these things to others.
\q1 The wise people are the only ones who can explain why the land will be completely ruined
\q1 with the result that everyone will be afraid to travel through it.”
\p
\v 13 Yahweh replied, “{These things will happen} because my people have rejected my laws which I gave to them; they have not paid attention to my instructions or lived according to them.
\v 14 Instead, they have stubbornly done the things that they wanted to do. They have worshiped the idols that represent the god Baal, which is what their ancestors did.
\v 15 So now listen to what I, Yahweh, the commander of the angel armies, the God of the Israelites, say: What I will do will be {like} giving these people bitter things to eat and poison water to drink:
\v 16 I will scatter them to nations which neither they nor their ancestors have known. I will enable their enemies to strike them with swords until I have destroyed them.”
\q1 \v 17 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the angel armies, says:
\q1 “Think about what is happening,
\q1 then summon those women who mourn {when someone has died} so that they may come.
\q1 \v 18 Tell them to come quickly and start to wail for us,
\q1 with the result that tears will stream down from your eyes.
\q1 \v 19 Listen to the people of Jerusalem lamenting, saying,
\q1 ‘We have experienced a terrible disaster!
\q1 Now we are very ashamed,
\q1 because {our enemies have forced us to} leave our land and because they have destroyed our houses.’”
\q1 \v 20 {I, Jeremiah, said:} “You women, listen to what Yahweh says.
\q1 Pay attention to his words.
\q1 Teach your daughters to wail.
\q1 Teach each other a funeral song.
\q1 \v 21 {Do that} because people will be dying in your houses and in palaces,
\q1 with the result that there will be no more children playing in the streets,
\q1 and no more young men gathering in the city squares.”
\q1 \v 22 {Yahweh told me to} say {this}: “This is what Yahweh says, “there will be corpses scattered across the fields like dung;
\q1 their dead bodies will lie there like fresh cut grain that has been cut by harvesters but not yet gathered,
\q1 so there will be no one still alive to bury them.’”
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh says this:
\q1 “Wise men should not boast about their being wise,
\q1 strong men should not boast about their being strong;
\q1 and rich men should not boast about their being rich.
\q1 \v 24 Instead, those who want to boast should boast about their knowing me
\q1 and about understanding that I am Yahweh,
\q1 who faithfully loves people,
\q1 and that I do what is just and righteous in the earth,
\q1 because I delight in these things.
\p
\v 25-26 There will be a time when I will punish all those people of Egypt, Judah, Edom, Moab, and the descendants of Ammon, all those people who cut their hair short {to please their gods} who live in the desert, all those who have {changed their bodies} by circumcising them but who have not changed their inner beings. I will also punish the people of Israel because they have not changed their inner beings.”
\c 10
\p
\v 1 You people of Israel, listen to what Yahweh says to you:
\q1 \v 2 This is what he says {to you}, “Do not act like the people of {other} nations act, and do not be terrified by strange things that you see in the sky,
\q2 even though they cause the {people of other} nations to be terrified.
\q1 \v 3 The customs of the people {of other nations} are worthless. {For example,} they cut down a tree in the forest
\q2 and then a skilled worker {cuts a section of it and} uses his chisel to carve an idol from that section.
\q1 \v 4 Then people decorate the idol with silver and gold.
\q2 Then they fasten it securely with nails in order that it will not fall over.
\q1 \v 5 Idols {stand there} like a scarecrow in a field of cucumbers.
\q2 Idols cannot speak,
\q1 and people must carry them,
\q2 because they cannot walk.
\q1 Do not be afraid of idols,
\q2 because they cannot harm {anyone},
\q2 and they cannot do {anything} good {to help anyone}.”
\q1 \v 6 Yahweh, there is no one like you.
\q2 You are great, and you are very powerful.
\q1 \v 7 You are the king of all the nations!
\q2 Everyone should revere you,
\q2 because that is what you deserve,
\q1 and because among all the wise people on the earth
\q2 and in all the kingdoms where they live,
\q2 there is no one like you.
\q1 \v 8 Those people are altogether very foolish.
\q2 Seeking instruction from a wooden idol is totally useless.
\q1 \v 9 People hammer silver from Tarshish and gold from Uphaz into sheets.
\q2 People then give those sheets of silver and gold to craftsmen and goldsmiths to cover the idols.
\q1 Then they put on those idols expensive violet and purple robes
\q2 that skilled workers have made.
\q1 \v 10 But Yahweh is the {only} true God;
\q2 he is the {only} God who is alive,
\q2 the king {who rules} forever.
\q1 When he is angry, all the earth shakes;
\q2 and the people of the nations cannot endure what he does when he is angry {with them}.
\p
\v 11 You Israelite people must tell this to those people: “Those idols did not make the sky and the earth, and they will disappear from the earth.”
\q1 \v 12 But Yahweh made the earth by his power;
\q2 he established it by his wisdom
\q2 and stretched out the sky by his understanding.
\q1 \v 13 When Yahweh loudly speaks, the sky makes a loud noise as it rains
\q2 and he causes clouds to form over every part of the earth.
\q1 He sends lightning with the rain
\q2 and releases the wind from where he keeps it.
\q1 \v 14 Every person {who worships idols} is stupid and does not know what is true.
\q1 Everyone who make idols is disappointed by them
\q2 because their idols do nothing for them.
\q1 The images that they make are not real gods;
\q2 they are lifeless.
\q1 \v 15 Idols are worthless; they are a creation that {people} should ridicule;
\q2 {Yahweh will ensure} that they perish, when he punishes them.
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh, the inheritance of Jacob and his descendants, is not like these idols;
\q2 he is the one who created everything.
\q1 We, the people of Israel, belong to him;
\q2 he is the commander of the angel armies.
\q1 \v 17 {You people of Jerusalem}
\q1 should gather up your possessions in a bundle {and prepare to leave},
\q2 because the army of your enemies surrounds your city.
\q1 \v 18 Yahweh says this {to the people of Jerusalem:} “I will soon expel you from this land
\q2 and cause you to experience great troubles
\q2 so that you will have severe pain.”
\q1 \v 19 {I, Jeremiah thought to myself,}
\q2 “Woe to us because it is as though we have been badly wounded!”
\q1 But I said,
\q2 “Surely this {is} like a sickness, and we must endure it.
\v 20 Our enemies will soon destroy our tents
\q2 and rip apart all the ropes {that hold them up}.
\q1 Our children will soon go away from us and will not return.
\q2 There will be no people left to put our tents up again and set up their curtains.
\q1 \v 21 Our leaders do not have any sense
\q2 and do not ask Yahweh to guide them,
\q1 so they have not prospered.
\q2 All those they lead will be scattered.
\q1 \v 22 A report is coming. Listen! Our enemies’ army in the north is making a very great noise
\q2 as their soldiers march toward us.
\q1 They will destroy the cities in Judah,
\q2 and those cities will become a place where jackals live.
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh, I know that no person controls what will happen to him;
\q2 no one is able to direct the events that he will experience.
\q1 \v 24 {So} correct us, Yahweh, but do it gently.
\q2 Do not correct us when you are angry,
\q2 because we would die if you did that.
\q1 \v 25 Punish {all} the nations whose people do not acknowledge that you are God;
\q2 punish all the families who do not worship you,
\q1 because they are completely destroying us people of Israel,
\q2 and they are causing our land to soon be an uninhabited wasteland.
\c 11
\p
\v 1 This is {another} message that Yahweh told me:
\v 2 “Listen to the words of this agreement and tell them to the people who live in Jerusalem and to the people who live in the other cities in Judah.
\v 3 Then tell them that I, Yahweh, the God whom the Israelite people worship, said that I will curse everyone who does not obey what Moses wrote in that agreement {that I made with them}.
\v 4 It is the same agreement that I made with your ancestors when I brought them out of Egypt. {What happened to them in Egypt was terrible; it was as though they were} living in a hot furnace. {When I brought them out of Egypt,} I told them to obey me, and to do everything that I had commanded them to do. {I also told them that} if they obeyed me, they would be my people and I would be their God.
\v 5 {Now tell these people that if they obey me,} I will do what I promised to do for their ancestors. I will enable them to continue living in this very fertile land in which they now live.”
\p I replied, “Yahweh, I hope that what you have said may happen.”
\p
\v 6 Then Yahweh said to me, “Go into the streets of Jerusalem and to the other cities in Judah. Proclaim my message to the people. Tell them to listen to the agreement {that I made with their ancestors} and to obey it.
\v 7 {Also tell them that} when I brought their ancestors out of Egypt, I solemnly pleaded with them many times to obey me, and I am still pleading with you now.
\v 8 But they did not obey me or pay any attention to me. Instead everyone continued to be stubborn and to do the evil things that they wanted to do. I commanded them to do what was written in the agreement, but they refused. So I punished them in all the ways that I promised in the agreement that I would.”
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh said to me, “The people of Jerusalem and the other cities in Judah are rebelling against me.
\v 10 Their forefathers refused to do what I told them to do, and now these people have returned to committing the sins that their forefathers committed. They are worshiping other gods. The people of Israel and the people of Judah disobeyed the agreement that I made with their ancestors.
\v 11 So now I, Yahweh, am warning them that I will cause them to experience disasters, and they will not escape. And when they call out for me {to help them,} I will not pay attention to them.
\v 12 When that happens, the people in Jerusalem and {other} cities in Judah will {offer sacrifices and} burn incense to their gods and ask for their help, but those gods will not be able to save them when those disasters happen to them.
\v 13 There are as many gods in Judah as there are cities in Judah. The people of Jerusalem have erected as many altars to {burn incense} to that shameful god Baal as there are streets in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 14 Jeremiah, do not pray for these people, and do not plead with me to rescue them, because I will not pay attention when they call out to me {to help them} because they are in distress.
\p
\q1 \v 15 The people {of Judah} whom I love certainly no longer have a right to come to my temple,
\q2 because they have done many evil things.
\q1 Making sacrifices of meat to me certainly will not protect them from disasters.
\q2 When they do evil things, then they rejoice.
\q1 \v 16 I previously said that they were {like} a beautiful olive tree that was full of green leaves
\q2 and had a lot of good olives on it.
\q1 But now I will send their enemies to attack them furiously,
\q2 and {it will be as though} I caused lightning to strike that olive tree and break off its branches and cause it to catch on fire.
\v 17 {It is as though} the people of Israel and Judah were a beautiful olive tree that I, Yahweh, commander of the angel armies, planted,
\q2 but now, because they have done an evil thing by burning incense {to the false god} Baal, they have caused me to become angry.
\q1 {So now} I have decided to destroy them.”
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh revealed to me my enemies’ evil plot, so I knew that they were planning to kill me.
\v 19 {Before he did that,} I was like a gentle lamb that someone was leading away in order to slaughter it and I did not know what they were planning to do. I did not know that they were saying, “Let us get rid of this tree and its fruit! Let us kill him so that no one will remember him.”
\q1 \v 20 {Then I prayed,} “Yahweh, commander of the angel armies, you judge people justly,
\q2 and you examine everything that we are thinking.
\q1 Allow me to watch you getting revenge on the people {who want to kill me},
\q2 because I have committed my cause to you.”
\p
\v 21 Therefore this is what Yahweh says concerning the men of {my own town,} Anathoth, who want to kill me, and they told me to stop prophesying what Yahweh told me to say or they would kill me.
\v 22 So Yahweh, commander of the angel armies, said about them, “I will certainly punish them. Their young men will die in wars, and their children will die because they will have no food.
\v 23 I have set a time when I will bring disasters to the people of Anathoth, and when that happens, none of them will remain alive.”
\c 12
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh, whenever I tell you that I am unhappy {about what is happening to me},
\q2 you always act justly.
\q1 So now allow me to ask about one more thing {that I do not understand}:
\q2 Why are wicked people prosperous?
\q2 Why do things go very well for all the dishonest people?
\q1 \v 2 You allow them to prosper
\q2 {like} trees that have taken root and grown tall and produced {much} fruit.
\q1 They say good things about you,
\q2 but in their inner beings, they really do not care about you.
\q1 \v 3 But you, Yahweh, you know me very well.
\q2 You see what I do and you know what I am thinking about regarding you.
\q1 So drag away those wicked people,
\q2 like {people drag away} sheep that they are going to butcher.
\q1 Set them aside like sheep that are about to be slaughtered!
\q1 \v 4 This land has become very dry and the grass in every field is withered
\q1 because of the evil behavior of the people who live in it.
\q2 The wild animals and the birds are dying
\q1 because the people have said,
\q2 “Yahweh will not see what will happen to us!”
\p
\v 5 {Then to show me that I needed to be prepared to endure even greater difficulties, Yahweh said to me,
\q1 “It is as though} you have become exhausted from racing against men;
\q2 so you will not be able to race against horses!
\q1 If you feel safe only in a peaceful land,
\q2 then you will not be able to handle the thickets by the Jordan River.
\q1 \v 6 For even your brothers and other members of your own family oppose you.
\q2 They plan to do evil things to you and say bad things about you.
\q2 So even if they say nice things about you,
\q1 do not trust them!
\q1 \v 7 I have abandoned my Israelite people,
\q2 the people whom I chose to belong to me.
\q1 I have allowed their enemies to conquer the people whom I love.
\q1 \v 8 My people have become to me like a lion in the forest.
\q2 {The way} they speak against me {is as though they were roaring at me like a lion},
\q2 so now I hate them.
\q1 \v 9 My chosen people have become {like} spotted birds of prey that attack me.
\q2 {However, the soldiers from} other nations will surround them {like} birds of prey {waiting to eat their flesh}.
\q1 Go and tell all {the soldiers} from the nations who are {like} wild animals to come
\q2 and destroy them.
\q1 \v 10 Many rulers {from other countries have come with their armies and} have devastated my people
\q2 {whom I care for like a farmer takes care of his} vineyard.
\q1 Those rulers have trampled down the land that is mine;
\q1 they have caused my beautiful land to become a barren wilderness where no one lives.
\q1 \v 11 They have caused it to become completely empty;
\q2 {it is as though} I hear the land crying mournfully.
\q1 The whole land is desolate,
\q2 and no one is concerned about it.
\q1 \v 12 The soldiers of our enemies have marched across all the barren hilltops in the wilderness.
\q1 because {I,} Yahweh, am using those armies to punish the people throughout your land,
\q2 and no one will escape.
\q1 \v 13 My people planted wheat,
\q2 but they have harvested thorns.
\q1 They have become very tired {because of much hard work},
\q2 but they have gained nothing {from all that work}.
\q1 You should feel ashamed by your {meager} harvests
\q2 that I have caused because you have made {me}, Yahweh, very angry.”
\p
\v 14 This is also what Yahweh said to me about all the wicked nations that surround us and attack our land Yahweh gave us, his people, to possess: “I will punish the evil nearby nations by forcing them to leave their land and I will take the people of Judah out of their lands.
\v 15 But later, after I have removed them from their land, I will act mercifully toward those nations and I will bring them back, each one to his own inheritance and each one will come back to their own land.
\v 16 And if the people of the other nations {whose armies have invaded Israel} will learn the religious practices of my people, to solemnly promise that they will do something by me, saying, ‘As Yahweh lives’ like how they taught my people to do by their god Baal, then I will cause them to become prosperous and to be my people.
\v 17 But I will expel any nation whose people refuse to obey me. Yes, I will expel and destroy that nation and its people.” {That will surely happen because} Yahweh, has said it.
\c 13
\p
\v 1 {One day} Yahweh said to me, “Go and buy a linen waistcloth. Put it on, but do not wash it.”
\v 2 So I bought a waistcloth as Yahweh had instructed me to do and I put it on.
\p
\v 3 Then {later} Yahweh gave me another message. Yahweh said,
\v 4 “Go to the Perath Stream {near Jerusalem} and hide your waistcloth that you bought, which is around your waist, in a hole in the large rocks there.”
\v 5 So I went to the Perath Stream and hid my waistcloth there as Yahweh had instructed me to do.
\p
\v 6 A long time later, Yahweh said to me, “Go {back} to the Perath Stream and get the waistcloth that I told you to hide there.”
\v 7 So I went to the Perath Stream and dug out the waistcloth from the hole in which I had hidden it, and it was ruined and useless.
\v 8 Then Yahweh gave me this message:
\v 9 “This is what {I,} Yahweh say, ‘In a similar way to how {your waistcloth was ruined} I will cause the things that the people of Jerusalem and the other places in Judah are very proud of to be ruined.
\v 10 Those wicked people refuse to pay attention to what I say. They stubbornly do just what they desire to do; they worship other gods. Therefore, they will become completely useless, like your waistcloth.
\v 11 For just like a waistcloth clings tightly to a man’s waist, I wanted the people of Israel and Judah to cling closely to me. I wanted them to be my people, people who would honor me, praise me, glorify me and cause the people of other nations to do the same. But they would not pay attention to me.
\p
\v 12 So, tell this to them: ‘Yahweh, the God whom you Israelite people worship, says that you should fill every jar with wine.’ And {when you tell that to them,} they will reply, ‘Of course we know that we should fill all of our jars with wine!’
\v 13 Then you must tell them, ‘{No, that is not what Yahweh means.} This is what he meant {when he said that.} He will certainly judge all the people of this land and cause them to be so confused {that it will be as if} they were drunk. That will include all of you—the kings who rule in succession of King David, the priests, the prophets, and all the common people who live in Jerusalem.’
\v 14 {He is saying,} ‘I will cause you to bash each other. Even parents will bash their children. I will not pity you or act mercifully toward you at all; so as not to punish you {for your sins}.’”
\q1 \v 15 {You people of Judah,} pay very careful attention.
\q2 Do not be arrogant, because Yahweh has spoken to you.
\q1 \v 16 {It is as though} he is ready to bring darkness {on you}
\q2 and to cause you to stumble and fall as you walk on the mountains when it is becoming dark.
\q1 {So} honor Yahweh your God before that happens.
\q1 If you do not do that, you will look eagerly for light,
\q2 but all you will see is darkness and gloom.
\q1 \v 17 And if you {still} refuse to obey what he tells you to do,
\q2 I will cry when I am alone because of your prideful attitude.
\q1 My eyes will be filled with tears
\q2 because {your enemies} will capture you, Yahweh’s people,
\q2 {whom he takes care of like} a shepherd takes care of his flock,
\q2 and they will take you {to other countries}.
\q1 \v 18 {Yahweh instructed me,} “Tell the king and his mother this message,
\q1 ‘Come down from sitting on your thrones
\q2 and {humbly} sit on the ground in the dust,
\q2 because {your enemies} will soon snatch from your heads your glorious crowns.
\q1 \v 19 The cities in the southern part of Judah will be surrounded by your enemies,
\q2 and the people in those cities will close the city gates. No one will be able to get through their lines {to rescue the people in those cities}.
\q1 {Your enemies} will capture {you people of} Judah and take you away;
\q2 they will take all of you away {to foreign countries}.’”
\q1 \v 20 {You leaders of Jerusalem,} open your eyes and look:
\q1 the enemy armies are ready to march down from the north.
\q1 {When that happens,} bad things will happen to {the people of Judah who are like} a beautiful flock of sheep,
\q2 people that he gave to you to take care of!
\q1 \v 21 You will not like it when Yahweh appoints people {from other countries} to rule over you,
\q2 people who you thought were your friends and whom you encouraged to be your allies.
\q1 You will suffer very much pain,
\q2 like a woman who is about to give birth to a baby.
\q1 \v 22 And if you think, “Why has this happened to me?”
\q2 {I will reply that it is} because of your many sins.
\q1 That is why {soldiers of the invading armies} will lift up the skirts of your women and rape them.
\q1 \v 23 A man from Ethiopia certainly cannot change the color of his {black} skin,
\q2 and a leopard certainly cannot change its spots.
\q1 Similarly, you cannot start doing what is good,
\q2 because you have become accustomed to doing evil things.
\q1 \v 24 {Yahweh says,} “Because of {you have sinned against me} I will scatter you like chaff
\q2 that is blown away by wind from the wilderness.
\q1 \v 25 That is what is certainly going to happen to you,
\q2 the things that I have determined will happen to you,
\q2 because you have forgotten me,
\q2 and you are trusting in false gods.
\q1 \v 26 {It is as though} I myself will pull your skirts up over your faces
\q2 and cause you to be very ashamed because everyone will be able to see your private parts.
\q1 \v 27 I have seen you commit adultery {when you worship Baal}.
\q2 You are like male horses that whinny {when they desire to mate with a female horse}.
\q1 I have seen your disgusting behavior as you act like prostitutes as you worship disgusting idols on the hills that are in the fields.
\q1 Woe to you, people of Jerusalem!
\q2 You must make yourselves acceptable to me again!”
\c 14
\p
\v 1 After there had been no rain {in Judah} for a long time, Yahweh gave me, Jeremiah, this message regarding it:
\q1 \v 2 {He said,} “The people in Judah are very distressed;
\q1 the commerce that took place at the city gates has stopped.
\q2 The people of Judah are mourning because of the condition of their land,
\q1 and in all of Jerusalem people are crying loudly.
\q1 \v 3 The rich people send their servants {to wells} to get water,
\q2 but when they go all the wells are dry.
\q1 The servants return with empty vessels;
\q1 so they cover their heads
\q2 because they are ashamed and disappointed.
\q1 \v 4 The ground is extremely dry and cracked open
\q2 because there has been no rain in your land.
\q1 The farmers are very worried
\q2 {so} they cover their heads.
\q1 \v 5 Even the female deer abandon their newborn babies,
\q2 because there is no grass {for them to eat}.
\q1 \v 6 The wild donkeys stand on the barren hills,
\q2 panting for breath like jackals.
\q1 They strain their eyes looking for grass to eat,
\q2 but there is no edible vegetation.”
\q1 \v 7 {Then I, Jeremiah, said,} “Yahweh, even though we are guilty because of our many wicked actions, please act {on our behalf} so that everyone can see that you are very great.
\q2 We have abandoned following you many times and have sinned against you.
\q1 \v 8 {Yahweh, you have always been} the only one whom we Israelites can confidently expect to deliver us when we need help.
\q1 I wish that you would not act as though you are a stranger in our land,
\q2 and like you are someone who is traveling through and staying here for only one night!
\q1 \v 9 I wish that you would not act like someone who is confused and like a warrior who is not able to save {us}!
\q1 But you, Yahweh, are {here} among us,
\q2 and {others know that} we are your people,
\q2 {so} do not abandon us!
\p
\v 10 This is what Yahweh says to the people of Judah:
\q1 “You love to wander away from me;
\q2 you run from one idol to another.
\q1 Therefore, now I will no longer accept you.
\q2 Now I will take action because of your iniquity and will punish you for your sins.”
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh told me, “Do not pray that I will do good things for this people.
\v 12 When they abstain from food I will not pay any attention to their cry. When they offer {to me} their offerings {of animals} to be completely burned {on the altar} and their offerings of flour, I will not accept them. Instead, I will get rid of them by wars, by famines, and by plagues.”
\p
\v 13 Then I replied, “Oh, Yahweh {my} Lord! Indeed, the prophets are telling the people that they will not experience wars or famines but will instead have lasting peace in this place.
\p
\v 14 Yahweh replied to me, “Those prophets say that they are speaking what I tell them to say, but they are telling lies. I did not send them or instruct them {to say the things that they are prophesying} and I did not speak to them. They say that they have received visions {from me,} and that they are telling things that I have revealed to them, but that is not true. They are saying foolish things that they have only thought of themselves.
\v 15 Therefore Yahweh says this about those prophets who are predicting what will happen, saying that he told them those things when, in fact, he did not. They are saying, ‘We Israelites will not die from wars and famines in our land.’ {But} those prophets {themselves} will die from wars or from famines.
\v 16 And the people to whom they are predicting these things, the corpses of those people will be thrown into the streets of Jerusalem because {they will have died} from famines and wars and there will not be anyone to bury them, they themselves or their wives or their sons or their daughters. I will punish them like they deserve to be punished.
\p
\v 17 So, Jeremiah, tell this to them {about yourself}:
\q1 ‘Day and night my eyes are full of tears.
\q2 I cannot stop crying.
\q1 I cry for my people,
\q2 {who are very precious to me, as if they were} my daughters.
\q2 I cry for them because they are severely wounded.
\q1 \v 18 If I go out into the fields,
\q2 I see {corpses of} people who have been slaughtered by our enemies.
\q1 If I walk along the streets of the city,
\q2 I see {corpses of} people who died from hunger.
\q1 The prophets and the priests travel through the land{, preaching to people,}
\q2 but they do not know {what they are doing}.’”
\p
\v 19 {Then I prayed this:
\q1 “Yahweh,} have you completely rejected {the people of} Judah?
\q2 Do you really despise {the people of} Jerusalem?
\q1 Why have you wounded us very badly,
\q2 with the result that we will never be healed?
\q1 We hoped that we would have good things come to us,
\q2 but we have not had good things.
\q1 We hoped that there would be a time when we would be healed,
\q2 but all that we received were things that terrified us.
\q1 \v 20 Yahweh, we admit that we are wicked people,
\q2 and that our ancestors also did many wicked things.
\q1 We have {all} sinned against you.
\q1 \v 21 But {Yahweh,} in order that people may honor you,
\q2 do not abandon us.
\q1 Do not dishonor {the city where} your glorious throne is.
\q1 {Please} pay attention to us,
\q2 and do not break your agreement with us.
\q1 \v 22 The worthless idols of the nations certainly cannot bring rain to us
\q2 and the sky certainly cannot cause rain to fall.
\q1 Yahweh our God, you are the only one who has done things like that.
\q2 So we will confidently expect you to help us.”
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh said {this} to me: “Even if Moses and Samuel {could come back from their graves and} stand in front of me {and plead with me for these Israelite people,} I would not act mercifully toward these people. {I would still tell you}, ‘Send the people of Judah away from me. Let them go away!’
\v 2 And if they ask you, ‘Where should we go?’, then you must tell them, ‘This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “The ones that I say must die, will die:
\q1 The ones that I say must die in wars, will die in wars.
\q1 The ones that I say must die from hunger, will die from hunger.
\q1 The ones that I say must be captured {and taken to other countries}, will be captured {and taken to other countries.}”’
\p
\v 3 I will send four things {that will get rid of them: I will send enemy soldiers using} swords to kill them. I will send wild dogs to drag away {their corpses}. I will send vultures to eat {their corpses}. And I will send {other} wild animals to eat {what remains of their corpses}.
\v 4 Because of {the wicked things that} King Manasseh, son of Hezekiah, king of Judah, did in Jerusalem, I will cause people in all the kingdoms of the earth to be horrified about {what will happen in Judah to} my people.
\q1 \v 5 You people of Jerusalem, no one will feel sorry for you.
\q2 No one will weep for you.
\q2 No one will care enough to take the time to ask about your well-being.
\q1 \v 6 You people have abandoned me;
\q2 {instead of seeking me} you have refused to obey me,
\q1 so, I will bring judgement upon you and destroy you {as if I were bringing down my fist on you to smash you}.
\q2 I will not act mercifully toward you any longer.
\q1 \v 7 At the gates of your cities, I will scatter you {like a farmer scatters the chaff from his grain by} throwing it up to allow the wind to blow the chaff away.
\q2 You, my people, have refused to turn away from your evil behavior.
\q1 So, I will get rid of you,
\q2 and I will allow your enemies to kill your children.
\q1 \v 8 I will cause there to be so many widows in Judah
\q2 that when I look at them {it will seem as if they} were more numerous than the grains of sand on the seashore.
\q1 At noontime{, when people will not be expecting it to happen,} I will cause an enemy army to attack you,
\q2 an army that will destroy your young men and cause their mothers to weep.
\q1 I will cause you to suddenly experience great suffering and become very terrified.
\q1 \v 9 A woman who has seven children will become faint and gasp for breath;
\q2 it will be {as though} daylight will become darkness for her,
\q1 {because most of} her children will be dead,
\q2 and she will be greatly humiliated.
\q1 And her children who are still alive, I will enable your enemies to kill them.
\q2 {That will surely happen because I,} Yahweh, have said it.’”
\p
\v 10 {I said to} my mother, “I am very sad;
\q1 I wish that you had not given birth to me;
\q2 everyone in this land opposes me and quarrels with me.
\q1 I am not a person who lends {money to people and threatens to sue or harm them if they do not pay me back when they should,}
\q2 and I am not a person who borrows {money from others and then refuses to pay it back,}
\q1 but everyone curses me.”
\p
\v 11 {But} Yahweh replied to me,
\q1 “{Jeremiah,} I will certainly deliver you for good purposes.
\q1 And at the times when your enemies encounter disasters and are distressed about them,
\q2 I will certainly cause them to come to you and plead with you {to ask me for help}.
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh also told me to say to the people of Judah, “Your enemies, who are as strong as iron or bronze, will attack you from the north;
\q2 no one will be able to stop them.
\q1 \v 13 I will give all the valuable possessions of your people as spoils of war {to your enemies},
\q2 without them paying for it.
\q1 I will do this because of all the sins that they have committed throughout your country.
\q1 \v 14 Then I will cause your enemies to force you to become their prisoners,
\q2 and take you to other lands where you have never been before,
\q2 and force you to become their slaves.
\q1 {That will happen} because I am extremely angry {with your people};
\q2 my being angry is {like} a fire that will burn against you.”
\q1 \v 15 Then I said, “Yahweh, you certainly know {what is happening to me}!
\q2 {Please} come and help me.
\q2 Punish those who are persecuting me.
\q1 {Please} do not continue to be patient with my persecutors
\q2 {because if you are} they will kill me.
\q1 Take notice of the fact that it is for your sake that they are reproaching me.
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh, I belong to you, and you are the commander of the angel armies.
\q1 When you spoke to me,
\q2 I eagerly accepted what you said,
\q1 and it caused me to be very joyful!
\q1 \v 17 When the people were carousing together,
\q2 I never joined them nor did I rejoice {with them}.
\q1 I sat alone, because of you,
\q2 because I was very angry {with those people because of their sins}.
\q1 \v 18 So, why do you allow me to continue to suffer?
\q2 Why do you allow me to continually endure being treated badly by others, which is like a living with a wound that never heals.
\q1 It seems that you are as undependable as a brook that has water in it only during certain seasons;
\q2 and like a spring that has dries up during the hot season.”
\p
\v 19 Then Yahweh replied to me saying this:
\q1 “If you will trust in me,
\q1 then I will restore you,
\q2 in order that you may serve me.
\q1 If you proclaim good messages and not worthless ones,
\q2 {then} you will be my spokesperson.
\q1 They should certainly follow you,
\q2 but you must certainly not follow them.
\q1 \v 20 They will fight against you,
\q2 but I will protect you, like people are protected from their enemies by a bronze wall.
\q1 They will not defeat you,
\q2 because I will be with you,
\q2 and I will surely rescue you.”
\q1 {That will happen because} Yahweh has said it.
\q1 \v 21 {Truly,} I will keep you safe from those wicked people,
\q2 and I will rescue you when cruel people try to seize you.”
\c 16
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said to me,
\v 2 “Do not get married and have children in this place,
\v 3 because this is what {I,} Yahweh, say about the children who are born in this place, and about their mothers and fathers:
\v 4 “{Many of} them will die from terrible diseases and no one will mourn for them or bury their corpses, which will remain lying on the ground, scattered like manure. {Others} will die in wars or from hunger, and {then} their corpses will become food for vultures and wild animals.”
\p
\v 5 This is {also} what Yahweh said to me: “Do not go to funerals to mourn or to show {those whose relatives have died} that you feel sorry for them, because I have stopped protecting them, and I have caused that things will not go well for them. I have stopped faithfully loving them and acting mercifully toward them.
\v 6 {Very many people} will die in this land, {including} those who are important and those who are not important. And no one will mourn for them, or bury their corpses. No one will cut himself or shave his head {to show that he is very sad}.
\v 7 No one will bring food to comfort those who are mourning, not even if it is their father or their mother {who has died}. No one will give them a cup of wine to console them.
\p
\v 8 And do not go into the houses where people are feasting to spend time there. Do not eat or drink anything with them.
\v 9 {I want you to do this} because this is what {I,} Yahweh, the commander of the angel armies, the God you Israelites worship, say: ‘While you are still alive and seeing it happen, I will cause there to be no more singing and laughing in this land. There will be no more joyful voices of bridegrooms and brides.’
\p
\v 10 When you tell these things to the people, they will ask, ‘Why has Yahweh declared that these terrible things will happen to us? What have we done for which we deserve to be punished like this? What sin have we committed against Yahweh our God?’
\p
\v 11 Then this is what you must tell them {that I am replying to them}: ‘It is because your ancestors have stopped worshiping me and they have pursued other gods and served them and bowed down to them {to worship them}. Yes, they have stopped worshiping me and have not obeyed my commands {which I gave them through Moses}.
\v 12 But you {who are living now} have certainly done more wicked things than your ancestors did! Each of you stubbornly does the evil things that he desires and refuses to pay attention to what I say.
\v 13 So, I will throw you out of this land, and I will send you to a land that you and your ancestors have not been familiar with. There you will serve other gods day and night. And I will not act mercifully toward you.’
\p
\v 14 Therefore, know this for certain, in the future there will be a time when people {who are solemnly promising to do something} will no longer say, ‘{I will do this, just as surely} as Yahweh lives—the one who brought {us} Israelite people here from Egypt.’
\v 15 Instead, they will say ‘{I will do this, just as surely} as Yahweh lives—the one who brought {us} Israelite people back to our own land from the lands to the north and from all the other lands to which he had exiled us.’ {They will be able to say that} because {some day} I will bring your descendants back to this land that I gave to your ancestors.
\p
\v 16 Know for certain that I am summoning {your enemies who will seize the people {of Judah like} fishermen catch fish. And afterward I will summon those who will search for them on every mountain and every hill, and in the caves, {like} hunters search for {animals to kill}.
\v 17 I am watching them carefully. They will not be able to hide from me. I see every sin that they commit.
\v 18 Because of all the wicked things that they have done, I will punish them twice as much {as I would normally punish them}. {I will do that} because they have caused my own land to become unacceptable to me because of their {worshiping} lifeless statues of detestable gods, and also because they have filled my land with the other evil things that they have done.”
\q1 \v 19 {Then I prayed, saying,} “Yahweh, you are the one who strengthens me and protects me;
\q2 you are the one to whom I go when I have troubles.
\q1 Some day people from nations all over the world will come to you and say,
\q2 ‘Our ancestors left us only something that was false;
\q1 they worshiped idols that are completely worthless.
\q1 \v 20 No one can make their own gods;
\q2 the gods that they make are {only idols}; they are not real gods.’
\q1 \v 21 {Then Yahweh said,} “Now I will certainly show the people {of Judah} that I am truly very powerful.
\q2 Then, finally, they will know that I, Yahweh, am the true God.”
\c 17
\q1 \v 1 {Yahweh said, “It is as though a list of} the sins committed by the people of Judah is engraved with an iron chisel, or engraved using the fine point of a very hard stone,
\q2 on their inner beings and on the altars {where they worship their idols}.
\q1 \v 2 {Even} their children eagerly go to the altars {to worship their gods},
\q1 and to the poles {that represent the goddess} Asherah {at which their parents worshiped},
\q2 shrines that are underneath the big trees
\q2 and on the high hills.
\q1 \v 3 So, I will allow your enemies to capture {Zion,} my {holy} mountain,
\q2 and all your wealth and all your valuable things as plunder
\q1 because of your sinful acts of idolatrous worship that you have done at your high places within all your territories.
\q1 \v 4 The land that I gave to you will no longer belong to you.
\q1 I will allow your enemies to take you to a land that you are not familiar with,
\q2 and you will become their slaves.
\q1 {I will do that} because I am extremely angry with you;
\q2 my being angry is {like} a fire that will burn forever.”
\p
\v 5 This is {also} what Yahweh says:
\q1 “I condemn those who trust in human beings to help them,
\q2 those who rely on themselves and other people
\q2 instead of trusting in me.
\q1 \v 6 They are like bushes in the desert,
\q2 they are people who will not experience any good things.
\q1 Those people will live in the barren desert
\q2 in a salty area, where people do not live {because very few things can grow there}.
\q1 \v 7 {But I,} Yahweh bless those who trust in me,
\q2 and who confidently expect me {to take care of them}.
\q1 \v 8 Those people are like fruit trees that have been planted along a riverbank,
\q2 trees that have roots that go down into {the wet ground beside} the water.
\q1 They are trees whose leaves remain green when it becomes hot,
\q2 trees that continue to bear fruit when there are many months in which there is no rain.
\q1 \v 9 Human hearts are extremely corrupt, more than anything else,
\q2 and no human can change that.
\q2 It is also completely impossible for anyone to understand the human heart.
\q1 \v 10 I, Yahweh, search what is in everyone’s inner being,
\q2 and I examine what they are thinking.
\q1 {I do this} in order to give to each person reward or punishment,
\q2 according to what they have done.”
\q1 \v 11 {I, Jeremiah, agree, because I know that} people who become rich by doing things that are unjust
\q2 are {like} birds that hatch eggs that they did not lay.
\q1 So, when those people have lived only half of the years {that they expect to live,} their wealth will disappear.
\q2 Then {other people will realize that} those rich people have been foolish.”
\q1 \v 12 {Yahweh,} your temple is {like} a glorious throne
\q2 that has been on a high hill since it was built.
\q1 \v 13 Yahweh, you are the one whom we Israelite people hope in,
\q2 and you will openly disgrace all those who forsake you.
\q2 Those people who reject the messages I give them from you, will {quickly disappear, like} writing in the dust {which quickly disappears when the wind blows it away}.
\q2 {They will soon disappear} because they have abandoned you, Yahweh, who are {like} a fountain where people obtain fresh water.
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh{, please} heal me, because if you heal me, I will experience true healing.
\q2 {Please} rescue me and I will live safely, because you are the {only} one whom I worship.
\q1 \v 15 People often ridicule me and say,
\q1 “You tell us messages that you say came from Yahweh,
\q2 but your predictions have not happened!”
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh, you {appointed me to take care of your people like} a shepherd {takes care of his sheep}; I have not abandoned that work.
\q2 {You know} that I have not wanted this disastrous time {to come to people who ridicule me}.
\q1 You know everything that I have said {to your people}.
\q1 \v 17 Do not cause me to be terrified!
\q2 When disasters occur, you are the one to whom I will go for safety.
\q1 \v 18 {So now,} cause those who persecute me to be ashamed and dismayed,
\q2 but do not allow me to be ashamed and dismayed.
\q1 Cause disastrous things to happen to them!
\q2 Do to them many things that will completely destroy them!
\p
\v 19 This is what Yahweh said to me: “Go to the city gates in Jerusalem. {First} go to the gate where the kings of Judah go in and out of the city, and {then} go to each of the {other} gates in Jerusalem.
\v 20 Say to the people {at each gate,} ‘You kings of Judah and everyone {else} who is living in Jerusalem and all you {other} people of Judah who enter these gates, listen to this message from Yahweh!
\v 21 He says, “Listen to this warning carefully! {Stop doing work} on Sabbath days! Stop carrying loads through these gates {on those days}!
\v 22 Do not carry loads out of your houses or do any {other} work on Sabbath days! Instead, cause Sabbath days to be set apart for me. I commanded your ancestors to do that,
\v 23 but they did not listen to me or obey me. When I did things to correct them, they stubbornly refused to pay attention to what I said or to accept it.
\v 24 But I say that if you obey me, and if you do not carry loads through these gates on Sabbath days or do any other work on Sabbath days, and if you dedicate the Sabbath days to me,
\v 25 kings of Judah and {their} officials will go in and out of these gates. There will {always} be someone who is a descendant of King David ruling {here} in Jerusalem. Kings and their officials will go in and out of these gates with the men of Judah and the people living in Jerusalem. They will be riding in chariots and on horses, and there will be people living in this city forever.
\v 26 And people will come {to Jerusalem} bringing offerings to be completely burned {on the altar} and {other} offerings. They will bring to the Temple grain offerings and incense and offerings to thank {me}. People will bring these offerings from the cities in Judah and the villages near Jerusalem and from the land where the tribe of Benjamin lives and from the western foothills and from the southern hill country and from the arid south country.
\v 27 But if you do not pay attention to what I say, and if you refuse to dedicate the Sabbath days to me, and if you {continue to} carry loads through these gates into the city on Sabbath days, I will burn these gates completely. The fire will spread to the palaces, and no one will be able to put out that fire.”’”
\c 18
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave another message to Jeremiah. He said,
\v 2 “Go to the shop of the man who makes clay pots. I will give you a message there.”
\v 3 So I went to that shop, and I saw the man who makes pots. He was working at his potter’s wheel.
\v 4 When the pot he was shaping from the clay did not turn out the way he wanted, he took the same clay and formed it into a different pot, shaping it as seemed best to him.
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh gave me this message:
\v 6 “People of Israel, I control what happens to you just as this potter {controls what he does with his clay}.” That is what Yahweh declared. “You are completely under my control, people of Israel, just as the clay is under the potter’s control.
\v 7 There may be a time when I announce that I will get rid of a certain nation or kingdom, {like someone} pulls up {a plant with its roots,} and smashes it, and destroys it.
\v 8 However, if the people of that nation stop doing the evil things that I spoke against, then I will not bring the disaster on them that I had planned.
\v 9 There may also be a time when I announce that I will establish a certain nation or kingdom and cause it to prosper.
\v 10 However, if the people of that nation start doing things that I consider to be evil and refuse to obey what I say, then I will not do the good things for them that I said I would do.
\p
\v 11 So now, {Jeremiah, please go and} tell the people of Judah and those who live in Jerusalem, ‘This is what Yahweh says: “Know this! I am planning to bring a disaster on you. I am devising something against you. So please, each of you, stop behaving in evil ways and start doing what is right.”’”
\p
\v 12 {So I told them that message,} but the people replied, “It is useless to hope. We will {continue to} follow our own plans, and each of us will stubbornly do whatever our evil desires lead us to do.”
\q1 \v 13 Therefore, this is what Yahweh says: “Please go and ask the people of other nations whether they have ever heard of anything like this. My Israelite people, {who I wanted to be pure like} virgins have done something very horrible.
\q1 \v 14 The snow on the rocky heights of Lebanon never melts away completely, and the cold streams flowing down from those distant mountains never dry up.
\q1 \v 15 But my people {are not as reliable as those things.} They have abandoned me. They burn incense to {honor} gods that are worthless. {It is as though} my people have left the ancient{, reliable} roads and now walk on rough side paths.
\q1 \v 16 Because of that, their land will become an empty wasteland and, forever, people who see it, will hiss {to ridicule it}. Everyone who travels by it will be appalled and will shake their heads {to show that they are shocked}.
\q1 \v 17 When their enemies {attack them}, I will scatter my people as an east wind blows dust away. I will reject them and refuse to help them when disaster comes on them.”
\p
\v 18 Then some of the people said, “Come, let us make plans to get rid of Jeremiah. After all, priests will always teach us God’s laws, wise people will always give us good advice, and prophets will always tell us God’s messages. {We do not need Jeremiah!} So let’s slander him and not pay attention to anything he says.”
\p
\v 19 {Then I prayed,} “Yahweh, please pay attention to me! Listen to what my opponents are saying about me.
\q1 \v 20 They should not be doing harmful things to me when I have done good things for them. But they are trying to kill me. Remember that I once stood in your presence and pleaded with you to do good things for them, and I tried to persuade you not to punish them even though you were very angry with them.
\q1 \v 21 So, let their children starve! Let their enemies kill them in battle! Let their wives lose their children and their husbands! Let their {older} men die from a plague, and let the young men die in battle!
\q1 \v 22 Cause people to scream in terror in their homes when you suddenly send a raiding enemy army against them! {Let all of this happen to them} because {they want to kill me. It is as though} they have dug a pit for me to fall into and have set hidden snares on my path.
\q1 \v 23 But Yahweh, you yourself know everything they have planned to do to try to kill me. Do not forgive the wrong things they have done but instead let them fall down in defeat before you. Deal with them when you are angry!”
\c 19
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me {another} message. He said, “Go and purchase a clay jar from someone who makes pottery. Then take some of the elders of the people and some of the older priests with you.
\v 2 Go out {of the city} to Son of Hinnom Valley, which is near the entrance of the Potsherd Gate. Then give them this message that I will tell you.
\v 3 Say to them, ‘You kings of Judah and you people of Jerusalem, listen to what Yahweh says! Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this: Pay attention! I am about to bring a terrible disaster on this place. {It will be so terrible} that everyone who hears about it will be shocked.
\v 4 {I will do this} because the people have abandoned me. They have turned this place into a place for worshiping foreign gods and they have burned incense here to other gods that neither they, nor their ancestors, nor the kings of Judah have known about. They have filled this place with the blood of innocent people {whom they have killed}.
\v 5 They have built worship sites to honor Baal, and at those places they have burned their sons in the fire as burnt offerings to Baal. I never commanded them to do that, I never told them to do that, and I never even thought about anyone doing that.
\p
\v 6 So pay attention, because a time will come when people will no longer call this place Topheth or Son of Hinnom Valley. Instead, they will call it the Valley of Slaughter.
\v 7 In this place I will ruin the plans of the people that live in Jerusalem and the rest of Judah. I will allow their enemies to kill many of them with swords and allow those who want to kill them to succeed. Then I will let their dead bodies lie on the ground where all the scavenging birds and wild animals will eat them.
\v 8 I will completely destroy this city so that it becomes a desolate place that people despise. Everyone who passes by it will be horrified and will gasp when they see all the destruction.
\v 9 I will allow your enemies who want to kill you to surround the city so tightly and for so long that the food will all run out. Then the people in the city will become so desperately hungry that they will eat the flesh of their own sons and daughters, and each person will eat the flesh of his neighbor.’
\p
\v 10 {Then Yahweh said to me,} “After you say all of that, smash the jar while the people who came with you are watching.
\v 11 Then say to them, ‘Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this: Just as someone smashes a potter’s jar so that no one can ever put it back together, I will smash this people and this city in a similar way. People will bury the dead in Topheth until there is no more room to bury anyone else.
\v 12 That is what I will do to this place and to the people who live here,’ declares Yahweh. ‘I will allow your enemies to desecrate this city like {king Josiah caused} Topheth {to be a place that was unfit for people to worship me in}.
\v 13 Your enemies will desecrate the houses throughout Jerusalem and the palaces of the kings of Judah as {King Josiah did to} Topheth. That includes every house where you burned incense on the rooftops to honor the stars {that you worshiped,} and where you poured out wine {as an offering} to your gods.’”
\p
\v 14 Then {I}, Jeremiah returned from Topheth, where Yahweh had sent me to tell them that message. I went and stood in the courtyard of Yahweh’s temple and said this to all the people who had gathered there:
\v 15 “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this: ‘Pay attention! I am about to bring on this city and on all the towns around it every disaster that I said I would, because the people have stubbornly refused to listen to what I have said to them.’”
\c 20
\p
\v 1 Pashhur, whose father was Immer, was a priest, and he was the chief supervisor in Yahweh’s temple. He heard Jeremiah prophesying these things.
\v 2 So Pashhur beat {me}, Jeremiah, the prophet, and then put me in the stocks at the Upper Benjamin Gate in Yahweh’s temple.
\v 3 The next day, Pashhur released {me,} Jeremiah from the stocks. Then {I,} Jeremiah said to him, “Yahweh is not going to call you Pashhur {anymore}. Instead, {he is going to call you} Terror on Every Side.
\p
\v 4 {Yahweh will call you this} because this is what he says regarding you: ‘Pay attention! I am going to make you a source of terror to yourself and to all your friends. You will watch as your enemies kill your friends with swords. I will let the king of Babylon conquer all the people of Judah. His soldiers will take some of them to Babylon, and they will kill others with swords.
\v 5 I will enable their soldiers to take away everything valuable from this city—all its wealth, all the goods people worked hard to produce, and all its precious things—along with all the treasures that belonged to the kings of Judah. The enemies will plunder everything and carry it to Babylon.
\v 6 And {as for} you, Pashhur, they will take you and everyone in your household, as captives to Babylon. You will die there, and people will bury you there—they will bury you and all your friends there, the ones you gave false prophecies to.’”
\p
\v 7 {Then I, Jeremiah, said,} “Yahweh, you persuaded me {to become your prophet,}
\q2 and I let you persuade me.
\q1 You were stronger than I was,
\q2 and you won.
\q1 Now people ridicule me throughout the day;
\q2 everyone makes fun of me.
\q1 \v 8 Whenever I speak your messages, I shout, ‘{Yahweh is going to cause you to experience} violence and destruction!’
\q1 So because I tell people your messages,
\q2 they insult me throughout the day.
\q1 \v 9 Sometimes I think to myself, “I will stop mentioning Yahweh,
\q2 and I will stop speaking messages from him.”
\q1 However, {when I try to remain silent,} his message becomes like a fire in my inner being,
\q2 that I cannot hold back.
\q1 I grow weary of trying to contain it,
\q2 and I cannot hold back {from speaking Yahweh’s messages}.
\q1 \v 10 I hear many people whispering {about me,
\q2 saying, “He is the man who proclaims that there will be things that cause us to be} terrified all around us! We must tell {the authorities} what he is saying!”
\q1 All the people who pretended to be my friends
\q2 are watching for me to make a mistake.
\q1 {They say,} ‘Maybe we can trick him into saying something wrong,
\q2 and then we can defeat him and get our revenge on him.’
\q1 \v 11 However, Yahweh is with me, and he defends me powerfully.
\q2 So those who persecute me will stumble and will not defeat me.
\q1 They will feel very ashamed because they did not succeed,
\q2 and no one will ever forget how God disgraced them.
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 you examine those who do what is right.
\q1 You know what everyone thinks and what motivates them.
\q1 Allow me to see you punish those people who want to harm me,
\q2 because I have told you about my case and asked you to help me.”
\q1 \v 13 Sing to Yahweh!
\q2 Praise Yahweh!
\q1 Because he rescues needy people
\q2 from the power of wicked people.
\q1 \v 14 I curse the day my mother gave birth to me!
\q2 May no one celebrate that day.
\q1 \v 15 {As for} the man who brought the news to my father,
\q2 and made him very happy by saying,
\q2 “{Your wife} has given birth to a baby boy”,
\q2 I wish someone would curse that man.
\q1 \v 16 May Yahweh destroy that man as he did to the cities he destroyed {in the past}, without acting mercifully {toward them}.
\q1 May that man hear people crying out in distress in the morning
\q2 and hear {enemy soldiers} shout their {battle} cries at noon.
\q1 \v 17 {I say this} because he did not kill me before I came out of my mother’s womb.
\q2 If he had, I would have died inside my mother
\q2 and never come out.
\q1 \v 18 I wish that I had not come out of my mother’s womb
\q2 so that I would not have to experience all these difficult things that cause me to feel so miserable,
\q2 and so that I would not come to the end of my life feeling disgraced.
\c 21
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me {another} message when King Zedekiah {of Judah} sent a man named Pashhur, whose father was Malkijah, and a priest named Zephaniah, whose father was Maaseiah{, to talk to me. They pleaded with me,} saying,
\v 2 “{The army of} King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon is attacking Judah, so please speak to Yahweh for us. {Ask him if he will help us.} Perhaps he will force Nebuchadnezzar’s {army} to leave by performing a miracle for us, like the miracles he performed {previously}.”
\p
\v 3 I replied to them, “{Go back to King} Zedekiah. Tell him this,
\v 4 ‘This is what Yahweh, the God whom we Israelites worship, says: “Pay attention! I will cause your weapons to be useless in fighting against the king of Babylon and his army that is outside the walls of Jerusalem, attacking. I will cause your soldiers to lay their weapons down in the midst of this city.
\v 5 I myself will fight against your army with my very great power, because I am very angry with you.
\v 6 I will send a very terrible plague on the people of this city, and on their domestic animals, and {many of} them will die.’
\v 7 Yahweh says, ‘After those things occur, I will enable {the army of} King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon, and your other enemies, and the others who seek to kill you, to capture {you,} {you} Zedekiah who rule in Judah, and your officials, and {all the other} people who remain in this city and have not died from the plague, from enemy soldiers, and from starvation. I will allow Nebuchadnezzar’s army to slaughter those of you who remain; they will not act mercifully toward you at all or spare people.’
\p
\v 8 And tell this to this people: ‘Yahweh says that you must pay careful attention and decide whether you want to choose the way that will result in your dying or choose the way that will result in your staying alive.
\v 9 Everyone who remains in Jerusalem will die because enemy soldiers will kill them or they will die from starvation or from diseases. But those who surrender to the army of Babylon that is surrounding your city will live. They will escape with nothing except their lives, but will remain alive.
\v 10 {This will happen} because I, Yahweh, have decided to cause the inhabitants of this city to experience disasters and not to experience something good. I have decided to allow {the army of} the king of Babylon to capture this city and his army will destroy it completely by fire.’”
\p
\v 11 Yahweh also told me to say this to the family of the king of Judah: “Listen to this message from Yahweh.
\v 12 This is what he says to you descendants of King David:
\q1 ‘Every day, make fair decisions for the people whom you judge.
\q2 Help those whom people have robbed.
\q2 Rescue them from robbers and abusers.
\q1 If you do not do that,
\q2 I will be angry and punish you with a fire that will be impossible to extinguish,
\q2 because of all the sins that you have committed.
\q1 \v 13 Know that I will oppose you people {of Jerusalem},
\q2 you who live on top of a rocky hill above the valley.
\q1 {I will oppose} you people who boast, saying,
\q2 “No one can attack us and break through our defenses.”
\q1 \v 14 I will punish you for your wicked deeds like you deserve to be punished;
\q1 {It will be as though} I will light a fire in your forests
\q2 that will burn up everything around you.’”
\c 22
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me this message: “Go down to the palace of the king of Judah and tell him this message.
\v 2 Say, ‘Listen to what Yahweh has to say to you, king of Judah, you who rule in succession to King David. You, your officials, and your people who enter through these gates must listen.
\v 3 This is what Yahweh says: Treat people fairly and do what is right. Rescue anyone who has had his things stolen. Rescue that person from the power of the one oppressing them. Do not mistreat foreigners who are living among you, or orphans, or widows. Do not harm anyone, and do not kill innocent people in this place.
\v 4 If you carefully obey these commands, then there will {always} be a king from David’s royal line ruling {in Jerusalem}. Each king with his officials and his people will ride through the gates of this palace in chariots and on horses.
\v 5 However, if you refuse to obey these commands, then I, Yahweh, swear by my own name that this palace will become a pile of rubble.’””
\p
\v 6 This is what Yahweh says about the palace of the king of Judah:
\q1 “{I value} this palace as much as I value the forests in {the region of} Gilead
\q2 and the mountains in Lebanon.
\q1 Nevertheless, I will certainly make it
\q2 a desolate place where no one lives.
\q1 \v 7 I will send enemy troops to destroy it;
\q2 each soldier with his own weapons.
\q1 They will chop down the fine cedar beams
\q2 and throw them into the fire.
\p
\v 8 People from many nations will pass by the ruins of this city and ask one another, ‘Why did Yahweh do this to such a great city?’
\v 9 {Other} people will answer, ‘{He did it} because his people stopped obeying the agreement they had made with Yahweh their God and instead worshiped and served other gods.’”
\p
\v 10 Do not weep for King Josiah who died; do not mourn for him. {Instead,} weep bitterly for {King Jehoahaz, his son, because} he will go away into exile, {weep} because he will never come back and he will never see his homeland again.
\p
\v 11 Yahweh says this about Shallum son of Josiah, king of Judah, who became king after his father Josiah and who was captured and taken away from here {to Babylon}: “He will never come back here again.
\v 12 He will die in the place where his enemies took him, and he will never return to this land.”
\q1 \v 13 {Yahweh says,} “Terrible things will happen to King Jehoiakim!
\q1 He does not act fairly when he forces people to build his palace.
\q2 He forces his neighbors to work for nothing
\q2 and does not pay them for their labor.
\q1 \v 14 He says, “I will {force my workers} to build me a huge palace
\q2 with large, spacious rooms on the upper level.
\q1 I will {make them} cut out windows for me,
\q2 and panel the walls with boards made of cedar wood,
\q2 and paint it bright red.”
\p
\v 15 Having a beautiful palace with cedar paneling does not make a king great! Your father {Josiah} had plenty of things to eat and drink, but he always treated people fairly and did what was right. Because of that, things went well for him.
\p
\v 16 Josiah made sure that judges treated poor and needy people fairly, and things went well because of that. That is what it means to truly know me.” {That is what} Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 17 “But Jehoiakim, you pay attention to nothing and desire nothing except getting things by cheating people and shedding the blood of innocent people, and oppressing people, and you use force to get what you want.
\p
\v 18 Therefore, this is what Yahweh says about Jehoiakim son of King Josiah, king of Judah:
\q1 “{When he dies} people will not mourn for him, saying,
\q2 ‘How sad, my brother! How sad, my sister!’
\q1 People will not mourn for him, saying,
\q2 ‘How sad, my master! How sad for him who was majestic!’
\q1 \v 19 People will bury his corpse the way they dispose of a dead donkey:
\q2 they will drag his body out
\q2 and throw it beyond the gates of Jerusalem.
\q1 \v 20 {You people of Jerusalem,} go up to Lebanon and cry out!
\q2 Shout loudly in Bashan!
\q1 Cry out from Abarim,
\q2 because your enemies have destroyed all the nations that were your political allies.
\q1 \v 21 I warned you when things were going well for you,
\q2 {but} you said, ‘I will not listen.’
\q1 You have behaved this way ever since you were young,
\q2 for you have never obeyed me.
\q1 \v 22 I will punish all your leaders;
\q1 {it will be as though} they have been blown away by the wind.
\q2 Your enemies will take your political allies away as prisoners.
\q1 Then you will certainly feel deeply ashamed and everyone will despise you
\q2 because of all the evil things you have done.
\q1 \v 23 You {people of Jerusalem} who think you are as safe as a bird nesting in the cedar trees that grow in Lebanon,
\q2 people will greatly pity you when you begin to suffer,
\q2 you will suffer severely as a woman does when she is giving birth!”
\p
\v 24 Yahweh declares, “As surely as I am alive, even if you, Coniah son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, were a ring on my finger that shows that I am the king, I would still pull you off.
\v 25 You are afraid of King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon and his huge army, {because} they are wanting to kill you. I will enable them to capture you.
\v 26 I will expel you and your mother who gave birth to you into another land where neither of you were born, and you will both die there.
\v 27 Neither of you will ever return to this land that you both will greatly desire to return to.”
\q1 \v 28 {People say,} “Coniah will be like a broken pot that people despise or a vessel which no one wants.
\q2 Enemy soldiers will force him and his children to go to a foreign land that they are not familiar with.”
\q1 \v 29 People of this land, people of this land, people of this land,
\q2 listen to what Yahweh says!
\q1 \v 30 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Write down {in the record about the kings of Judah} that {it will be as though} this man has no descendants,
\q2 and that he has not been successful during his lifetime,
\q1 because none of his children will become king
\q2 and rule in succession of King David as king over Judah.”
\c 23
\p
\v 1 Yahweh declares, “Terrible things will happen to the leaders of my people, {who were supposed to guide and care for my people like shepherds guide and care for their sheep}. Terrible things will happen to the leaders of my people because they have scattered and harmed my people {instead of caring for them}.
\v 2 So this is what I, Yahweh, the God whom the Israelite people worship, say to those leaders: ‘Instead of taking care of my people as a shepherd does for his sheep, you have scattered them and driven them away, and you have not paid attention to them. Know that I will make sure to punish you for the evil things that you have done.’” This is what Yahweh declares.
\v 3 “Then I myself will gather the rest of my people from all the countries where I have driven them. I will bring them back to their own land, where they will have many children and increase in number.
\v 4 I will appoint other leaders for my people who will take care of them. My people will never again be afraid or dismayed, and none of them will go missing.” This is what Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 5 Yahweh {also} declares,
\q1 “Listen! A time is coming when I will appoint from David’s family line a righteous descendant.
\q2 He will rule as king and will act wisely.
\q1 He will do what is just and right throughout the land.
\q1 \v 6 At the time he begins to reign, Yahweh will rescue the people of Judah,
\q2 and the people of Israel will live securely.
\q1 People will call him by this name:
\q2 ‘Yahweh makes us right with him.’”
\p
\v 7 “So listen! A time is coming,” Yahweh declares, “when people {who are making a solemn promise} will no longer say, ‘{I promise, as surely} as Yahweh lives, the one who brought the Israelite people up from the land of Egypt.’
\v 8 Instead, they will say, ‘{I promise, as surely} as Yahweh lives, the one who brought {us} Israelite people back {to our own land when} he led us from the land to the northeast and from all the {other} countries where he had exiled us.’ Then they will live in their own land {again}.”
\p
\v 9 {This is what I, Jeremiah, say} about the prophets:
\q1 My mind is greatly distressed.
\q2 My entire body is shaking.
\q1 I am so overcome by strong feelings that I am less capable of doing things,
\q2 like a man who has had too much wine.
\q1 {I feel this way} because of Yahweh,
\q2 and because of his holy words.
\q1 \v 10 The land is full of people who commit adultery.
\q2 Because Yahweh has placed a curse on the land, the land dries up
\q1 and the pastures in the wilderness have dried up.
\q2 The people do evil things,
\q2 and those who have powerful positions use them to do things that are not right.
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh declares, “Even the prophets and the priests have turned away from me.
\q2 I have found them doing evil things even in my temple.
\q1 \v 12 So I will make the events of their life be as though they were walking on slippery paths in the dark.
\q2 I will ensure that they experience hardships {so that it will be as though} I am pushing them down and they are falling,
\q1 when I cause disastrous things to happen to them
\q2 at the time when I punish them.” This is what {I} Yahweh declare.
\q1 \v 13 “I saw that the prophets in Samaria were doing something that was wrong:
\q2 They were prophesying, saying that Baal gave them the messages they were proclaiming
\q2 and they were deceiving my people, the Israelites.
\q1 \v 14 And {now} I have seen the prophets in Jerusalem doing something horrible:
\q2 They commit adultery
\q2 and habitually tell lies.
\q1 They have encouraged people who do evil
\q2 so that no one stops doing evil things.
\q1 All of them have become as wicked as the people of Sodom,
\q2 and the people of Jerusalem have become as wicked as the people of Gomorrah.”
\p
\v 15 So this is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says about those prophets:
\q1 “Know that I will cause them to suffer so that it will be as if they will eat bitter things
\q2 and drink poisoned water,
\q1 because on account of the lies being prophesied by the prophets of Jerusalem,
\q2 wicked things are done throughout the whole land.”
\p
\v 16 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says:
\q1 “Do not pay any attention to what those prophets say to you,
\q2 {because} they are only deceiving you.
\q1 They tell you about things that they imagined in their own minds.
\q2 Yahweh did not give them those messages.
\q1 \v 17 They keep saying to people who despise me,
\q2 ‘Yahweh says that things will go well for you.’
\q1 They tell everyone who stubbornly does whatever he wants to do,
\q2 ‘Nothing bad will happen to you.’
\q1 \v 18 But none of these prophets has ever been in a council meeting where Yahweh gave his private counsel
\q2 or seen and heard what Yahweh said.
\q1 None of them has paid attention to what Yahweh said and truly listened.
\q1 \v 19 Look! Yahweh has sent out a furious storm.
\q2 It whirls down
\q1 on the heads of wicked people.
\q1 \v 20 Yahweh will not stop being angry
\q2 until he has completely accomplished everything that he has planned.
\q1 In the future,
\q2 you will clearly understand this.
\q1 \v 21 “I did not send these prophets,
\q2 yet they rushed {to tell people their messages}.
\q1 I did not speak to them,
\q2 yet they {kept on} prophesying.
\q1 \v 22 If they had sought my counsel,
\q2 then they would have proclaimed my words to my people,
\q1 and they would have caused my people to stop doing evil things.”
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh {also} declares, “I am not a God who is only nearby. I am also a God who is far away.
\q1 \v 24 No one can hide in a secret place where I cannot see him.” Yahweh declares, “I am present everywhere, in heaven and on the earth.” This is what Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 25 “I have heard what those prophets have said, the ones who claim to speak for me but prophesy lies. They say, ‘I had a dream {that God gave me}! I had a dream {that God gave me}!’
\v 26 These prophets will not stop prophesying false things that come from their own deceitful minds.
\v 27 They plan to make my people forget about me by telling their dreams to one another, just as their ancestors forgot about me when they started to worship Baal.
\p
\v 28 Allow any prophet who has a dream to tell his dream. However, those who have messages that truly come from me must speak my messages faithfully. As straw and grain are certainly very different, so messages from false prophets are certainly very different from messages from true prophets.” It is {I}, Yahweh, who declare this.
\v 29 “My message has great power. It is as though my messages burn like a fire; they strike people’s inner beings like someone strikes a rock with a hammer and smashes it into pieces.” {I} Yahweh declare this.
\p
\v 30 “So, I oppose all those prophets who steal messages from each other and then claim those messages came from me,” {I} Yahweh declare this.
\v 31 “I oppose those prophets {who steal messages from each other and then} use their tongues to say, ‘Yahweh declares this,’” This is what {I} Yahweh declare.
\v 32 “I am against the ones who prophesy lying dreams. Those people tell their lying dreams to my people and their lies and careless behavior cause my people to stop obeying me. I did not command them {to speak those things} or send them {to my people} and as a result they certainly do not help my people.” This is what {I}, Yahweh declare.
\p
\v 33 {Yahweh said to me, Jeremiah,} “When any of these people or a prophet or a priest asks you, ‘What message has Yahweh given you?’ you must reply to them, ‘What burden? Yahweh declares that he will forsake you!’
\v 34 And if any prophet or priest or Israelite person says, ‘I have a message from Yahweh,’ I will punish that person and his family.
\v 35 What you should ask each other is, ‘What is Yahweh’s answer?’ or ‘What has Yahweh said?’
\v 36 But stop saying ‘{I have} a message from Yahweh,’ because when each person claims his own words are a message from Yahweh, he distorts the message of the living God, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, our God.
\v 37 This is what you should ask each prophet: ‘What has Yahweh answered you?’ or ‘What has Yahweh said?’
\v 38 But if he says, ‘I have a message from Yahweh,’ then {I} Yahweh say this: ‘Because you have kept saying, “{I have} a message from Yahweh,” even though I told you, “You must not say, ‘{I have} a message from Yahweh.’”
\v 39 So I {Yahweh,} will certainly forget about you and forsake you and this city that I gave to you and to your ancestors. {I will expel you} from my presence.
\v 40 I will cause people to make fun of you forever. People will never forget that I caused you to be ashamed.’”’”
\c 24
\s1 The good figs and the bad figs
\p
\v 1 After King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon had taken away Jeconiah son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, along with the officials of Judah, the craftsmen, and the smiths from Jerusalem and brought them to Babylon, Yahweh showed me something. I saw two baskets of figs that someone had placed in front of the temple of Yahweh.
\v 2 One basket had very good figs in it, like figs that ripen early in the season. The other basket had very bad figs in it that no one could eat because they were so bad.
\p
\v 3 Then Yahweh said to me, “Jeremiah, what do you see?”
\p I replied, “Figs. The good figs are very good, and the bad figs are very bad, so bad that no one could eat them.”
\p
\v 4 Then Yahweh gave me this message. He said,
\v 5 “This is what {I} Yahweh, the God of the Israelite people, say: Like these good figs, similarly I will regard favorably the people of Judah whom I sent away from this place to the land of the Chaldeans.
\v 6 I will watch over them for the purpose of doing good things for them, and I will cause them to return to this land. I will establish them and not destroy them. I will cause them to prosper, and I will not remove them.
\v 7 I will enable them to desire to know me, that I am Yahweh. They will be my people, and I will be their God, for they will return to me sincerely.
\p
\v 8 But, this is {also} what {I,} Yahweh, say: Like the bad figs that are so bad that people cannot eat them {and instead get rid of them}, so I will treat Zedekiah the king of Judah, his officials, those who still remain in Jerusalem, those who are still living in this land, and those who have gone to live in the land of Egypt.
\v 9 I will deliver them over so that horrible things that will harm them will happen to them with the result that people in every kingdom on the earth will reproach them, and they will be the subject of proverbs, and taunts, and curses, in all the places where I will exile them.
\v 10 I will cause them to experience wars and famines and diseases, until they have disappeared from the land that I gave to them and to their ancestors.”
\c 25
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message to {me,} Jeremiah, about all the people of Judah. This happened during the fourth year that Jehoiakim son of Josiah ruled as king of Judah, which was also the first year that Nebuchadnezzar ruled as king of Babylon.
\v 2 {I,} Jeremiah, a prophet {of Yahweh}, spoke {this message} to all the people of Judah and to all the people living in Jerusalem. I said,
\v 3 “For twenty-three years Yahweh has been giving me messages —from the thirteenth year that Josiah son of Amon ruled as king of Judah until today. I have spoken to you again and again, but you have not listened.
\p
\v 4 Yahweh also, repeatedly, sent to you all his {other} prophets who serve him, but you did not heed their warnings. You refused to pay attention.
\v 5 {Those prophets} said, ‘Each of you must stop your evil habits and stop doing evil things. If you do that, you will be able to continue living in this land that Yahweh gave to you and to your ancestors to be yours forever.
\v 6 Do not cause {me,} Yahweh to become angry by serving and worshiping idols that you have made with your hands. If {you do not do those things}, I will not cause harmful things to happen to you.’
\p
\v 7 Yahweh says, ‘Yet you refused to pay attention to the messages that I gave my prophets, and as a result you made me very angry by {worshiping} the idols that you made. That caused harmful things to happen to you.
\p
\v 8 Therefore, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this: Because you have not paid attention to what I said {to you through my prophets},
\v 9 know that I am about to summon all the people groups who live north of you’ —this is what {I,}Yahweh say —‘I will also summon my servant Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon. I will bring them against this land and against the people living in it and against the people of all of these surrounding nations and I will completely destroy them. I will make them an object of horror and scorn, and they will stay in ruins forever.
\v 10 I will cause happy singing and laughing to cease in your land. There will no longer be bridegrooms or brides talking {joyfully}. No one will hear the grinding of millstones, and there will not be people lighting lamps {in the evening}.
\v 11 This entire land will become an empty ruin, and the people of these nations will serve the king of Babylon for seventy years.
\p
\v 12 Then, when seventy years have passed, I will punish the king of Babylon and his people for the wrong things they have done’ —this is what Yahweh says. ‘I will also punish the land of the Chaldeans and make it an empty ruin forever.
\v 13 I will cause them to experience everything that I have said I would do to it —everything that Jeremiah has prophesied against all the nations, everything that {my servant} wrote in this book.
\v 14 The people of many nations and powerful kings will then force the Babylonians to serve them, like the people of Babylonia caused my people to become slaves. I will give the Babylonians what they deserve for the things they have done.’”
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh, the God of {us} Israelites, {gave me a vision. In the vision he was holding a cup of wine}. He said this to me: “Take this cup from my hand. The wine in it {represents} the punishment I am about to send. Go and make all the nations drink from it—all the nations to which I am sending you.
\v 16 When they drink {it}, they will stagger and will act like crazy people because of the wars that I am going to send against them.”
\v 17 So I took the cup from Yahweh, and I made the people of all the nations to which Yahweh had sent me, drink from it.
\v 18 {First I went to} Jerusalem and the {other} cities of Judah, and the king and the officials. And, starting from that day, they all {eventually} lost their positions of authority and became people whom others ridiculed, and they became the object of peoples curses.
\v 19 {In the vision I then went to Egypt and gave some of the wine to} Pharaoh king of Egypt, along with his servants, his officials, and all his people,
\v 20 and all the foreigners {living in Egypt. In the vision I also went to the land of Uz and gave some of the wine to} all the kings of the land of Uz, and all the kings of the land of the Philistines —those in Ashkelon, Gaza, Ekron, and the few people remaining in Ashdod.
\v 21 {I also gave some of the wine to} the people of Edom, Moab, and to the Ammonites.
\v 22 I also {gave some of the wine to} all the kings of Tyre and all the kings of Sidon, and the kings of the coastlands across the sea.
\v 23 {In the vision I then went} to the people of Dedan, Tema, and Buz {and gave them some of the wine}, and all those who cut their hair short on the sides.
\v 24 I also {gave} all the kings of Arabia {some of the wine}, and all the kings of the foreign people who live in the wilderness.
\v 25 I also {gave} all the kings of Zimri, all the kings of Elam, and all the kings of Media {some of the wine}.
\q1 \v 26 {I also gave} all the kings of the north {some of the wine}, both those near and those far away {from Israel}, one after another —{I went to} all the kingdoms on the earth. And {finally in my vision, I went to} the king of Babylon {and gave him the wine that symbolized that Yahweh would punish the people of Babylon because he was angry with them} and he shall drink it after the other kings.
\p
\v 27 “Then {in the vision Yahweh said to me,} tell them, ‘Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this: Drink and become drunk and vomit. Fall down and never get up again, because of the wars that I am sending against you.’
\v 28 If any of them refuse to take the cup from your hand and drink, tell them, ‘Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this: You absolutely must drink!
\v 29 Pay attention! I am already starting to cause the people of my own city to experience disaster and I will certainly punish you! I will punish you, for I am causing there to be wars in all the nations on the earth” —this is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says.
\p
\v 30 “Now prophesy all these words to them and say to them,
\q1 ‘Yahweh will shout loudly from heaven above,
\q2 from the holy place where he lives he will raise his voice.
\q1 He will shout loudly against the people of his own land, so loudly that {it will sound like} the roar of a lion.
\q2 He will shout to everyone on the earth, {like} people shout triumphantly when they are treading on grapes {to make juice for wine}.
\q1 \v 31 Even people in the farthest places on earth will hear the sound of his shouting,
\q2 because Yahweh will bring charges against the nations.
\q1 He will say why he will judge and punish people from all nations.
\q2 He will cause enemies to kill wicked people with swords’” —this is what Yahweh says.
\q1 \v 32 {Tell them that} Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this:
\q2 “Pay attention! There will be disasters in one nation after another.
\q1 I am preparing to punish them {like} a great storm
\q2 that starts in the most distant places on the earth.
\q1 \v 33 On that day, the people whom Yahweh kills
\q2 will lie everywhere, from one end of the earth to the other.
\q1 No one will mourn for them. No one will gather them up
\q2 or bury them. Their bodies will rot on the ground like manure.
\q1 \v 34 Cry and wail, you leaders who are {supposed} to take care of my people {like} shepherds {care for their flock}!
\q2 {You should fall down and} roll in the dust, you leaders who should take care of my people {as if} they were your flock {and you were their shepherd}!
\q1 The time has come for your enemies to slaughter you and scatter you.
\q2 I will allow things to happen to you that will ruin you, like a precious vessel is ruined that falls to the ground and is shattered.
\q1 \v 35 The leaders {who are supposed to be} caring for my people
\q2 will have nowhere to hide
\q2 and no way to escape.
\q1 \v 36 {People will hear} your leaders{, who are supposed to be} caring for my people {like} a shepherd cares for his flock, crying out and wailing
\q2 because {I,} Yahweh, am ruining your nation.
\q1 \v 37 I will allow your enemies to destroy your peaceful fields,
\q2 because {I,} Yahweh, am very angry.
\q1 \v 38 I, Yahweh have left my dwelling place, like a lion leaves his lair {to attack other animals},
\q2 and I will cause your land to become an empty ruin
\q1 because of the fierceness of your enemies
\q2 and because Yahweh is fiercely angry with you.”
\c 26
\p
\v 1 Soon after Jehoiakim son of Josiah began to rule as king of Judah, Yahweh gave {me, Jeremiah,} this message. He said,
\v 2 “This is what {I,} Yahweh, am telling you: Stand in the courtyard {in front} of my temple, and speak to all the people from the various cities in Judah who come there to worship me. Tell them everything that I command you to say to them. Do not leave anything out.
\v 3 {If you do tell them everything,} perhaps they will pay attention and each of them will stop doing evil things. Then I will change my plans and not do to them the disastrous things that I was planning to do to them because of their evil actions.”
\p
\v 4 Yahweh {also} said, “Say to them, ‘This is what Yahweh says: If you do not pay attention to me in order to obey the teachings that I have given to you through Moses,
\v 5 and if you do not listen to what my servants the prophets are saying -- the prophets whom I have sent to you again and again, though you have not payed attention to them --
\v 6 then I will destroy this temple like I destroyed {the one at} Shiloh. And I will cause Jerusalem to be a place whose name people in every nation on the earth will say when they curse someone.”
\p
\v 7 The priests, the prophets, and many other people listened as Jeremiah spoke this message in the temple of Yahweh.
\v 8 But as soon as Jeremiah finished telling them everything that Yahweh had commanded him to say to all the people, then the priests, the prophets, and all the people seized him and said, “You must certainly die!
\v 9 You should not be claiming to speak with Yahweh’s authority and saying that this temple will end up the same as Shiloh and that this city will become so desolate that no one will live in it anymore!” All the people assembled against Jeremiah in the temple of Yahweh.
\p
\v 10 When the officials of Judah heard about all of this, they rushed up from the royal palace to the temple of Yahweh and sat down {to judge the case} at the entrance of the New Gate of Yahweh’s {temple}.
\v 11 The priests and the {false} prophets told the officials and the other people {who were there}, “We should execute this man, because he has prophesied that this city will be destroyed, and you yourselves have heard him say it!”
\p
\v 12 Then Jeremiah replied to all the officials and to all the people. He said, “Yahweh sent me to prophesy everything that you have heard me say about what will happen to this temple and to this city.
\v 13 So now, change your behavior and pay attention to what Yahweh your God has told you to do. If you do that, Yahweh will change his plans and not cause the disastrous things to happen to you that he said he would.
\v 14 As for me, you have control over what happens to me. {So you can} do to me whatever you think is good and right.
\v 15 However, know for certain, that if you kill me, then you will be guilty of killing someone who has not done anything wrong. You will cause yourselves to become guilty, and this city, and everyone who lives here. I tell you truthfully, that Yahweh has sent me to speak all these things in your hearing.”
\p
\v 16 Then the officials and the other people said to the priests and the {false} prophets, “Because Jeremiah has spoken to us with the authority of Yahweh our God, you should not execute him.”
\p
\v 17 Then some of the elders of the land stood up and spoke to all the people who had gathered {there}. They said,
\v 18 “{Think about what} Micah{, the prophet} from the town of Moresheth, prophesied during the time that Hezekiah was the king of Judah. He told the people of Judah, ‘This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says:
\q1 Enemies will plow up Zion {as} farmers plow a field.
\q1 Jerusalem will become a heap of ruins,
\q1 and the temple hill will become an overgrown forest.’
\p
\v 19 King Hezekiah of Judah, and the people of Judah, certainly did not kill Micah {for saying that}! No! Instead, Hezekiah deeply respected Yahweh and pleaded with Yahweh to show mercy to them. So Yahweh changed his plans and did not send the terrible disaster that he had said he would send. If we kill Jeremiah, we will cause disastrous things to happen to us!”
\p
\v 20 And {at that time} there was also a man named Uriah son of Shemaiah from the town of Kiriath Jearim, who was also prophesying with Yahweh’s authority. He was predicting the same disasters for this city and this land that Jeremiah was predicting.
\v 21 When King Jehoiakim, along with all his army officers and officials, heard what Uriah was saying, the king tried to kill Uriah. When Uriah heard about it, he became very afraid and ran away to Egypt.
\v 22 Then King Jehoiakim sent Elnathan son of Akbor along with several other men to Egypt {to get Uriah}.
\v 23 They captured Uriah and brought him from Egypt {back to Jerusalem} to King Jehoiakim. The king then {had a soldier} kill Uriah with a sword. Then they buried his corpse in the place where the common people bury their dead.
\p
\v 24 However, Ahikam son of Shaphan supported Jeremiah and did not allow the mob to kill him.
\c 27
\p
\v 1 Soon after Zedekiah became the king of Judah, Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah. He said,
\v 2 “This is what Yahweh said to me: ‘Make an ox yoke of wooden bars and fasten it around your neck with leather straps.
\v 3 Then send messages to the kings of Edom, Moab, Ammon, Tyre, and Sidon through the ambassadors from those countries who have come to Jerusalem to {meet with} King Zedekiah.
\v 4 Tell the ambassadors to give this message to their kings: “This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelite people worship, says. You must say this to your kings:
\v 5 ‘Because I am very powerful was able to make the earth and the people and the animals that live on it and I give the earth to whoever I please.
\v 6 Now I have allowed my servant King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon to control all these lands. I have also given him the wild animals to use as he wishes.
\v 7 The people of all the nations will serve him, and then his son {after him}, and then his grandson {after him}, until the time comes for his own nation {to serve the nations that conquer it}. Then many nations and great kings will conquer him and make him serve them.
\p
\v 8 I will punish any nation or kingdom that refuses to submit to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon and refuses to serve him. I will cause those people to experience war, famine, and diseases”—this is what Yahweh declares—“until I have used Nebuchadnezzar to destroy them completely.
\p
\v 9 So do not listen to your {false} prophets, your fortune-tellers, your dream interpreters, your mediums, or your sorcerers. They keep telling you, ‘The king of Babylon will not conquer our nation.’
\v 10 Those people are prophesying lies to you, which will result in removing you far away from your home. I will drive you out, and you will die {in a foreign land}.
\v 11 However, if the people of any nation submit to the king of Babylon and serve him, I will let them remain in their own land”—this is what Yahweh declares—“and they will farm it and live in it.’”’”
\p
\v 12 Then I gave the same message to King Zedekiah of Judah. I said to him, “Submit to the king of Babylon and serve him and his people, and then you will stay alive.
\v 13 There is no reason for you and your people to die by war, famine, and disease, which is what Yahweh has said will happen to any nation that refuses to serve the king of Babylon.
\v 14 Do not pay attention to the prophets who are saying to you, ‘The king of Babylon will not conquer your country,’ because they are prophesying lies to you.
\v 15 I did not send those prophets”—this is what Yahweh declares—“and yet they are saying that I gave them their messages, but they are lying. The result will be that I will drive you out of your land, and you will die, both you and those {false} prophets who are prophesying {lies} to you.”
\p
\v 16 Then I spoke to the priests and to all this people, and I said, “This is what Yahweh says: Do not pay attention to what your {false} prophets say, who tell you, ‘Look, someone will bring back from Babylon the articles from Yahweh’s temple very soon,’ because they are prophesying lies to you.
\v 17 Do not pay attention to them. Serve the king of Babylon and then he will not kill you. There is no reason for this {entire} city to become a pile of ruins.
\p
\v 18 If those men are really prophets who speak messages from me, then tell them to please plead with me, the commander of the heavenly armies, so that I will not allow {soldiers from Babylon} to carry off to Babylon the articles that {still} remain in my temple, in the palace of the king of Judah, and in {the rest of} Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 19 I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, say this about the pillars {that stand in front of the temple}, the large bronze water basin, the bronze stands, and all the other articles that are still in this city.
\v 20 King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon left those things here when he took Jeconiah son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, away from Jerusalem to Babylon as a prisoner, along with all the leading people of Judah and Jerusalem.
\v 21 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelite people worship, says about the valuable articles that still remain in Yahweh’s temple and in the king’s palace, and in Jerusalem:
\v 22 “Babylonian soldiers will bring them to Babylon. They will stay there until the day that I decide to give them my attention again”—this is what Yahweh declares—“and then I will bring them back and return them to this place.”
\c 28
\p
\v 1 In the fifth month of the fourth year after Zedekiah began to rule as king of Judah, Hananiah the son of Azzur spoke to me. Hananiah, who {claimed to be} a prophet and was from the city of Gibeon spoke to me in the temple of Yahweh while the priests and all the people watched. He said,
\v 2 “This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says: ‘I have ended the control that the king of Babylon has over you.
\v 3 Before two years have ended, I will bring back to this place all the objects from the temple of Yahweh that Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon took from here and carried to Babylon.
\v 4 I will also bring back to this place Jeconiah son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, along with all the people of Judah who went to Babylon {as captives.’} This is what Yahweh declares. ‘I will do this because I will end the control that the king of Babylon has over you.’”
\p
\v 5 Then Jeremiah the prophet replied to Hananiah the prophet while the priests and all the people who stood in the temple of Yahweh watched.
\v 6 Jeremiah the prophet said, “Yes, may it be so! I hope Yahweh does that! I hope Yahweh fulfills everything that you have prophesied, so that people bring back from Babylon the objects from the temple of Yahweh and bring back all the people who are captive there.
\v 7 Nevertheless, listen to what I am saying in your presence and in the presence of all the people:
\v 8 The prophets who preceded me and you, prophesied long ago that many nations and powerful kingdoms would experience wars, terrible suffering, and deadly diseases.
\v 9 So when a prophet prophesies that things will go well, {only} when what that prophet says {actually} happens will people recognize that Yahweh truly sent him.
\p
\v 10 Then Hananiah the prophet grabbed the yoke bar from Jeremiah’s neck and broke it. {The yoke bar represented the control that Babylon had over the nations.}
\v 11 Then Hananiah spoke while all the people watched. He said, “This is what Yahweh says: ‘Just as Hananiah has broken this yoke bar, within two full years I will end the control that Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon has over all the nations.’” After Hananiah said that, Jeremiah the prophet left.
\p
\v 12 After Hananiah the prophet broke the yoke bar that had been on Jeremiah the prophet’s neck, Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah:
\v 13 “Go and tell Hananiah that this is what Yahweh says: ‘You broke yoke bars made of wood, but I will replace them with yoke bars made of iron.
\v 14 I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, say this: I have forced all these nations to serve Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, {as though} they had iron yoke bars on their necks, and they will serve him. And I have given him control over the wild animals as well.’”
\p
\v 15 Then Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananiah the prophet, “Please pay attention to me, Hananiah! Yahweh did not send you and you have certainly told lies to these people, and they have believed your lies.
\v 16 So this is what Yahweh says: ‘I am about to make you die. You will die this year, because you have encouraged people to rebel against Yahweh.’”
\p
\v 17 Hananiah the prophet died in the seventh month of that same year.
\c 29
\p
\v 1-2 After King Jeconiah, his mother, his court officials, the leaders of Judah and Jerusalem, and the craftsmen and metalworkers had left Jerusalem {and gone to Babylon,} Jeremiah the prophet wrote a letter and sent it from Jerusalem to the remaining elders among the exiles, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the other people whom Nebuchadnezzar had taken away from Jerusalem to Babylon.
\p
\v 3 {He sent the letter} through Elasah son of Shaphan, and Gemariah son of Hilkiah, whom Zedekiah the king of Judah was sending to King Nebuchadnezzar in Babylon. This is what the letter said:
\p
\v 4 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this to all of you whom I sent away from Jerusalem to Babylon:
\v 5 Build houses {there} and live in them. Plant gardens and eat what they produce.
\v 6 Marry and have sons and daughters. Find wives for your sons and give your daughters in marriage so that they also have children. Increase in number there; do not become fewer.
\v 7 Also, do things that will cause things to go well for the people who live in the city where I have sent you to live, and pray to Yahweh for that city, because when things go well for that city, things will also go well for you.
\p
\v 8 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this: Do not let your prophets who are among you and your fortune-tellers trick you. Do not pay attention to the dreams that you are encouraging yourselves to dream,
\v 9 because those prophets are lying to you and claiming to speak for me. I did not send them.” That is what Yahweh has declared.
\p
\v 10 “Yahweh says this: When seventy years have passed for Babylon, I will help you and keep my good promise to you by bringing you back to this place.
\v 11 I know what I intend to do for you,” Yahweh has declared. “I intend to help you prosper, not to harm you. I intend to give you a hopeful future.
\v 12 At that time you will call out to me. You will come and pray to me, and I will pay attention to you.
\v 13 You will earnestly desire for me {to help you} and you will find me when you pursue me with complete sincerity.
\v 14 I will let you find me,” Yahweh has declared. “I will bring you back from captivity and gather you from all the nations and all the places where I have scattered you,” Yahweh has declared. “I will bring you back to the place from which I sent you away.”
\p
\v 15 {I am saying this} because you have said, “Yahweh has appointed prophets for us {here} in Babylon.”
\v 16 This is what Yahweh says about the king who sits on David’s throne and about all the people who are living in this city -- your relatives who did not go into exile with you.
\v 17 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this: “Pay attention! I am sending war, famine, and disease against them. I will make them like figs that are so bad that no one can eat them.
\v 18 I am about to cause them to experience wars, famines, and diseases. I will make the people of every kingdom on earth horrified when they hear about them. Among all the nations where I have scattered them, they will be the object of peoples curses, and people will feel shocked, will whistle in contempt, and will mock them.
\v 19 {I will do this} because those people refused to pay attention to what I said to them,” Yahweh has declared, “messages that I persistently gave to my prophets whom I sent to them. But you {exiles also} did not pay attention.” That is what Yahweh has declared.
\p
\v 20 So listen to what Yahweh says, all of you exiles whom I have sent away from Jerusalem to Babylon.
\v 21 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this about Ahab son of Kolaiah and Zedekiah son of Maaseiah, who are speaking lies to you and claiming to speak for me: “I am about to allow the soldiers of King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon, to seize them and take them to King Nebuchadnezzar, and he will execute them while you are watching.
\v 22 All you exiles from Judah in Babylon will say this when you curse someone, ‘I hope Yahweh will do to you what he did to Zedekiah and Ahab, whom the king of Babylon burned in a fire!’
\v 23 {That will happen} because they did something disgraceful among the Israelite people; they committed adultery with their neighbors’ wives and they spoke lies, claiming to speak for me, even though I never told them to say those things. I know about this, and I can testify to it,” Yahweh has declared.
\p
\v 24 {Yahweh told me to} say this to Shemaiah the Nehelamite,
\v 25 “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this: Because you sent letters on your own authority to all the people who are in Jerusalem, and to Zephaniah the priest, son of Maaseiah, and to all the {other} priests, and you wrote:
\p
\v 26 ‘Yahweh has appointed you as priest in place of Jehoiada the priest, to be in charge of overseeing Yahweh’s temple. You should put into the stocks and the iron collar anyone who acts like a crazy person and claims to be a prophet.
\v 27 So why haven’t you done anything to stop Jeremiah from {the town of} Anathoth, who claims to be a prophet among you?
\v 28 For because of this he sent {a message} to us {here} in Babylon, saying, “Your exile will last a long time. Build houses and settle down. Plant gardens and eat what they produce.”’
\v 29 And {when} Zephaniah the priest {received} the letter he {brought it to me and} read it in my presence.
\p
\v 30 Then Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah. Yahweh said,
\v 31 “Send {a message} to all the exiles and tell them, ‘Yahweh says this about Shemaiah the Nehelamite: Because Shemaiah has prophesied to you even though I did not send him, and he has caused you to trust in lies,
\v 32 because of that Yahweh says: know that I am about to punish Shemaiah the Nehelamite and his descendants. None of his descendants will live among this people, and he will not experience the good things that I will do for my people,’” Yahweh has declared, “’because he has incited you to rebel against Yahweh.’”
\c 30
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave {another} message to Jeremiah. He said,
\v 2 “This is what {I,} Yahweh, the God whom the people of Israel worship, am telling you. You {Jeremiah} should write down in a scroll everything that I have told you.
\v 3 I want you to know that a time is coming when I will free my people Israel and Judah from captivity {in Babylon}.” This is what Yahweh declared. He {also} said, “I will bring them back to the land that I gave to their ancestors, and they will take possession of it again.”
\p
\v 4 Yahweh gave this message {to me, Jeremiah} regarding the people of Israel and Judah.
\v 5 Yes, this is what Yahweh says: “You hear people crying out because they are terrified, and there is no safety {for them}.
\q1 \v 6 Think about this:
\q2 Men certainly do not give birth to babies.
\q1 Yet, I see every strong man
\q2 with their hands on their stomachs
\q2 like a woman who is about to give birth.
\q1 Everyone’s face has turned pale {because they are afraid}.
\q1 \v 7 How terrible that time will be.
\q2 There will never be another time like it.
\q1 It will be a time when the people of Israel will suffer greatly,
\q2 but God will rescue them from that suffering.
\p
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this: “At that time, I will free my people from the King of Babylon who has has been forcing them to obey him. People in other countries will no longer be their bosses.
\q1 \v 9 Instead, my people will serve {me,} Yahweh their God, and they will serve a king whom I will appoint for them, a king who is a descendant of King David.
\p
\q1 \v 10 So you people of Israel who serve me,
\q2 do not be afraid.” This is what Yahweh declares.
\q2 “You should not worry because I will certainly rescue you and your descendants from the land where others have taken you as prisoners.
\q1 Then you people of Israel will return and will live peacefully and safely,
\q2 and no nation will cause you to fear.
\q1 \v 11 I am with you in order to rescue you.” This is what Yahweh declared. He said, “I will completely destroy all the nations where I have scattered you, but I will not completely destroy you. I will discipline you fairly, but I will certainly not let you go without any punishment.”
\p
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh {also} says this {to his people}: “
\q1 You have suffered very much.
\q2 It is {as though} you have a terrible wound that cannot be healed.
\q1 \v 13 There is no one helping you;
\q2 {you have suffered much and it is as though} there is no medicine that can heal your wounds.
\q1 \v 14 The nations that used to be your allies have all abandoned you
\q2 and no longer care about you.
\q1 I have punished you severely,
\q2 {like} your enemies would wound you,
\q1 because you have sinned so many times.
\q1 \v 15 So do not complain about how I have punished you;
\q2 your suffering that seems never ending.
\q1 I did these things to you
\q2 because you have sinned so many times
\q1 \v 16 I will cause {foreign armies} to destroy all those who try to destroy you,
\q2 and all of your enemies will certainly go away as prisoners.
\q1 Everyone who steals your possessions will lose their own possessions,
\q2 and everyone who attacks you will be the ones whom others attack.
\q1 \v 17 People say that you are outcasts
\q2 and that you live in Jerusalem, a city that no one cares about.” This is what Yahweh declared. He says,
\q1 “I will cause things to go well again for you.
\p
\q1 \v 18 This is what Yahweh says: “I be compassionate to the people of Israel and allow them to return to their ruined homes. People will rebuild the city of Jerusalem on top of its ruins, and the palace will stand again in its proper place.
\q1 \v 19 The people will sing songs of thanksgiving and they will rejoice. I will make them more numerous so that they will not become few, and I will cause others to honor them so that no one looks down on them.
\q1 \v 20 Their children will thrive as they did long ago. I will establish their community in my presence, and I will punish every nation that oppresses them.
\q1 \v 21 Their leader will be one of their own people, and their ruler will come from among them. I will invite him to approach me, and he will come into my presence, because no one would dare to come near to me unless I invited him.” This is what Yahweh declared.
\q1 \v 22 “You will be my people, and I will be your God.”
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh will be very angry;
\q2 he will punish wicked people; and will act against them
\q1 like a powerful storm that swirls around them.
\q1 \v 24 Yahweh will not stop being angry
\q2 until he has accomplished everything that he has planned to do.
\q1 In the future, you will clearly understand all of this.
\c 31
\p
\v 1 Yahweh declares this: “At that time, I will be the God whom all the clans of Israel worship, and they will be my people.”
\p
\v 2 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “The people who survived when their enemies attacked them in war
\q2 saw that I showed kindness to them, even out in the desert;
\q2 when the people of Israel sought rest.”
\p
\v 3 Yahweh appeared to us, Israelite people, in the past and said,
\q1 “I have loved you in the past and I will continue loving you forever.
\q2 Because of this I have lovingly drawn you close to myself.
\q1 \v 4 People of Israel, I will make you a strong nation again. You will again pick up your tambourines and joyfully dance.
\q1 \v 5 You will once again plant vineyards on the hills of Samaria, and you yourselves will enjoy eating the grapes that you grow there.
\q1 \v 6 There will be a time when watchmen will call out from the hills in the region of Ephraim, ‘Come, let us go up to Jerusalem, to {where} Yahweh our God {dwells so that we might worship him there!’”
\b
\p
\v 7 This is also what {I,} Yahweh say:
\q1 “Sing joyfully {about what I have done} for the people of Israel!
\q1 Shout because your nation is the greatest of the nations!
\q2 Loudly praise me, saying,
\q2 ‘Yahweh, rescue your people,
\q2 those who are still alive in Israel!’
\q1 \v 8 Know that I will bring my people back from the lands in the north, and I will gather them from the most distant places on the earth. Among them will be people who are blind and people who cannot walk well, women who are pregnant and women who are about to give birth. A huge group of people will return here.
\q1 \v 9 They will be crying as they return,
\q2 and as they pray to me, I will lead them back.
\q1 I will guide them to streams of water,
\q2 along a smooth road where they will not trip.
\q1 I will do this because I care for the people of Israel as a father cares for his children,
\q2 and the people of Ephraim are as dear to me as a man’s oldest son.
\b
\q1 \v 10 People of the nations, listen to this message from {me,} Yahweh, and proclaim it to the people who live on the coastlands far away. Say, ‘The one who scattered the people of Israel will gather them again and will guard them, just as a shepherd takes care of his sheep.’
\q1 \v 11 {I,} Yahweh will buy the Israelite people back from those who were more powerful than they were.
\q1 \v 12 So they will return and sing joyfully on the heights of Jerusalem, and they will rejoice because of the good things that I give them—
\q2 grain and new wine and olive oil and young sheep and cattle.
\q1 They will be strong and joyful, like a garden that gets plenty of water,
\q2 and they will never be sad again.
\q1 \v 13 Then the young women will dance joyfully, and the young men and the old men will rejoice along with them. I will cause them to be joyful instead of mourning; I will comfort them and I will make them glad instead of sad.
\q1 \v 14 I will give the priests plenty of food, and I will completely satisfy my people with the good things that I give them. I, Yahweh, declare that this will happen!
\b
\p
\v 15 Yahweh says this:
\q1 “People in the town of Ramah heard a sound, the sound of women wailing and weeping bitterly.
\q1 {The women who were the descendants of Ephraim and Manasseh, the two grandsons of} Rachel {the wife of Jacob,} were weeping for their sons
\q2 and refusing to let anyone comfort them,
\q2 because their sons were dead.”
\p
\v 16 Yahweh says this:
\q1 “Do not cry anymore
\q2 and stop shedding tears, because I will reward you for {the good} things you have done;
\q1 your children will return from the land of their enemies.
\q1 \v 17 You can confidently expect that I will cause good things to happen to you in the future;
\q2 your children will return to their own land.” This is what Yahweh declares.
\b
\q1 \v 18 I, Yahweh, have certainly heard the people of Israel grieving and saying to me,
\q2 ‘You disciplined us severely,
\q2 the way a farmer trains a calf that is not trained {to wear a yoke so that it can work}.
\q1 Bring us back {to you}, and we will return to you,
\q2 because you are Yahweh our God.
\q1 \v 19 We turned away {from you},
\q2 but afterward we were sorry for our sin;
\q1 after you caused us to understand {that we were guilty because of what we had done}, we struck our thighs {to show that we were very ashamed}.
\q2 We felt greatly ashamed because of the wrong things we did when we were young.’
\b
\q1 \v 20 The people of Israel are certainly my precious children, the ones I delight in.
\q1 For as often as I have to punish them,
\q2 but I still certainly remember them.
\q1 So my inner being yearns for them,
\q2 and I will certainly act mercifully toward them.” Yahweh, declares this.
\b
\q1 \v 21 {You people of Israel,} set up road signs for yourselves;
\q2 put up for yourselves posts {along the roads}
\q2 to mark the road on which you traveled away from here.
\q1 Return my precious people of Israel,
\q2 return to your towns here.
\q1 \v 22 You people who have turned away from me like daughters who have forsaken their parents,
\q2 you have consistently been unfaithful to me.
\q1 I, Yahweh, will cause something new to happen in the land:
\q2 the women of Israel will be protecting the strong warriors!”
\b
\p
\v 23 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the people of Israel worship, says: “When I bring my people back to the land of Judah from the countries where they were taken, the people in the cities of Judah will once again say, ‘We pray that Yahweh will bless this place where he lives, this sacred mountain!’
\v 24 The people of Judah and everyone in its cities will live there together, including the farmers and the shepherds who move around with their flocks.
\v 25 I will satisfy those who are weary, and I will refresh all those who are sorrowful.”
\b
\p
\v 26 After {I, Jeremiah,} dreamed those things, I woke up and looked around. I had slept very peacefully!
\b
\p
\v 27 Yahweh, declares “Pay attention, there will be a time when I will greatly increase the number of people and the number of animals in the land of Israel and the land of Judah.
\v 28 In the past, I made sure that their enemies removed the people from their land, tore down what they had built, and ruined their land, bringing many disasters on it. But in the future, I will make sure that they build houses and plant crops again.” Yahweh, declares this.
\v 29 {Previously, the people often said,} ‘The parents ate sour grapes, but it is their children’s teeth that became numb.’ {They meant that it was not fair for God to punish them for their ancestors’ sins. But when I bring them back, they will no longer say that}.
\m
\v 30 Instead, each person will die because of his own sin; {so that it will be as if} anyone who eats sour grapes, will have their own teeth become numb.”
\v 31 Yahweh, declares this, “Know that there will be a time when I will make a new agreement with the people of Israel and with the people of Judah.
\v 32 This new agreement will not be like the agreement that I made with their ancestors when I took them by their hands and led them out of Egypt. They disobeyed that agreement, even though I cared for them the way a husband cares for his wife.” Yahweh, declares this.
\v 33 “This is the {new} agreement that I{, Yahweh} will make with the people of Israel after that time: I will cause them to love and believe my teachings in their inner beings. I will be their God, and they will be my people.” Yahweh declares this.
\v 34 They will no longer need to teach their neighbors and their relatives, saying, ‘{You need to} know Yahweh,’ because everyone, from the least important to the most important, will {already} know me. And I will forgive them for the wicked things they have done, and I will never again think about the sins that they committed.” Yahweh declares this.
\p
\v 35 Yahweh is the one who provides the sun
\q2 to give light during the day, and who causes
\q1 the moon and the stars to give light during the night.
\q1 He stirs up the sea, so that its waves roar.
\q1 His name is Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 and this is what he says:
\p
\v 36 “Just like I have established those laws to govern the universe and will never get rid of those laws, so I will not ever allow the Israelites to stop being a nation in my presence.” Yahweh, declares this.
\p
\v 37 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “No one is able to measure the sky above
\q2 or to find out what supports the earth below.
\q1 In the same way, I will certainly never reject all the descendants of Israel,
\q2 for all {the evil} things that they have done.” Yahweh declares this.
\p
\v 38 Yahweh also declares this: “Pay attention to this: there will be a time when people will rebuild the {whole} city of Jerusalem for me, from the Tower of Hananel {at the northeast corner, west} to {the gate named} the Corner Gate.
\v 39 Then workers will again stretch a measuring string straight over Gareb Hill and around to Goah.
\v 40 And the whole area, including the valley where they throw dead bodies and ashes, and all the fields as far as the Kidron Valley, over to the corner of the Horse Gate on the east side, I will set apart as holy for myself. People will never again tear down the city of Jerusalem or destroy it.”
\c 32
\p
\v 1 After Zedekiah had been ruling Judah for almost ten years, Yahweh gave me this message. That was during the time that Nebuchadnezzar {had been ruling Babylonia} for almost eighteen years.
\p
\v 2 At that time, the army of the king of Babylon was surrounding Jerusalem, {and King Zedekiah} had locked me in a prison area in the courtyard where the guards of the king of Judah’s palace {stayed}.
\v 3 King Zedekiah had put me there because I had kept prophesying. I had kept saying, “Yahweh says this: ‘I am about to hand this city over to the king of Babylon, and his army will capture it.
\v 4 King Zedekiah, king of Judah, will not escape from the power of the army of Babylonia. {Instead, I will certainly hand him over to the king of Babylon, and Zedekiah will speak with the king of Babylon face to face and will see him with his own eyes.
\v 5 The king of Babylon will force Zedekiah to go to Babylon, and he will remain there until I decide how to punish him. So even though {Zedekiah and his soldiers fight against the army of Babylonia, they will not succeed.’” Yahweh declares this.
\p
\v 6 At that time, I, Jeremiah, said this: “Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 7 “Listen! {Your cousin} Hanamel, the son of your uncle Shallum, will come to you. He will say to you, ‘Buy my field at Anathoth, because you are my closest relative, so you have the right to buy it before anyone else does.’”
\p
\v 8 And just as Yahweh had predicted, my cousin Hanamel came to see me in the courtyard where the guards stayed. He said to me, “Please buy my field at Anathoth in the area where the descendants of Benjamin live. Because you are my closest relative, you have the right to buy it and to keep it in our family. So buy it for yourself.” Then I knew that this message had truly come from Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 So I bought the field at Anathoth from my cousin Hanamel. I weighed out for him the silver {to pay for it}, which was about seven ounces of silver.
\v 10 I signed and sealed the deed {on which it was written that I was buying it,} while others were witnessing it. Then I weighed the silver on a scale {and gave it to him}.
\v 11 Then I took the deeds of purchase. I took {both} the sealed and the unsealed {copy}, which stated the terms and conditions {of the purchase}.
\p
\v 12 And I gave them to Baruch, who was the son of Neriah and the grandson of Mahseiah. I did this while my cousin Hanamel, the witnesses who had signed the document, and all the men of Judah who were sitting in the courtyard where the guards stayed were watching.
\p
\v 13 While all of them were watching, I commanded Baruch. I said this,
\v 14 “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom we Israelites worship, says this: ‘Take {both of these documents of purchase, the sealed copy and the copy that is not sealed, and put them in a clay jar, so that they will last for a long time.
\v 15 {Do that} because Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom we Israelites worship, says this: {Some day} people will again buy houses and fields and vineyards in this land.’”
\p
\v 16 After I had given the deed of purchase to Baruch, the son of Neriah, I prayed to Yahweh. I said,
\mi
\v 17 Oh Yahweh {my} Lord! Surely, because of how very powerful you are, you yourself were able to make the sky and the earth. Nothing is too difficult for you {to do}.
\v 18 You faithfully love thousands of people, but you also punish children for the sins that their parents committed. You are the great and mighty God; you are Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\v 19 You make wise plans and you do many {mighty} deeds. You know everything that people do, and you reward each person according to how that person has behaved.
\v 20 You performed many miracles in the land of Egypt, {miracles that people still remember} to this day. And you continue {to work great miracles here} in Israel and throughout the world. And you have made yourself famous to this day.
\v 21 You brought our Israelite ancestors out of the land of Egypt by performing many miracles by your very great power, causing your enemies to be greatly terrified.
\v 22 You gave us Israelite people this land that you had solemnly promised to give to our ancestors, a very fertile and productive land.
\v 23 Our ancestors came here and took control of this land and started to live in it, but they did not obey you or do all that you commanded them to do through Moses. So you caused them to experience all these disasters.
\pi
\v 24 Look! {The army of Babylonia} has built ramps up against the walls of our city in order to capture it. Because of war and because of famine and diseases, the army of Babylonia, which is fighting against us, is about to capture our city. The things that you said would happen are happening, and you see it happening.
\v 25 Yet you yourself, Yahweh {my} Lord, told me, ‘Buy the field for yourself with silver and have some men watch you do it so that they can testify about it’— even though the army of Babylonia is about to capture this city!
\s1 Yahweh's reply about buying the land
\p
\v 26 Then gave Yahweh me this message:
\v 27 “I am Yahweh, the God who rules over every living thing. Know for certain, that there is nothing that is too difficult for me to do!
\v 28 So {I,} Yahweh, say this: I am certainly about to hand this city over to the army of Babylonia and to their king, king Nebuchadnezzar, and his army will capture it.
\v 29 The soldiers of Babylonia who are now attacking this city will enter it and set it on fire and burn it down. They will burn down the houses where the people made me very angry by burning incense on the tops of their roofs to honor Baal and by pouring out offerings of wine to other gods.
\p
\v 30 The people of Israel and the people of Judah have done only what I consider to be evil from the time that they became a nation. The people of Israel have surely made me very angry by the idols that they made.” Yahweh declares this.
\v 31 From the time that the Israelite people took Jerusalem {from the Jebusites} and began expanding it and fortifying it, until now, the people of this city have made me extremely angry. So now I will remove it from my presence.
\v 32 The people of Israel and the people of Judah have made me angry by all the evil things that they did. This includes their kings, their officials, their priests, their prophets, and all the people of Judah and the people who live in Jerusalem.
\v 33 My people refused to pay attention to me. Although I taught them again and again, yet they would not pay attention in order to act upon the things I told them.
\v 34 They set up their detestable idols in the temple which belongs to me, and so they caused it to become unacceptable for people to worship me there.
\v 35 They built shrines for worshiping Baal in the Valley of Ben Hinnom, and there they sacrificed their sons and daughters in the fire to the god Molech. I never commanded them to do that; and I did not even think about commanding them to do such a detestable thing. By doing it, they caused the people of Judah to sin.
\s1 The promise to restore Judah and Israel
\p
\v 36 But now, therefore, {I,} Yahweh, the God whom you Israelite people {say you belong to}, say this about this city about which you people of Jerusalem are saying, ‘The army of the king of Babylon is about to capture it by warfare and because of famines and diseases.’
\v 37 {But I, Yahweh, say,} Listen! I will certainly gather my people from all the countries to which I will force them to go because I am extremely angry {with them}. I will bring them back to this place and allow them to live here safely.
\v 38 They will be my people, and I myself will be their God.
\v 39 I will cause them to have a single-minded purpose and way of behaving so that they will revere me forever. This will benefit them and their descendants.
\v 40 I will make an agreement with them that will last forever: I will never stop doing good things for them, and I will cause them to revere me, so that they will not stop obeying me.
\v 41 I will be happy to do good things for them, and I will faithfully settle them permanently in this land. I will do that wholeheartedly and enthusiastically.
\p
\v 42 {I,} Yahweh, say this: ‘Just as I caused my people to experience all these great disasters, so I will also do for them all the good things that I have promised them.
\v 43 {People} will {once again} buy fields in this land, the land about which you people of Jerusalem now say, “It is desolate. There are no longer any people or animals here, and the army of Babylonia has captured it.”
\v 44 People will buy fields with silver, sign documents, seal them, and have other men watch them do it so that they can testify about it. People will do that in the land where the descendants of Benjamin live, in the villages around Jerusalem, in the cities of Judah, in the cities of the hill country, in the cities of the western foothills, and in the cities of the southern wilderness. I will cause my people to prosper again.’” Yahweh declares this.
\c 33
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah a second message while soldiers were still guarding me in the guard’s courtyard {inside King Zedekiah’s palace}. Yahweh said,
\v 2 “This is what {I,} Yahweh, the one who made the earth, Yahweh who shaped it and put it in its place, say {to the people of Jerusalem:}—My name is Yahweh.
\v 3 ‘Call out to me, and I will respond to you; I will tell you great things that I have previously hidden and that you could never discover on your own and do not know.’
\v 4 This is what I, Yahweh, the God of Israel, say about the houses of this city and the palaces of the kings of Judah: ‘{The people of this city} have torn down {some of} their houses, and {even parts of} the king’s palace, to get materials to strengthen the walls around the city, {so that the soldiers of Babylonia will not be able to break through the walls after they climb up} the ramps that they have built against the walls and {kill the inhabitants of the city with} their swords.
\v 5 The people are expecting to fight against the army from Babylonia, but {what will happen is that} the corpses of the men of this city will fill these houses. I will let their enemies kill those men because I am extremely angry with them. I have abandoned this city because of all the wicked things that its people have done.
\v 6 Know that there will be a time when I will restore the people of this city. I will reveal to them my abundantly peaceful way of living and teach them my perfectly right way to live.
\v 7 I will cause the people of Judah and the people of Israel to prosper again, and I will enable them to rebuild their towns as they were before.
\v 8 I will remove all their guiltiness that they incurred by sinning against me, and I will forgive them for all the evil things that they have done against me by which they rebelled against me.
\v 9 When that happens people from all the nations of the earth will rejoice and praise me and honor me. They will hear about all the good things that I am doing for the people of this city, and because of that they will fear me, and they will tremble because I have blessed the people in this city with many good things and have caused them to live peacefully.’
\p
\v 10 This is also what {I,} Yahweh, say: ‘You people are saying that this land is a desolate land that no longer has people and animals living in it. However, some day in the streets of Jerusalem that are now empty of people and animals, and in the other towns of Judah, people will hear
\v 11 others making joyful sounds. People will hear the sound of brides and bridegrooms {celebrating}. They will hear the sound of people saying, “Give thanks to Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies for he is good and faithfully loves us forever,” as they bring their offerings to my temple. {They will sing that song} because I will cause the people of this land to prosper as much as they did before.’ {This is what I} Yahweh say.
\p
\v 12 This is what {I,} Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, say: ‘This land is now desolate; there are no people or animals living here However, in this land there will again be pastures where shepherds will lead their flocks to rest.
\v 13 In the cities of the hill country, in the cities of the western foothills, in the cities of the southern wilderness, in the region where the descendants of Benjamin live, in the areas around Jerusalem, and in the cities of Judah, shepherds will again count their sheep as the sheep walk past them.’ {That will surely happen because I,} Yahweh have said it.
\p
\v 14 Listen to this! A time is coming when I{, Yahweh} will do for the people of Israel and the people of Judah all the good things that I promised to do for them.
\q1 \v 15 At that time I will appoint a righteous man who will be a descendant of King David.
\q2 Throughout the land, he will do what is just and right.
\q1 \v 16 At that time, I will rescue the people of Judah,
\q2 and the people who live in Jerusalem will be safe.
\q1 People will call their city, ‘Yahweh is the one who makes things right for us.’
\p
\v 17 This is also what {I,} Yahweh, say: ‘There will always be a descendant of King David to rule over the people of Israel.
\v 18 There will also always be priests, who are descendants of Levi, standing in front of me to serve me. They will offer sacrifices that they completely burn on the altar, and they will burn grain offerings and present other sacrifices to me.’”
\p
\v 19 Then Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah {another} message. He said,
\v 20 “This is what {I,} Yahweh, say: ‘I have set up the fixed order that governs the daytime and the nighttime, so that they always come at their proper times, and you certainly cannot annul that order.
\v 21 In a similar way, you cannot annul the covenant that I made with King David, who served me, my promise that his descendants will always rule as kings. You also cannot annul my covenant with the descendants of Levi, who serve me as priests.
\v 22 As no one can count the stars in the sky, and no one can measure the grains of sand along the seashore. In a similar way, I will give a huge number of descendants to King David, who serves me, and to the descendants of Levi, who serve me as priests.’”
\p
\v 23 Then Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah, another message. He said,
\v 24 “You have surely noticed what these people are saying. They say, ‘Yahweh chose the two groups, the people {of Israel} and the people {of Judah}, but now he has rejected them.’ By saying this, they are despising my people, and they no longer regard the Israelites as a nation.
\v 25 {However,} this is what I, Yahweh, say: ‘I will certainly not change my laws of nature that I have established to control the day and the night, the sky and the earth.
\v 26 Just as surely as I have firmly established those laws of nature, I will never abandon the descendants of Jacob or the descendants of David, who served me. I will always choose rulers from among David’s descendants to rule over the descendants of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. I will cause them to prosper again, and I will act mercifully toward them.’”
\c 34
\p
\v 1 King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon came with his whole army and with the armies of all the kingdoms that he ruled, and they fought against Jerusalem and against all the other cities of Judah. At that time, Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah, this message. He said,
\v 2 “{I am} Yahweh, the God whom you Israelites worship, and I am telling you to go to Zedekiah, the king of Judah, and tell him this. ‘Yahweh, says this: “I am about to enable the army of the king of Babylon to capture this city, and they will burn it down.
\v 3 You will not escape from them. They will surely capture you and hand you over to the control of the king of Babylon. You will meet the king of Babylon in person and speak with him directly, and {then} they will take you to Babylon.’
\p
\v 4 But King Zedekiah, listen to what I, Yahweh, promise about you: ‘You will not die in battle.
\v 5 You will die peacefully. {At your funeral} people will burn incense to honor you, just as they did for your ancestors, the kings who ruled before you. They will mourn for you, {crying out,} “We are very sad, our master!” This {will surely happen} because I myself have spoken it. This is what {I,} Yahweh, declare.’”
\p
\v 6 So {I,} Jeremiah the prophet, told all of this to King Zedekiah while I was in Jerusalem.
\v 7 At that time the army of the king of Babylon was attacking Jerusalem and all the remaining cities of Judah, Lachish and Azekah. Because those were the only cities in Judah that had high walls around them that the Babylonians had not yet captured.
\p
\v 8 Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah, a message after King Zedekiah had made a solemn agreement with all the people who were in Jerusalem, that they must free their slaves.
\v 9 {He decreed that} the people must free their Hebrew slaves, both the men and the women. No one was to keep a fellow Jew as a slave any longer.
\v 10 So all the officials and the rest of the people who had agreed to this obeyed, and they freed their slaves.
\v 11 But later they changed their minds. They forced the men and women whom they had freed to become their slaves again.
\p
\v 12 So Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah, this message. He said,
\v 13 “{I,} Yahweh, the God whom you Israelites worship, say this. Long ago, when I rescued your ancestors from Egypt, where they were slaves, I made a solemn agreement with them. I told them this:
\v 14 ‘Every seventh year, each of you must free any fellow Hebrew who has sold himself to you and has served you for six years. You must let him go free.’ But your ancestors did not pay any attention to what I said.
\v 15 Recently, you yourselves did what pleases me: you stopped doing what was wrong. Each of you promised to free your fellow Israelites, and you made a solemn agreement about this before me in my temple, the place that belongs to me.
\v 16 But now you have disregarded what you solemnly promised and dishonored me. Each of you has taken back the male and female slaves whom you had freed to live where they desired, and you have forced them to be your slaves again.
\p
\v 17 Therefore, this is what {I,} Yahweh, say: ‘Because you have not obeyed me by each of you freeing your fellow Israelites, I will certainly now set you free—free to die in war, from disease, and from famine! I will make the people from all the kingdoms of the earth horrified when they see what happens to you.
\v 18 I will cut apart the people who have disobeyed what I said in my solemn agreement and who did not do what they solemnly promised before me. To confirm that agreement, they cut a calf into two pieces and walked between the pieces.
\v 19 {This includes} the officials of Judah and the officials of Jerusalem, the palace officials, the priests, and all the rest of the common people—everyone who walked between the pieces of the calf.
\v 20 I will enable their enemies, who want to kill them, to capture them, and their enemies will kill them. Their dead bodies will become food for the birds and the wild animals.
\p
\v 21 I will enable the army of the king of Babylon to capture King Zedekiah of Judah and his officials. Although the king of Babylon and his army have left Jerusalem {for a short time}.
\v 22 Know that I{, Yahweh,} am giving the command that will cause the army of Babylon to return to this city. They will attack it, capture it, and burn it down. And I will make sure that the cities of Judah are destroyed and no longer have anyone living in them.’” That is what Yahweh declares.
\c 35
\p
\v 1 {Several years earlier,} when Jehoiakim, whose father was Josiah, was the king of Judah, Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah, this message. He said,
\v 2 “Go to the place where the families of the Rechabite clan live. Invite them to my temple. {When they arrive,} take them into one of the side rooms and offer them wine.”
\p
\v 3 So I went to get Jaazaniah and his brothers and all his sons, who represented the Rechabite clan. Jaazaniah was the son of {another man named} Jeremiah and the grandson of Habazziniah.
\v 4 I took them to the temple of Yahweh, {and we went} into the room where the sons of Hanan stayed. Hanan, the son of Igdaliah, was a prophet. That room was next to the room that the officials used, and it was above the room that belonged to Maaseiah the son of Shallum, who was the gatekeeper of the temple.
\p
\v 5 Then I set pitchers full of wine and some cups in front of them, and I said to them, “Drink some wine.”
\v 6 They {refused} and said, “We do not drink wine, because our ancestor Jonadab son of Rechab commanded us that we and our descendants must never drink wine.
\v 7 He also told us that we must not build houses or sow seed or plant vineyards, nor own any of these things. But instead, we must always live in tents, so that we would live for many years in this land where we live as foreigners.
\v 8 So we have obeyed everything that our ancestor Jonadab commanded us. We, our wives, our sons, and our daughters have never drunk wine.
\v 9 We have not built houses to live in, and we do not have vineyards or fields or crops.
\v 10 We have lived in tents, and we have obeyed everything that our ancestor Jonadab commanded us.
\p
\v 11 However, when King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon and his army attacked this country, we said, ‘We must go to Jerusalem to escape from the Babylonian army and the Aramean army.’ So we are now living in Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah, this message. He said,
\v 13 “Go and tell the people of Judah and those who live in Jerusalem that this is what {I,} Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, say to them: ‘You refuse to accept my correction and pay attention to my messages’”. That is what Yahweh declares.
\v 14 The descendants of Jonadab son of Rechab have obeyed what their ancestor commanded them, so they do not drink wine to this day. I, however, have spoken to you people again and again, but you have not obeyed me.
\v 15 I have sent all my servants the prophets to you again and again. They told you, “Each one of you must stop doing wicked things and do what is good. Stop worshiping other gods. Then you will live in the land that I gave to you and your ancestors.” Yet you did not pay attention to me or obey me.
\v 16 The descendants of Jonadab son of Rechab have obeyed the command that their ancestor gave them, but you people have refused to obey me.’
\p
\v 17 So this is what {I,} Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, say: ‘Know that I am going to bring on the people of Judah and everyone living in Jerusalem all the disasters that I threatened because I spoke to them, but they did not pay attention; I called out to them, but they did not respond.’”
\p
\v 18 Then {I,} Jeremiah, said to the Rechabite clan, “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this: ‘Because you have obeyed the command of your ancestor Jonadab and have kept all his instructions and have done everything that he commanded you,
\v 19 therefore Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, promises this: ‘There will always be descendants of Jonadab son of Rechab who will serve before me.’”
\c 36
\p
\v 1 During the fourth year that Jehoiakim son of Josiah was the king of Judah, Yahweh spoke this message to {me,} Jeremiah. He said,
\v 2 “Take a scroll and write on it all the messages that I have spoken to you about Israel and about Judah and about all the other nations. Write down everything that I have told you, from the time when Josiah was king until today.
\v 3 It may be that the people of Judah will hear about all the disasters that I am planning to bring on them, each of them will stop behaving wickedly. If they do that, I will forgive them for their sinful behavior.”
\p
\v 4 So {I,} Jeremiah summoned Baruch son of Neriah, and while I spoke aloud all the messages that Yahweh had given me. Baruch wrote them on a scroll.
\v 5 Then {I,}Jeremiah instructed Baruch, “The authorities have forbidden me to enter the temple of Yahweh.
\v 6 You must go there {instead}. On a day when the people are abstaining from food, read aloud from the scroll the messages of Yahweh that I dictated to you, so that the people in Yahweh’s temple can hear them. Read them aloud also so that all the people of Judah who come there from their cities can hear them.
\v 7 Perhaps they will humbly plead with Yahweh {that he would act mercifully toward them,} and each of them will stop behaving wickedly, because Yahweh is extremely angry with these people and has threatened that he will punish them severely.”
\v 8 Baruch son of Neriah did everything that {I,} Jeremiah the prophet had commanded him {to do}. He read aloud from the scroll the messages of Yahweh at the temple of Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 During the ninth month of the fifth year that Jehoiakim son of Josiah was the king of Judah, all the people in Jerusalem and all the people who had come to Jerusalem from the cities of Judah proclaimed a time of fasting before Yahweh.
\v 10 So at the temple of Yahweh, Baruch read aloud from the scroll the messages that {I,} Jeremiah, {had given him}, so that all the people could hear them. He read them in the room that belonged to Gemariah son of Shaphan, the scribe. That room was in the upper courtyard, next to the entrance of the New Gate of the temple of Yahweh.
\p
\v 11 Micaiah, the son of Gemariah and the grandson of Shaphan, heard all the messages of Yahweh {that Baruch read} from the scroll.
\v 12 Then he went down to the palace, into the room of the scribe, where all the king’s officials were sitting. {They were} Elishama the scribe, Delaiah son of Shemaiah, Elnathan son of Akbor, Gemariah son of Shaphan, Zedekiah son of Hananiah, and all the {other} officials.
\v 13 Micaiah told them everything that he had heard when Baruch read aloud from the scroll to the people.
\v 14 So all the officials sent Jehudi, the son of Nethaniah and the grandson of Shelemiah and the great-grandson of Cushi, to say to Baruch, “Bring us that scroll that you read aloud to the people, and come here.” So Baruch son of Neriah took the scroll in his hand and went to them.
\v 15 They said to him, “Please sit down and read it to us.” So Baruch read it aloud to them.
\v 16 When they heard all the messages, they became afraid, each person expressing this to their neighbor. They said to Baruch, “We must certainly report all these messages to the king.”
\v 17 Then they asked Baruch, “Please tell us: How did you get all these messages? Did Jeremiah dictate them to you?”
\v 18 Baruch answered them, “{Yes,} Jeremiah spoke all these messages aloud to me, and I wrote them on the scroll with ink.”
\v 19 Then the officials said to Baruch, “You and Jeremiah must go and hide. Do not let anyone know where you are.”
\p
\v 20 The officials put the scroll in the room of Elishama the scribe. Then they went to the king in the courtyard and reported to him everything {that Baruch had read to them}.
\v 21 So the king sent Jehudi to bring the scroll, and Jehudi brought it from the room of Elishama the scribe. Jehudi read it aloud to the king and to all the officials who were standing beside the king.
\v 22 It was the ninth month, {the cold season,} so the king was sitting in the part of the palace where he lived during the winter, and in front of him {a fire} was burning in a pot {to keep him warm.}
\v 23 Each time Jehudi finished reading three or four columns of the scroll, the king cut off that section with a scribe’s knife and threw it into the fire in the pot. He did this again and again, until the whole scroll had finished {burning} in the fire that was in the pot.
\v 24 The king and all his officials who heard all these messages were not afraid, and they did not tear their clothes {to show that they were sorry for what they had done}.
\v 25 And even though Elnathan, Delaiah, and Gemariah urged the king not to burn the scroll, he refused to pay attention to them.
\v 26 Then the king commanded his son Jerahmeel, Seraiah son of Azriel, and Shelemiah son of Abdeel to arrest Baruch the scribe and {me,} Jeremiah the prophet. But {they were unable to do that because} Yahweh had hidden us.
\p
\v 27 After King Jehoiakim burned the scroll that held the messages that Baruch had written down while {I,} Jeremiah spoke them aloud, Yahweh spoke this message to {me,} Jeremiah. Yahweh said,
\v 28 “Get another scroll and write on it all the messages that were on the first scroll, the one that Jehoiakim the king of Judah burned.
\v 29 Regarding Jehoiakim the king of Judah you must say, ‘Yahweh says this: “you burned this scroll and you rebuked Jeremiah for writing on it that the king of Babylon would certainly come and destroy this land and put an end to every person and animal in it.
\v 30 So now {I,}Yahweh say this about you Jehoiakim king of Judah: ‘None of your descendants will rule this kingdom. {After you die,} people will throw your corpse out on the ground, where it will lie in the heat during the day and in the frost during the night.
\v 31 {I,} Yahweh will punish you and your descendants and your officials because of the wicked things that you have all done. I will bring on you, and on everyone who lives in Jerusalem, and on all the people of Judah, every disaster that I warned you about, because none of you would pay attention to me.’”
\p
\v 32 So {I,} Jeremiah took another scroll and gave it to Baruch son of Neriah, the scribe. While I spoke aloud, Baruch wrote on it all the messages of the scroll that Jehoiakim the king of Judah had burned in the fire. I, Jeremiah also dictated many other similar messages, and Baruch added them to the scroll.
\c 37
\s1 King Zedekiah asks Jeremiah to pray
\p
\v 1 Zedekiah son of Josiah became the king of Judah, ruling in place of Coniah son of Jehoiakim. Nebuchadnezzar, the king of Babylon, was the one who made Zedekiah king over the land of Judah.
\v 2 But King Zedekiah, his palace officials, and the other people in the land refused to pay attention to the messages that Yahweh had given them through {me,} Jeremiah the prophet.
\v 3 King Zedekiah sent Jehucal son of Shelemiah and the priest Zephaniah son of Maaseiah to {me,} Jeremiah the prophet with this request: “Please pray to Yahweh our God for us.”
\v 4 At that time {I,} Jeremiah was still moving about freely among the people, because no one had yet put me in prison.
\v 5 At that time the army of Pharaoh{, the king of Egypt} had marched out from Egypt. When the army of Babylonia who were surrounding Jerusalem, heard the report {about that army}, they stopped surrounding Jerusalem and marched away.
\p
\v 6 Then Yahweh gave this message to {me,} Jeremiah, the prophet. He said,
\v 7 “{I,} Yahweh, the God of Israel say this: The king of Judah who sent {messengers} to you to ask me {what is going to happen}. Tell the king that {even though} the army of the king of Egypt, came to help him, they are about to return to their own land.
\v 8 Then the Babylonian army will come back, attack this city, capture it, and burn it down.
\v 9 I {also} say this {to you Israelites}: You should not deceive yourselves by thinking, ‘The Babylonian army has certainly left us for good.’ For they will not stay away.
\v 10 Because even if you could defeat the entire Babylonian army that is fighting against you, so that only wounded men remained lying in their tents, those wounded men would get up and burn this city down.”
\s1 Officials put Jeremiah in prison
\p
\v 11 When the Babylonian army withdrew from Jerusalem because the army of Pharaoh was approaching,
\v 12 then, {I,} Jeremiah left Jerusalem to go to the territory of Benjamin, in order to claim there the share of family property that belonged to me among my people.
\v 13 However, when I reached the Gate of Benjamin, the officer in charge there, a man named Irijah son of Shelemiah son of Hananiah, arrested {me,} Jeremiah the prophet and accused me, saying, “You are deserting {us and going} to the soldiers of the Babylonian army!”
\v 14 {I,} Jeremiah replied, “That is a lie! I am not deserting to the soldiers of the Babylonian army.” However, Irijah refused to pay attention to me. He arrested me and took me to the officials.
\v 15 The officials were furious with {me,} Jeremiah. They {commanded the guards to} beat me and {then to} put me in the house of Jonathan the royal secretary, which they had turned into a prison.
\v 16 They put {me,} Jeremiah into a dungeon cell, and I stayed there for a long time.
\p
\v 17 Then King Zedekiah sent {a servant} who brought me to the palace. There the king questioned me privately, “Is there any message from Yahweh?” {I,} Jeremiah answered, “Yes, there {is a message}. Yahweh will hand you over to the king of Babylon.”
\v 18 Then Jeremiah said to King Zedekiah, “I have not done anything wrong to you, or to your officials, or to these people. You had no reason to put me in prison.
\v 19 Consider also your prophets, the ones who prophesied to you that the king of Babylon would not attack you or this land. Consider that their messages were not fulfilled.
\v 20 So now, your majesty the king, please pay attention to me. Please grant the request that I am making to you: do not send me back to the house of Jonathan the royal secretary, so that I will not die there.”
\v 21 So King Zedekiah gave orders that his men place me in the courtyard {of the palace} where the guards watched me. {He} also {ordered that} every day they bring me a loaf of bread from the street where the bakers worked, until there was no more bread anywhere in the city {because the Babylonian army besieged the city again.} So {I,} Jeremiah stayed in the courtyard of the guard.
\c 38
\s1 Officials throw Jeremiah into a cistern
\p
\v 1 {Four officials,} Shephatiah son of Mattan, Gedaliah son of Pashhur, Jucal son of Shelemiah, and Pashhur son of Malkijah heard the messages that {I,} Jeremiah was delivering to all the people. I had been telling them that
\v 2 Yahweh said that everyone who stayed in this city would die in war or from famine or from disease, but that everyone who left the city in order to surrender to the Babylonian army would stay alive. He said that they will escape with nothing, but the Babylonian soldiers will spare their lives.
\v 3 Yahweh {also} said that he would certainly hand this city over to the army of the king of Babylon, and that army would capture it.
\v 4 So those officials said to the king, “Please command your men to kill this man! Because by saying such things, he is making the soldiers who are still in this city lose their courage, and he is discouraging all the other people as well. This man does not want to help these people; he only wants to harm them.”
\v 5 King Zedekiah answered, “All right, you may do with him whatever you want to; I cannot do anything to stop you.”
\v 6 So those officials took {me,} Jeremiah and threw me into the cistern that belonged to Malkijah, a son of the king. That cistern was in the courtyard {of the palace} where the guards watched me, and they lowered me into it with ropes. There was no water in the cistern, only mud, so I sank down into the mud.
\p
\v 7 But Ebed Melech, a man from the land of Cush, served as an official in the king’s palace. He heard that the officials had put {me,} Jeremiah into the cistern. At that time the king was sitting and deciding people’s cases at the Benjamin Gate.
\v 8 So Ebed Melech left the palace and spoke to the king. He said,
\v 9 “Your majesty the king, these men have acted very wickedly in everything that they have done to Jeremiah the prophet. They threw him into a cistern, and he will starve to death down there, because there is no more food in the city.”
\v 10 So the king commanded Ebed Melech, who was from the land of Cush, “Take thirty men from here with you, and lift Jeremiah the prophet out of the cistern before he dies.”
\v 11 So Ebed Melech took the men with him and went into the palace, to a room underneath the storeroom. He got some worn-out rags and worn-out clothes from there, and he lowered them to Jeremiah in the cistern with ropes.
\v 12 Then Ebed Melech, who was from the land of Cush, called down to {me,} Jeremiah, “Please put these worn-out rags and clothes under your armpits, to pad the ropes.” And I did that.
\v 13 Then the men pulled {me,} Jeremiah up out of the cistern with the ropes. After that, I stayed in the courtyard {of the palace} where the guards watched me.
\s1 Zedekiah questions Jeremiah again
\p
\v 14 Later King Zedekiah sent men who brought {me,} Jeremiah the prophet, to him at the third entrance to the temple of Yahweh. The king said to me, “I want to ask you about something. Do not hide anything from me.”
\v 15 Jeremiah answered Zedekiah, “If I tell you, you will certainly kill me. And if I give you advice, you will not pay attention to me.”
\v 16 So King Zedekiah secretly made a solemn promise to {me,} Jeremiah. He said, “As surely as Yahweh lives, the one who gave us the lives that we have, I will not kill you, and I will not hand you over to these men who are trying to kill you.”
\p
\v 17 Then {I,} Jeremiah, said to Zedekiah, “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the people of Israel worship, says this {to you}: ‘If you go out {and surrender} to the officials of the king of Babylon, you will stay alive, and no one will burn this city down, and you and your family will remain alive.
\v 18 However, if you do not go out {and surrender} to the officials of the king of Babylon, then I will hand this city over to the Babylonian army, and they will burn it down, and you will certainly not escape from them.’”
\v 19 King Zedekiah said to {me,} Jeremiah, “I am afraid of the Judeans who have deserted to the soldiers of Babylonia. The Babylonian soldiers may hand me over to those people of Judah, and those Judeans will abuse me.”
\v 20 Jeremiah answered, “The Babylonian soldiers will not hand you over to them. Please obey what Yahweh is telling you through me. Then things will go well for you, and you will stay alive.
\v 21 However, if you refuse to go out {and surrender}, this is what Yahweh has revealed to me.
\v 22 Know for certain that men will bring out all the women who are still in the palace of the king of Judah, {and they will hand over} those women to the officials of the king of Babylon. Then those women will say to you:
\q1 ‘Your friends, the men you trusted, misled you and got the better of you.
\q2 Now you are completely helpless {it is as though} you are stuck in mud, and those men have abandoned you.’
\m
\v 23 Men will bring out all your wives and your sons to the Babylonian army, and you yourself will not escape from them. The king of Babylon will capture you, and the Babylonian soldiers will burn down this city.”
\p
\v 24 Then Zedekiah said to {me,} Jeremiah, “Do not let anyone at all know about what you told me; if you tell anyone, my officials may kill you.
\v 25 If my officials find out that I have talked with you, they may come to you and say to you, ‘Please tell us what you and the king were talking about. If you tell us, we will not kill you.’
\v 26 If that happens, then say to them, ‘I was pleading with the king not to send me back to the house of Jonathan, because I would die there.’”
\v 27 Then all the officials came to {me,} Jeremiah and questioned me. I told them exactly what the king had commanded me to say. So they stopped questioning me, because no one had overheard what the king and I had said. to each other.
\v 28 So {I,} Jeremiah stayed in the courtyard {of the palace} where the guards watched me. I stayed there until the day when the Babylonians captured Jerusalem and was there when the Babylonians captured it.
\c 39
\s1 The Babylonians capture Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 During the tenth month of the ninth year that Zedekiah was king of Judah, Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon led his whole army to Jerusalem. They surrounded the city and attacked it.
\v 2 {About a year and a half later,} on the ninth day of the fourth month of the eleventh year that Zedekiah was king, the Babylonian soldiers broke through the wall of the city.
\v 3 Then all the officials of the king of Babylon came into the city and sat down in the Middle Gate. They were Nergal Sarezer, Samgar Nebu, Sarsekim the chief officer, Nergal Sarezer the chief adviser, and all the other officials of the king of Babylon.
\p
\v 4 When Zedekiah the king of Judah and all his soldiers saw these officials, they ran away. During the night they left the city by way of the king’s garden, going out through the gate between the two walls. Then Zedekiah headed toward the Jordan River Valley.
\v 5 But the Babylonian army chased after them and caught up with Zedekiah in the plains near the city of Jericho. They captured him and brought him to Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, who was at the city of Riblah in the land of Hamath. There Nebuchadnezzar judged Zedekiah for rebelling against him and decided how to punish him.
\v 6 At Riblah the king of Babylon forced Zedekiah to watch as his soldiers killed Zedekiah’s sons. His soldiers also killed all the nobles of Judah.
\v 7 Then the king ordered his men to gouge out Zedekiah’s eyes and to put bronze chains on him, so that they could take him to the city of Babylon.
\p
\v 8 The Chaldean soldiers also burned down the king’s palace and the houses of the people of Jerusalem, and they tore down the walls of Jerusalem.
\v 9 Then Nebuzaradan, the commander of the {king’s} bodyguards, took away to Babylon as captives the people who still remained in the city, along with those who had deserted to the king of Babylon and {most of} the other people who remained.
\v 10 But Nebuzaradan, the commander of {the king of Babylon’s} bodyguards, allowed some of the very poor people, those who owned nothing, to stay in the land of Judah, and on that day he gave to them vineyards and fields {to take care of}.
\s1 The Babylonians set Jeremiah free
\p
\v 11 Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon had {previously} given orders to Nebuzaradan, the commander of his bodyguards, regarding {me,} Jeremiah. He said,
\v 12 “Take Jeremiah and look after him. Do not harm him in any way. Instead, do for him whatever he asks you to do.”
\v 13 So Nebuzaradan the commander of the bodyguards, Nebushazban the chief officer, Nergal Sharezer the king’s adviser, and all the other chief officers of the king of Babylon sent some of their men to get Jeremiah.
\v 14 Those men sent and brought {me,} Jeremiah out of the courtyard {of the palace} where the guards watched me and handed me over to Gedaliah, who was the son of Ahikam and the grandson of Shaphan. Gedaliah was to take {me,} Jeremiah back to my own home. So I lived among my own people.
\p
\v 15 {Earlier,} while the guards were still keeping {me,} Jeremiah in the courtyard {of the palace}, Yahweh had given me this message. He said,
\v 16 “Go to Ebed Melech, the man from the land of Cush, and tell him that Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelites worship, says this: ‘Pay attention! I am about to do to this city everything that I warned I would do. I will cause the people of this city to experience disasters; I will not enable this people to prosper. At that time you will see enemy soldiers destroy Jerusalem.
\v 17 But at that time I will rescue you. I will not allow those people whom you are afraid of to conquer you.’ That is what Yahweh declares.
\v 18 ‘I will certainly rescue you. You will not die in war. You will escape with nothing except your own life, but you will remain alive, because you have trusted in me.’” That is what Yahweh declares.
\c 40
\s1 Nebuzaradan sets Jeremiah free
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave {me,} Jeremiah a message after Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guards, had released me when I was in the town of Ramah {north of Jerusalem}where I and many other people from Jerusalem and {other places in} Judah had been sent after {the Babylonian soldiers} had fastened chains around our wrists. {They had brought us to Ramah because they planned} to take us to Babylon.
\v 2 The commander of the bodyguards took Jeremiah aside and said to him, “Yahweh your God said that he would bring this disaster on the people who live here in Jerusalem.
\v 3 Now Yahweh has caused it to happen; he has done exactly what he said he would do. All this has happened to you people of Judah because you sinned against Yahweh and did not obey what he commanded you.
\v 4 But today I have taken off the chains that were on your wrists, and I am setting you free. If you want to come with me to Babylon, then come, and I will take good care of you. But if you do not want to come with me to Babylon, then do not come. Look, the whole land is open to you. Go anywhere you think it is good and right to go.”
\v 5 But before Jeremiah turned to leave, Nebuzaradan said to him, “Go back to Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam and the grandson of Shaphan, the man whom the king of Babylon has appointed to govern the cities of Judah. Live with him among the people. Or go anywhere else that you think it is right to go.” Then the commander of the bodyguards gave Jeremiah an allowance of food and a gift, and he let him go.
\v 6 So Jeremiah went to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam at the town of Mizpah, and he lived there with him among the people whom the Babylonians had allowed to remain in the land.
\s1 The people who remained gather to Gedaliah
\p
\v 7 All the commanders of the groups of Judean soldiers who were still out in the open country, along with their men, heard that the king of Babylon had appointed Gedaliah the son of Ahikam to govern the land. They also heard that the king had put in Gedaliah’s care the men, women, and children who were the poorest people in the land, those whom he had not taken away to Babylon as captives.
\v 8 So those commanders went {to talk} to Gedaliah at Mizpah. {Those who went included} Ishmael the son of Nethaniah; Johanan and Jonathan the sons of Kareah; Seraiah the son of Tanhumeth; the sons of Ephai from the town of Netophah; and Jezaniah, whose father was from the region of Maacah. Their men went with them.
\v 9 Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam and the grandson of Shaphan, made a solemn promise to those commanders and their men. He said, “Do not be afraid to serve the Chaldeans. Settle down in this land and serve the king of Babylon. If you do that, things will go well for you.
\v 10 As for me, I will stay here at Mizpah in order to represent you before the {officials from} Babylonia whenever they come to {talk with} us. But you should {return to your towns and} harvest grapes to make wine, gather the fruit {that ripens in} the hot season, and pick olives {to make oil}. Store all of it in your jars, and live in the towns that you have taken for yourselves.”
\v 11 Then all the Jews who were living in Moab, among the Ammonites, in Edom, and in all the other countries also heard that the king of Babylon had allowed some of the people to remain in Judah, and that he had appointed Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam and the grandson of Shaphan, to govern them.
\v 12 So all those Judeans came back from all the places where the Babylonians had scattered them. They came to the land of Judah, to Gedaliah at Mizpah. There they harvested a very large amount of grapes and summer fruit.
\s1 Johanan warns Gedaliah about a plot
\p
\v 13 {Some time later} Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops who were still out in the open country came to Gedaliah at Mizpah.
\v 14 They said to him, “Are you aware that Baalis, the king of the Ammonites, has sent Ishmael the son of Nethaniah to kill you?” But Gedaliah the son of Ahikam did not believe them.
\p
\v 15 Then Johanan the son of Kareah spoke privately to Gedaliah at Mizpah. He said, “Please let me go and kill Ishmael the son of Nethaniah. No one will find out who did it. You must not let him kill you! If he kills you, all the Judeans who have gathered around you will scatter, and the rest of the people of Judah will die.
\p
\v 16 But Gedaliah the son of Ahikam said to Johanan the son of Kareah, “Do not do that because you are lying about Ishmael.”
\c 41
\s1 Ishmael murders Gedaliah
\p
\v 1 In the seventh month {of that year}, Ishmael the son of Nethaniah and grandson of Elishama came to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam at Mizpah. Ishmael belonged to the royal family and had been one of the king’s chief officers. Ten men came with him. They all ate a meal together at Mizpah.
\v 2 Ishmael the son of Nethaniah and the ten men who were with him got up and attacked Gedaliah the son of Ahikam and grandson of Shaphan with their swords, and Ishmael killed him. Gedaliah was the man whom the king of Babylon had appointed to govern the land.
\v 3 Ishmael also killed all the Judeans who were with Gedaliah in Mizpah, along with the Babylonian soldiers who were there.
\v 4 On the day after Ishmael killed Gedaliah, no one had yet found out what he had done.
\v 5 Eighty men came from the cities of Shechem, Shiloh, and Samaria. They had shaved off their beards, torn their clothes, and cut themselves {to show that they were mourning}. They were carrying grain offerings and incense to bring to the temple of Yahweh {in Jerusalem}.
\v 6 Ishmael the son of Nethaniah went out from Mizpah to meet them, and he wept continually as he walked along. When he met them, he said to them, “Come with me to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam.”
\p
\v 7 When they had come into the middle of the city, Ishmael the son of Nethaniah and his men killed them and threw their bodies into a cistern.
\v 8 However, ten of those men said to Ishmael, “Do not kill us because we have supplies of wheat, barley, olive oil, and honey that we have hidden in a field.” So Ishmael stopped and did not kill them along with their fellow Judeans.
\v 9 The cistern which Ishmael’s {men} threw all the bodies of the men whom they had killed along with Gedaliah was the same one that King Asa’s {men} had dug so that {they would have water in the city} if the army of King Baasha of Israel would surround the city. The men of Ishmael the son of Nethaniah filled that cistern with the bodies of the people they had killed.
\v 10 Then Ishmael {and his men} captured all the rest of the people who were at Mizpah, including the king’s daughters and everyone else who still remained there. Nebuzaradan the commander of the king’s bodyguards had put all those people in the care of Gedaliah the son of Ahikam. Ishmael the son of Nethaniah took them as his captives and started off to cross over to the Ammonites.
\s1 Johanan rescues the captives
\p
\v 11 Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops who were with him heard about all the evil things that Ishmael the son of Nethaniah had done.
\v 12 So they took all their men and went to fight against Ishmael the son of Nethaniah. They found him at the large pool in Gibeon.
\v 13 When all the people whom Ishmael had captured saw Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops who were with him, they were very glad.
\v 14 All the people whom Ishmael had captured at Mizpah turned around and went over to Johanan the son of Kareah.
\v 15 However, Ishmael the son of Nethaniah escaped from Johanan with eight of his men, and he went to the Ammonites.
\p
\v 16 Then Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops who were with him gathered all the rest of the people whom they had rescued from Ishmael the son of Nethaniah. Ishmael had taken those people away from Mizpah after he killed Gedaliah the son of Ahikam. Among them were soldiers, women, children, and royal officials. Johanan brought them all back from Gibeon.
\v 17 They set out and stayed at Geruth Kimham, which is near Bethlehem, because they intended to go on into Egypt.
\v 18 They wanted to get away from the Chaldeans, because they were afraid of them. They were afraid {of what the Babylonian soldier would do} because Ishmael the son of Nethaniah had killed Gedaliah the son of Ahikam, the man whom the king of Babylon had appointed to govern the land.
\c 42
\s1 Jeremiah warns the people not to go to Egypt
\p
\v 1 Then all the commanders of the troops, along with Johanan the son of Kareah and Jezaniah the son of Hoshaiah, and all the rest of the people, from the least important to the most important, came forward.
\v 2 They said to {me,} Jeremiah the prophet, “Please do this thing we are requesting of you, and pray to Yahweh your God for us, for all of us who remain. Pray for us because formerly we were very many people, but as you can see, only a few of us have survived.
\v 3 {Pray} so that Yahweh your God will tell us where we should go and what we should do.”
\v 4 {I,} Jeremiah the prophet said to them, “I have heard you. I will indeed pray to Yahweh your God, just as you have asked me to do. Whatever Yahweh answers you, I will tell you; I will tell you everything he says.”
\v 5 Then they said to Jeremiah, “May Yahweh himself be a truthful and reliable witness against us if we do not carefully do everything that Yahweh your God sends you to tell us.
\v 6 Whether his answer pleases us or not, we will obey Yahweh our God, the one to whom we are sending you. If we obey Yahweh our God, then things will go well for us.”
\p
\v 7 Ten days later, Yahweh gave his message to {me,} Jeremiah.
\v 8 So {I,} Jeremiah summoned Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops who were with him, and all the rest of the people, from the least important to the most important.
\v 9 I said to them, “You sent me to Yahweh, the God whom we Israelites worship, to present your request to him, and this is what Yahweh says:
\v 10 ‘I am sad about the disaster that I caused you to suffer so you {should} change your mind and stay in this land, and if you do that then I will make you strong as a nation and I will not destroy you; I will settle you here permanently and I will not drive you out.
\v 11 Do not be afraid of the king of Babylon, the man whom you now fear. Do not be afraid of him’—this is what Yahweh declares—‘because I will be present with you in order to save you and in order to rescue you from him.
\v 12 I will be merciful to you by causing the king of Babylon to act mercifully toward you, so that he will let you return to your own land.’
\p
\v 13 However, you may refuse to obey Yahweh your God and say that you will not stay in this land.
\v 14 You may instead say that you will go to the land of Egypt, where you will not experience any war or hear the sound of a horn {signaling our soldiers to get ready for battle} or be hungry for food, and that you will live there.
\v 15 If you say that, then listen to what Yahweh tells you, you people of Judah who remain. Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom we Israelites worship, says this: ‘If you insist on going to Egypt, and you go and live there as sojourners,
\v 16 then you will experience there in the land of Egypt the very war that you are afraid of, and you will also suffer there the very famine that you worry about. You will certainly not avoid it by going to Egypt, and you will die there.
\v 17 Every person who has decided to go to Egypt and live there as sojourners will die in war, or from famine, or from disease. Not one of them will survive or escape the disaster that I am about to bring on them.’
\p
\v 18 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom we Israelites worship, says this: ‘I became extremely angry with the people who lived in Jerusalem, and I punished them severely. In the same way, I will become very angry with you and punish you severely when you go to Egypt. You will be an object of cursing and people will be horrified when they see what happens to you and you will be the thing by which people pronounce curses and you will be the object of people’s insults, and you will never see this place again.’
\p
\v 19 You people of Judah who remain, Yahweh has told you not to go to Egypt. You should clearly understand that I am warning you today.
\v 20 {I am warning you} that you were insincere when you requested me to pray to Yahweh our God for you and to tell you everything that Yahweh our God would say, and you said that you would do it.
\v 21 Today I have told you what he said, but you have not obeyed Yahweh your God; you have not obeyed anything that he sent me to tell you.
\v 22 So now, you should clearly understand that you will die in war or from famine or from disease in the place where you want to go and live as sojourners.”
\c 43
\s1 The people of Judah refuse to obey Yahweh and go to Egypt
\p
\v 1 {I,} Jeremiah told all the people everything that Yahweh their God had sent me to tell them. When I finished
\p
\v 2 Azariah the son of Hoshaiah and Johanan the son of Kareah and all the arrogant men said to {me} Jeremiah, “You are lying! Yahweh our God did not send you to tell us that we must not go to Egypt and live there.
\v 3 Rather {we think that it is} Baruch the son of Neriah, who is urging you to speak against us so that the Babylonian soldiers will seize us in order to kill us or take us away to Babylon as captives.”
\v 4 So Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops and all the people refused to obey what Yahweh commanded them; they would not stay in the land of Judah.
\v 5 Instead, Johanan the son of Kareah and all the commanders of the troops took away all the people of Judah who still remained. These were the people who had come back to live in the land of Judah from all the nations where their enemies had driven them.
\v 6 {They took} the men, the women, and the children, and the king’s daughters and every person whom Nebuzaradan the commander of the king’s bodyguards had left with Gedaliah the son of Ahikam and the grandson of Shaphan. They took {me,} Jeremiah the prophet and Baruch the son of Neriah as well.
\v 7 They went into the land of Egypt, because they refused to obey what Yahweh told them. They traveled as far as the city of Tahpanhes.
\s1 Yahweh warns that Nebuchadnezzar will conquer Egypt
\p
\v 8 There in Tahpanhes, Yahweh spoke a message to Jeremiah. He said,
\v 9 “Pick up some large stones and bury them in the clay under the bricks of the pavement at the entrance to the palace of Pharaoh in Tahpanhes. Do this while the men from Judah are watching you.
\v 10 Then tell those men that this is what I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, say to them: ‘I am about to send for Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, the man who does my work, and I will bring him here. I will place his throne over these stones that I have told Jeremiah to bury here, and he will rule as king from that place. He will spread out his royal tent over these stones {to show that he now rules over Egypt}.
\v 11 He will come and attack the people of the land of Egypt. He will kill the people whom I have decided must die, his soldiers will capture the people whom I have decided must become captives, and they will kill with their swords the people whom I have decided must die in war.
\v 12 Nebuchadnezzar’s soldiers will burn down the temples where the Egyptians worship their idols. {But before} they burn the temples, they will take away their idols as plunder. He will take complete control of the land of Egypt without any difficulty just as a shepherd easily controls his garment when he wraps it around himself, and then Nebuchadnezzar will leave that land safely, because no one there will harm him.
\v 13 Nebuchadnezzar’s soldiers will tear down the sacred stone pillars at Beth Shemesh, the place in the land of Egypt where the people worship the sun, and he will burn down the temples where the Egyptians worship their gods.’”
\c 44
\s1 Yahweh warns the Judeans in Egypt about their idol worship
\p
\v 1 This is the message that Yahweh gave to {me,} Jeremiah for all the Jewish people who were living in the land of Egypt, that is, those who were living in the cities of Migdol, Tahpanhes, and Memphis, and in the region of Pathros {in southern Egypt}. Yahweh said this:
\p
\v 2 “This is what {I,} Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of you Israelites say: ‘You yourselves saw all the terrible things that I caused {the people in} Jerusalem and all the other cities of Judah to experience. Look at them today: they lie in ruins, and hardly anyone lives in them.
\v 3 {That happened} because of the evil things that the people there did. They made me very angry by going and burning incense and worshiping other gods that they had {previously} not known about. Neither they nor you nor your ancestors had {previously} known those gods.
\v 4 Again and again I sent all my servants the prophets to you, and I told them to plead with you not to do this detestable thing that I hate.
\v 5 However, your ancestors did not pay any attention {to my prophets}. They refused to stop their wicked behavior, and they would not stop burning incense to other gods.
\v 6 So I showed them how extremely angry I was, and I punished {the people in} the cities of Judah and on the streets of Jerusalem. Those places became an empty ruin, and they are still like that today.’
\p
\v 7 So now Yahweh, the god who commands the heavenly armies, the God of you Israelites, says this: ‘Do you not realize that {because of} what you are doing you are causing yourselves to experience these terrible disasters with the result that {soon} you will cause there to be no more men, women, children or infants left among you {who have come here to Egypt from Judah}?
\v 8 You are making me angry by burning incense to other gods you have made here in the land of Egypt, where you have come to live. The result {if you continue to do this} will be that you destroy yourselves, and that people in every nation on the earth will use your name when they curse someone and will despise you.
\v 9 You have forgotten {how I punished} your ancestors for the wicked things they did, and {how I punished} the kings of Judah and queens of Judah for the wicked things they did, and you and your wives for the wicked things that you did in the land of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem.
\v 10 To this day you have not humbled yourselves or revered me. You have not obeyed all my laws that I gave to you and to your ancestors.’
\s1 Yahweh promises to destroy the Judeans who fled to Egypt
\p
\v 11 So Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom you Israelites worship, says this: ‘I have firmly decided to bring disaster on you and to destroy all the people of Judah.
\v 12 I will take away the people of Judah who survived, the ones who were determined to go to the land of Egypt and live there. All of them will perish there in the land of Egypt, from the least important of them to the most important. They will die in war or from famine. People will make them an object of cursing and people will be horrified when they see what happens to them and they will be the thing by which people pronounce curses and they will be the object of people’s insults.
\v 13 I will punish the people who are living in the land of Egypt just as I punished the people of Jerusalem: by war, by famine, and by disease.
\v 14 None of the people of Judah who survived and came to live in the land of Egypt will get away or stay alive to return to the land of Judah, even though they long to go back and live there. Only a very few of them will escape and return.’”
\s1 The people refuse to stop worshiping the Queen of Heaven
\p
\v 15 Then all the men who knew that their wives were burning incense to other gods, and all the women who were standing there in a large crowd, and all the people who were living in the region of Pathros in the land of Egypt, answered {me,} Jeremiah. They said,
\v 16 “You are saying that Yahweh gave messages to you to tell us on his behalf, {but} we will not pay any attention to your messages.
\v 17 We will certainly do everything that we said we would do. We will burn incense to the goddess whom we call the Queen of Heaven, and we will pour out wine offerings to her, just as we and our ancestors and our kings and our officials did in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem. At that time we had plenty of food, we prospered, and we did not experience difficult things.
\v 18 However, from the time that we stopped burning incense to the Queen of Heaven and pouring out wine offerings to her, we have lacked nearly everything, and many of our people have died in war and from famine.”
\p
\v 19 Then {the women said,} “When we burned incense to the Queen of Heaven and poured out wine offerings to her, our husbands certainly knew that we were making cakes that resembled her idol to offer her and that we were pouring out wine offerings to her, and they agreed to it.”
\s1 Jeremiah answers the people
\p
\v 20 Then {I,} Jeremiah spoke to all the people, to the men and to the women and to everyone who had answered him in that way. I said,
\v 21 “Yahweh certainly did not forget the incense that you burned in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem, both you and your ancestors and your kings and your officials and all the people of the land. Yahweh remembered what you did and kept thinking about it.
\v 22 Finally Yahweh could no longer endure your evil deeds and the detestable things that you were doing. So he caused your land to become a place of ruins and a horrifying place, a land that people mention when they pronounce curses, a land where no one lives, just as it still is today.
\v 23 Because you burned incense {to idols} and sinned against Yahweh, and did not obey what Yahweh said to you and did not live according to all his commands, therefore you have experienced {all} these disasters, as is clearly evident today.”
\p
\v 24 Then {I,} Jeremiah said to all the people, including all the women, “All you people of Judah who are in the land of Egypt, listen to this message from Yahweh.
\v 25 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God of Israel, says this {to you men of Judah}: ‘You and your wives have said that you would certainly {continue} to do what you vowed by burning incense and pouring out wine to {the goddess whom you call} the Queen of Heaven and you have done what you said. {Since you insist, go ahead and} keep your vows! Do exactly what you vowed to do!’
\p
\v 26 So now, all you people of Judah who are living in the land of Egypt, pay attention to this message from Yahweh. He says, ‘I have solemnly sworn by my own great name that never again will anyone from Judah anywhere in the land of Egypt use my name to make a promise by saying, “{I will do it as surely as} Lord Yahweh lives.”
\v 27 I am watching over these people, intending to bring disaster on them and not good things. Nearly all the people of Judah who are in the land of Egypt will die in war or from famine, until none of them are left.
\v 28 Only a few people will survive the war and return from the land of Egypt to the land of Judah. Then all the people of Judah who survived and came to live in the land of Egypt will know whose words came true, mine or theirs.
\v 29 I will give you a sign’—this is what Yahweh declares—‘that I will indeed punish you here in this place, so that you may know that my messages about bringing disaster on you will certainly come true.’
\p
\v 30 Yahweh says this: ‘I am about to allow the men who want to kill Pharoah Hophra, the king of Egypt, to capture him just as I allowed Nebuchadnezzar, the king of Babylon, who wanted to kill Zedekiah, the king of Judah, {to have his soldiers} capture him.’”
\c 45
\s1 Yahweh gives a message to Baruch
\p
\v 1 During the fourth year that Jehoiakim son of Josiah was the king of Judah, Baruch son of Neriah was writing on a scroll the messages that {I,} Jeremiah the prophet was speaking aloud to him. At that time I gave Baruch this message. I said,
\p
\v 2 “Baruch, this is what Yahweh, the God of Israel, says to you:
\v 3 You said, ‘Terrible things {are happening} to me. Yahweh has caused me to experience things that have caused me to be sorrowful in addition to the things he caused me to experience that are painful. I am exhausted from my groaning and I am unable to rest.’
\p
\v 4 {Yahweh told me} I must tell you this {in response}: ‘I Yahweh say this: {Like} a builder who builds a house and then tears it down, I myself established this nation, {but now} I am destroying it and {like} a farmer who plants a crop and then uproots it I myself settled this people in their land, but now I am removing them from it. I will do this throughout all the earth.
\v 5 As for you, I am surprised that you are seeking great things for yourself. Do not seek them, because I am about to bring a terrible disaster on everyone. That is what I, Yahweh, declare. However, wherever you go, I will let you escape with nothing except your own life, but you will remain alive there.’”
\c 46
\s1 Yahweh gives messages about the nations
\p
\v 1 This is the message that Yahweh gave to {me,} Jeremiah the prophet about the {other} nations.
\s1 Yahweh gives a message about Egypt
\p
\v 2 {Yahweh gave this message} about Egypt, regarding the army of Pharaoh Neco the king of Egypt. That army was at the city of Carchemish beside the Euphrates River when {the army of} Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon defeated it. {This happened} during the fourth year that Jehoiakim son of Josiah was the king of Judah.
\q1 \v 3 {Yahweh said, “You soldiers of Egypt,} get your small shields and your large shields ready,
\q2 and march out to fight the battle!
\q1 \v 4 Harness the horses;
\q2 you horsemen, mount them and take your places.
\q1 Put on your helmets, sharpen your spears,
\q2 and put on your armor!
\q1 \v 5 This is what I see:
\q2 the soldiers of Egypt are panicking, and they are retreating.
\q1 Their enemies are beating down their warriors.
\q2 They run away as fast as they can,
\q1 and they never turn back to face their enemies,
\q2 because enemies surround them on every side and they greatly fear them.”
\q1 This is what Yahweh declares.
\q1 \v 6 “The fastest runners will not get away,
\q2 and the warriors will not escape.
\q1 In the north, beside the Euphrates River,
\q2 they will stumble, and their enemies will kill them.
\q1 \v 7 {Look at} who this is that is rising up {and covering the land}
\q2 like the Nile River, like its streams when their waters surge {at flood stage}!
\q1 \v 8 {It is the army of} Egypt that is rising up {and covering the land}
\q2 like the water of the Nile River when it floods and covers the land.
\q1 The Egyptians boast that they will advance and cover the whole earth,
\q2 and that they will destroy cities and the people who live in them.
\q1 \v 9 You {riders of} horses, charge {into the battle}!
\q2 You chariot {drivers}, drive furiously!
\q1 All you warriors from Cush and Put,
\q2 who carry your shields,
\q1 you warriors from Lydia
\q2 who shoot arrows, come!
\q1 \v 10 {You need to know that,} that is the time when {I,} Lord Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies;
\q2 will pay my enemies back for what they have done.
\q1 {The soldiers from Babylon} will use their swords to kill so many of my enemies
\q2 that they will finally have killed all that they wanted to kill.
\q1 {The Egyptian soldiers who will die} in the north, beside the Euphrates River,
\q2 {will be like} a sacrifice to {me}, Lord Yahweh the Commander of the heavenly armies.
\q1 \v 11 You people of Egypt, go to the region of Gilead to get help!
\q2 In vain I have done many things to try to help you,
\q1 {and because you stubbornly refuse to obey me} nothing can help you {now}.
\q1 \v 12 {People in} the {other} nations will hear {how I Yahweh have caused} enemy soldiers to disgrace you.
\q2 {People everywhere on} earth have heard you crying out.
\q1 {Your} warriors will stumble over each other,
\q2 and they will all fall down together.”
\p
\v 13 This is the message that Yahweh spoke to {me,} Jeremiah the prophet about how Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon would come and attack the land of Egypt.
\q1 \v 14 {Yahweh said,} “Announce this in Egypt, and proclaim it in the cities of Migdol, Memphis, and Tahpanhes.
\q2 Tell the people there, ‘Take your positions and get ready {to fight},
\q2 because your enemies will kill everyone around you in battle.’
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh will certainly destroy your soldiers.
\q2 They will not be able to resist their enemies,
\q1 because I Yahweh will defeat them.
\q1 \v 16 I, Yahweh will make many of your soldiers fall;
\q2 indeed, they will collide with one another as they flee.
\q1 They will say {to each other}, ‘Get up! Let us go back to our own people,
\q2 back to the land where we were born,
\q1 so that we can escape from the enemies who are killing us!’
\q1 \v 17 There {in Egypt} people will say,
\q2 ‘Pharaoh the king of Egypt boasts loudly;
\q2 {but when} he had an opportunity {to defeat our enemies}, our army failed.’
\q1 \v 18 The King, whose name is Yahweh the commander of the heavenly armies, declares this:
\q2 “As {surely as} I live,
\q1 an enemy king’s army will certainly come {to fight against the army of Egypt}.
\q2 That king’s army will be {extremely powerful as though it were as tall} as Mount Tabor which towers over mountains {around it},
\q2 or {as high} as Mount Carmel which towers over the Mediterranean Sea.
\q1 \v 19 You people who live in Egypt, pack your possessions that you can carry,
\q2 because your enemies will take you away as captives.
\q1 They will ruin the city of Memphis completely;
\q2 they will burn it, and no one will live there anymore.
\q1 \v 20 Egypt is {like} a very beautiful young cow,
\q2 but the army of a powerful king} from the northeast will surely come to attack it,
\q2 {like} a horsefly {bites a cow}.
\q1 \v 21 The foreign soldiers {from other countries} whom the Egyptians hired will become like calves that farmers fatten in a stall.
\q2 They also will turn around and flee together;
\q1 they will not stand {and fight},
\q2 because it will be a day when there will be disaster in Egypt,
\q2 the time when {I, Yahweh} will punish them.
\q1 \v 22 {The soldiers of} Egypt will flee, making only a faint sound, like a snake slithering away.
\q2 Their enemies will advance with a great army;
\q2 they will come against Egypt carrying axes, as woodcutters do.”
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh, declares this:
\q2 “Those enemy soldiers will bring down the soldiers {of Egypt}
\q1 as though they were woodcutters and the Egyptian soldiers were an immense forest of trees that cannot be explored.
\q1 {The enemy soldiers will be able to kill the numerous Egyptian soldiers} because their soldiers will be more numerous than a swarm of locusts;
\q2 which no one is able to count.
\q1 \v 24 The people of Egypt will be ashamed,
\q2 {because} Yahweh will allow the people from the northeast to conquer them.”
\p
\v 25 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelites worship, says this: “Pay attention! I am about to punish Amon, the god whom the people of the city of No worship. I will also punish Pharaoh, and Egypt, and the other gods that the Egyptians worship, and their kings. I will punish Pharaoh and everyone who trusts in him.
\v 26 I will let their enemies capture them, the people who want to kill them, that is, Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon and his officials. However, after that, people will live {in Egypt again} as they did long ago.” That is what Yahweh, declares.
\q1 \v 27 {Yahweh says,} “As for you people of Israel who serve me,
\q2 do not be at all fearful,
\q1 because I will surely save you and bring you back from distant places,
\q2 and I will bring your descendants home from the land where others have taken them as prisoners.
\q1 You people of Israel will return and live peacefully and safely again,
\q2 and no nation will cause you to be afraid.
\q1 \v 28 You people of Israel who serve me, do not be afraid, because I am with you {to help you}.”— This is what Yahweh declares.—“I will completely destroy all the nations where I have scattered you,
\q2 but I will not completely destroy you.
\q1 I will discipline you fairly,
\q2 but I will certainly not let you go without any punishment.”
\c 47
\s1 Yahweh gives a message about Philistia
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message to {me,} Jeremiah the prophet, about the people of Philistia. {He gave it to me} before the Egyptian army attacked and captured the city of Gaza.
\p
\v 2 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Look! An army is coming from the north,
\q2 that will cover the land like a flood.
\q1 Those soldiers will overrun the land and everything in it,
\q2 including every city and all the people who live there.
\q1 Everyone who lives in the land
\q2 will cry out and wail loudly.
\q1 \v 3 When the people hear the sound of the galloping hooves of the enemy’s stallions,
\q2 and the enemy’s chariots rattling and their wheels rumbling,
\q1 the fathers will not even turn back to rescue their own children,
\q2 because they will be so afraid.
\q1 \v 4 {That will happen} because the time will have come
\q2 when {I,} Yahweh will destroy all the people of Philistia
\q1 and will kill every one of them who could still help the cities of Tyre and Sidon.
\q1 I will destroy the people of Philistia,
\q2 those who survive along the seacoast, whose ancestors came {long ago} from the island of Crete.
\q1 \v 5 The people of Gaza will shave their heads bald to show that they are mourning.
\q1 {I, Yahweh will allow enemy soldiers} to destroy the city of Ashkelon
\q2 and all the people who remain in their valley.
\q1 You people of Ashkelon who survive
\q2 will go on cutting yourselves to show how deeply you grieve.”
\b
\q1 \v 6 {The people of Philistia say,} ‘Oh Yahweh,
\q2 we wish that you would {make our enemies stop killing us} with their swords!
\q1 Make them put their swords back into their sheaths
\q2 and keep them there!’
\q1 \v 7 {But I, Yahweh, say,} It would not be right for {your enemies’ swords} to stay in their sheaths,
\q2 because Yahweh has commanded your enemies {to do something more}.
\q1 He intends to tell them to attack {the people living in} the city of Ashkelon
\q2 and {in the other cities} along the coast.”
\c 48
\p
\v 1 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelites worship, says about Moab:
\q1 “Terrible things will happen to the town of Nebo;
\q2 {soon} enemy soldiers will ruin it!
\q1 They will capture the town of Kiriathaim, and its people will be humiliated.
\q2 the high wall around their city will be ruined, and {its people} will become ashamed.
\q1 \v 2 No one will boast about Moab anymore.
\q2 In the city of Heshbon their enemies are making plans to harm them.
\q2 {Those enemies say,} ‘Come, let us destroy Moab so that it is no longer a nation.’
\q1 You people of the town of Madmen, they will silence you as well;
\q2 enemy armies will chase after you.
\q1 \v 3 The people of the town of Horonaim will cry out.
\q2 {They will be wailing when their town} is completely destroyed!
\q1 \v 4 {I} will destroy the nation of Moab,
\q2 {and even} its little children will be distressed and cry.
\q1 \v 5 They will weep bitterly
\q2 as they ascend the steep path up to Luhith.
\q1 At the road down to Horonaim
\q2 their enemies will hear them crying {because their town is being} destroyed.
\q1 \v 6 Run away! Save your lives!
\q2 You will barely survive, and you will have nothing left, out in the wilderness.
\q1 \v 7 You people of Moab trusted in what you had accomplished and in the wealth you had stored up,
\q2 so enemies will capture you as well.
\q1 They will carry the idol of your god Chemosh away to a distant land,
\q2 and they will take his priests and his officials along with it.
\q1 \v 8 A destroying army will attack every city {in Moab},
\q2 and none of them will escape.
\q1 {That army} will ruin the cities in the valley and destroy the cities on the plateau,
\q2 because {I,} Yahweh, have said that this will happen.
\q1 \v 9 Help the people of Moab get away quickly, because they must escape!
\q2 Their cities will become an empty wasteland,
\q2 and no one will live in them.
\q1 \v 10 {I,} Yahweh will curse anyone who will not eagerly do what I desire.
\q2 I will curse anyone who refrains from using his sword to kill those those {who are in Moab}.
\q1 \v 11 The people of Moab have lived securely ever since they first became a nation.
\q2 No enemy has captured them and taken them away to another country.
\q1 So they have not changed.
\q2 They are {like} wine that people have left undisturbed {in a container to ferment} but not removed from its dregs and placed in another container and which therefore remains unchanged in taste and smell.
\q1 \v 12 So listen!”—Yahweh declares this—“The time is coming when I will send enemies against the people of Moab. Those enemies will destroy Moab and get rid of its people {like} people pouring wine out {on the ground} and then smashing the wine jars.
\q1 \v 13 Then the people of Moab will feel ashamed of {their god} Chemosh,
\q2 just as the people of Israel felt ashamed of {their idol} at Bethel that they had trusted in.
\q1 \v 14 {You people of Moab}, you should not boast saying, ‘We are mighty warriors,
\q2 and we are brave men who are ready for battle!’
\q1 \v 15 Enemies will devastate the country of Moab and attack its cities,
\q2 and they will slaughter its finest young men. I am the King, and my name is Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies. That is what I declare.
\q1 \v 16 Moab will be destroyed very soon!
\q1 \v 17 All you {people who live in nations} near Moab, all of you who know how famous Moab is,
\q2 should mourn for Moab,
\q1 and say, ‘How completely Moab’s mighty rule has ended’
\q1 \v 18 You people who live in the city of Dibon, stop being proud because people have honored you,
\q2 come down and sit on the dry, parched ground,
\q1 because the army that destroys Moab will attack your city
\q2 and tear down the high walls that protect you.
\q1 \v 19 You people who live in the city of Aroer,
\q2 stand beside the road and watch.
\q1 Ask the men and the women who are running away to save their lives;
\q2 ask them, ‘What has happened?’
\q1 \v 20 {Enemies of} the people of Moab have disgraced them, and they are dismayed.
\q2 Weep and cry out loudly!
\q1 Announce along the Arnon River
\q2 that {enemies} have devastated Moab.
\q1 \v 21 I am going to punish the cities {in Moab that are} on the plateau:
\q2 Holon, Jahaz, and Mephaath,
\q1 \v 22 Dibon, Nebo, and Beth Diblathaim,
\q1 \v 23 Kiriathaim, Beth Gamul, and Beth Meon,
\q1 \v 24 Kerioth, and Bozrah.
\q2 I am punishing every city in the land of Moab,
\q2 both the towns that are far away and the ones that are nearby.
\q1 \v 25 {I, Yahweh} will end the powerful reign of Moab;
\q2 {it will be as though} Moab will have a broken arm”—That is what Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 26 “{You people of Moab} have exalted yourselves against {me,} Yahweh, so {now} I will cause you all to {stagger like} drunken people. {You people of} Moab will wallow in your own vomit and people will laugh at you.
\v 27 You people of Moab, you ridiculed the people of Israel. You treated them as though they were thieves. Every time you spoke about them, you shook your head {to show your scorn}.
\q1 \v 28 You people who live in Moab,
\q2 leave your cities and go live in the cliffs.
\q1 Be like doves that make their nests
\q2 in the entrances of caves.
\q1 \v 29 We have {all} heard how proud the people of Moab are.
\q2 They are extremely arrogant and they think they are better than everyone else.
\q1 \v 30 I, {Yahweh} surely know”—Yahweh declares this—“how insolently they boast,
\q2 but their boasting is empty, and they will never accomplish what they claim.
\q1 \v 31 So I will wail for the people of Moab;
\q2 I will cry out for everyone who lives there,
\q2 and I will moan for the people of the city of Kir Hareseth.
\q1 \v 32 You people of the city of Sibmah, I will weep for you
\q2 even more than I did for the people of Jazer.
\q1 You have many vineyards. The branches of your vines extend all the way to the sea
\q2 and reach all the way to the sea of Jazer.
\q1 Now, however, a destroying army will ruin
\q2 the fruit that ripens in the summer and the grapes you would have harvested.
\q1 \v 33 No one is glad or shouts for joy anymore
\q2 in the orchards and fields of Moab.
\q1 I have stopped the grape juice from flowing from the winepresses.
\q2 People will not shout joyfully as they tread on the grapes;
\q2 people will shout, but they will not be shouting joyfully.
\p
\v 34 People will cry out loudly from the city of Heshbon to the town of Elealeh to the village of Jahaz, from the city of Zoar as far as the town of Horonaim and the village Eglath Shelishiyah. Even the water at the stream of Nimrim will dry up.
\v 35 I, {Yahweh,} will put a stop to everyone in Moab who offers sacrifices at the altars on the hilltops and who burns incense to their gods.” That is what Yahweh declares.
\v 36 “So I{, Yahweh,} lament for the people of Moab like {the sound of a funeral song that someone plays on} a flute. Yes I lament for the people of the city of Kir Hareseth like {the sound of a funeral song that someone plays on} a flute. Therefore the wealth they have gained will perish.
\v 37 Every man there will shave his head bald and cut off his beard {to show they are mourning}. They will all slash their hands, and wear sackcloth around their waists {to show that they are mourning}.
\v 38 On every rooftop in Moab and in all its public squares, people there will be people who are mourning, because I {Yahweh} will have destroyed the nation of Moab like someone smashes an {old} jar that no one wants {any more}.” This is what Yahweh declares.
\q1 \v 39 “The people of Moab will be greatly dismayed and will wail!
\q2 The people of Moab will turn away because they are ashamed!
\q1 Moab will become a nation that people ridicule.
\q2 The people in nearby countries will be horrified about what has happened there.”
\p
\v 40 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “Know that an enemy army will suddenly rush down and attack Moab by surprise,
\q2 like an eagle swooping down to attack {its unsuspecting prey}.
\q1 \v 41 Enemies will capture Kerioth
\q2 and seize its fortresses.
\q1 On that day the soldiers of Moab
\q2 will feel afraid, like a woman who is giving birth.
\q1 \v 42 I, {Yahweh,} will destroy Moab so that it is no longer a nation,
\q2 because its people boasted against {me,} Yahweh.
\q1 \v 43 Terrible things are going to happen to you people who live in Moab.
\q1 You will fall into pits and be caught in snares.”
\q2 This is what Yahweh declares.
\q1 \v 44 The Moabites who run away from the terrifying {invading army} will fall into deep pits,
\q2 and anyone who climbs out of the pits will be caught in a trap,
\q1 because the time is surely coming
\q2 when I will punish the people of Moab.” This is what Yahweh, declares.
\q1 \v 45 “The people who are running away will stop exhausted in the shadows of the walls of the city of Heshbon,
\q2 because the city will be on fire.
\q1 A fire will burn from the midst of the territory where King Sihon once ruled.
\q1 That fire will burn the foreheads of the people of Moab,
\q2 yes {it will burn} the heads of those people who loudly boast.
\q1 \v 46 Terrible things are going to happen to you, people of Moab!
\q2 You people who worship {the god} Chemosh will perish.
\q1 {Enemy soldiers} will capture your sons and your daughters,
\q2 and take them away as captives.
\q1 \v 47 But in the future I will restore the former prosperity of the people of Moab.” This is what Yahweh declares. {The message about} how Yahweh will judge Moab ends here.
\c 49
\p
\v 1 This is what Yahweh says about the people of Ammon:
\q1 “The Israelites certainly have plenty of descendants left
\q2 {to occupy the land of the tribe of Gad}.
\q1 So the king of Ammon had no right to seize the territory of the tribe of Gad,
\q2 and his people had no right to settle in the cities there.
\q1 \v 2 So pay attention”—Yahweh declares this— “the time is coming
\q2 when I will send an army shouting its battle cry against Rabbah, the capital city of the Ammonites.
\q1 That city will become a heap of rubble,
\q2 and enemy soldiers will burn down the villages around it.
\q1 Then the Israelites will take back their land from the people who took it away from them.”
\q2 {That is what} Yahweh says.
\q1 \v 3 Wail you people of the city of Heshbon,
\q2 because enemies have ruined the town of Ai!
\q1 You people who live in the villages around Rabbah, cry out!
\q2 Put on rough clothing to show that you are mourning, and weep,
\q1 and run back and forth inside the walls,
\q2 because enemies will take your king away to another country,
\q2 and they will take his priests and his officials away as well.
\q1 \v 4 You rebellious people of Ammon,
\q2 you should not boast about your fertile valleys.
\q1 You trust in the wealth you have stored up,
\q2 {and you say,} ‘Certainly no army will dare to attack us!’
\q1 \v 5 But listen to this! I am about to let every nation around you terrify you.”
\q2 This is what the Lord Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares.
\q1 “Your enemies will drive you out, and each of you will go straight ahead,
\q2 and no one will gather your scattered people together.
\q1 \v 6 Yet later on I will restore the former prosperity of the people of Ammon.”
\q2 That is what Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 7 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says about the people of Edom:
\q1 “The region of Teman no longer has any wise people!
\q2 There are no discerning people left who can give others {good} advice.
\v 8 Turn and run away you people who live in Dedan.
\q2 Go and hide yourselves in deep caves,
\q1 because I am going to cause disastrous things to happen to the descendants of Esau
\q2 at the time when I punish them.
\q1 \v 9 Those who harvest grapes,
\q2 always leave a few grapes on the vines.
\q1 When thieves come during the night,
\q2 they surely steal only as much as they want.
\q1 \v 10 However, I myself will take everything away from the descendants of Esau;
\q2 I will expose every place where they hide,
\q2 so that they will not be able to hide from me at all.
\q1 I will destroy their children, their relatives, and their neighbors,
\q2 and the people of Edom will no longer exist.
\q1 \v 11 You men of Edom will die, so leave your orphaned children with me, and I will keep them alive,
\q2 and your widows can trust me to take care of them.”
\p
\v 12 This is what Yahweh says: “Listen! Even people who did not deserve to suffer my punishment must certainly suffer it. So I will certainly not treat you people of Edom as though you were innocent. I will punish you severely.
\v 13 I have solemnly promised, swearing by my own name, that I will completely ruin the city of Bozrah. People will shudder in horror when they see it, they will insult it, and they will use its name when they curse others. All the towns that belong to it will remain ruins forever. That is what I, Yahweh, declare.”
\q1 \v 14 I, Jeremiah, have heard a message from Yahweh.
\q2 He has sent a messenger to the nations to tell them,
\q1 “Gather your armies together and attack Edom!
\q2 Get ready to go to war!”
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh says to the people of Edom, “Listen! I am making you the least important of all the nations,
\q2 and everyone will despise you.
\q1 \v 16 You have terrified other people, and that has fooled you;
\q2 you have become very proud,
\q1 because you live in caves in the rocky cliffs
\q2 and you occupy the highest hills.
\q1 Yet even if you build your homes as high up as an eagle builds its nest,
\q2 I will bring you down from there. That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 17 What happens to Edom will horrify everyone.
\q2 People who travel past it will gasp in shock,
\q2 and they will hiss to show their scorn at how badly enemies have ruined the land.
\q1 \v 18 Yahweh says, “I completely destroyed the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah and the towns near them, and I will destroy Edom in the same way.
\q2 After that, no one will live in Edom at all;
\q2 not even a traveler will stay there for a while.”
\q1 \v 19 Listen! An enemy will suddenly attack the people of Edom while they feel safe in their land, just as a lion comes up out of the thick bushes along the Jordan River and attacks a flock grazing in a good pasture.
\q1 In an instant I will chase the people of Edom out of their land,
\q2 and I will put whichever leader I choose in charge of them.
\q1 No one is equal to me, and no one can take me to court.
\q2 No ruler anywhere can stand against me.”
\q1 \v 20 So listen to what Yahweh has decided to do to the people of Edom,
\q2 to what he has planned to do to those who live in Teman:
\q1 Enemies will drag the Edomites away, even the youngest and weakest among them,
\q2 and Yahweh will turn their homeland into a wasteland.
\q1 \v 21 When the people of Edom fall in battle, the noise will be so loud
\q2 that the earth will shake.
\q1 People as far away as the Sea of Reeds
\q2 will hear the people of Edom crying out.
\q1 \v 22 Look! An enemy army will swoop down on the city of Bozrah like an eagle that swoops down and spreads its wings over its prey.
\q1 On that day the brave soldiers of Edom
\q2 will feel such terror that they will writhe like a woman suffering labor pains.
\p
\v 23 This is what Yahweh says about the city of Damascus:
\q1 “The people of the cities of Hamath and Arpad feel ashamed,
\q2 because they have heard bad news about Damascus.
\q1 They have lost all their courage;
\q2 they are as anxious and restless as the sea during a storm,
\q2 and they cannot calm down.
\q1 \v 24 The people of Damascus have become weak;
\q2 they have turned to run away, and they tremble with fear.
\q1 They suffer great anguish and pain,
\q2 like a woman who is giving birth to a child.
\q1 \v 25 How sad it is that the people have not abandoned that famous city,
\q2 the town that once made me glad!
\q1 \v 26 So enemies will kill her young men in the open squares of the city,
\q2 and on that day they will kill all her soldiers.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declare.
\q1 \v 27 I will set the walls of Damascus on fire,
\q2 and that fire will completely burn up the fortresses that King Ben Hadad built.”
\p
\v 28 This is what Yahweh says about the people of Kedar and about the kingdoms of Hazor, which King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon attacked:
\q1 “You soldiers, get ready and go attack the people of Kedar!
\q2 Destroy those tribes who live in the desert to the east!
\q1 \v 29 Those soldiers will seize the tents and the flocks of those people;
\q2 they will carry off for themselves their tent curtains, all their goods, and their camels.
\q1 People will shout at them,
\q2 ‘Terrible things are happening to us from every direction!’
\q1 \v 30 You people who live in Hazor, run away! Go far away!
\q2 Hide yourselves in deep caves. That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon has decided to attack you;
\q2 he has made a plan to destroy you.
\q1 \v 31 You soldiers of Babylon, get ready and attack that nation whose people live at ease and feel completely secure.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 They have no city gates and no bars to lock them;
\q2 they live all by themselves.
\q1 \v 32 You will take their camels as plunder,
\q2 and you will seize their large herds of livestock as spoils of war.
\q1 I will scatter in every direction those people who cut short the hair at the sides of their heads,
\q2 and I will bring disaster on them from every side. That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 33 Jackals will live in the ruins of Hazor,
\q2 and it will remain a wasteland forever.
\q1 No one will live there,
\q2 and not even a traveler will stay there for a while.”
\p
\v 34 Yahweh gave this message to Jeremiah the prophet about the people of Elam. He gave it to him soon after Zedekiah became the king of Judah.
\p
\v 35 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says:
\q1 “Listen! I am about to destroy the archers of Elam,
\q2 the men who first made that nation strong.
\q1 \v 36 I will bring enemies against Elam from every direction,
\q2 from all four corners of the world,
\q1 and I will scatter the people of Elam in every direction.
\q2 I will drive them out of their land,
\q2 and they will go to every nation on the earth.
\q1 \v 37 I will terrify the people of Elam
\q2 in front of their enemies, in front of those who are trying to kill them.
\q1 Because I am extremely angry with them, I will bring disaster on them.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 I will send soldiers with swords to pursue them
\q2 until I have destroyed all of them.
\q1 \v 38 I will rule and judge the people of Elam,
\q2 and I will destroy their king and their officials there.”
\q2 This is what Yahweh declares.
\q1 \v 39 “But in the future I will restore the former prosperity of the people of Elam.”
\q2 That is what Yahweh declares.
\c 50
\p
\v 1 Yahweh spoke this message about the city of Babylon and about the land of the Chaldeans, {that is, Babylonia,} and he gave it to Jeremiah the prophet to announce:
\q1 \v 2 “Tell the nations what will happen, and make sure that they hear it;
\q2 raise a flag to signal them, and do not keep any of it secret.
\q1 Tell them that enemies have captured Babylon,
\q2 and that its gods Bel and Marduk could not protect it at all.
\q1 Everyone can now see that the idols of Babylon are worthless
\q2 and that its images can do nothing to help anyone.
\q1 \v 3 A nation from the north will march against Babylon
\q2 and will turn its land into a place that horrifies everyone who sees it.
\q1 No one will live there anymore;
\q2 both the people and the animals will run away from it.
\q1 \v 4 I, Yahweh, declare that in those days, at that time,
\q2 the people of Israel and the people of Judah will come back together.
\q1 They will weep as they travel,
\q2 and they will come looking for me, Yahweh their God.
\q1 \v 5 They will ask which road leads to Zion
\q2 and will set out in that direction, because they have decided to go there.
\q1 They will urge one another to swear loyalty to me, Yahweh,
\q2 by making an agreement with me that will last forever,
\q2 an agreement that they will never forget.
\q1 \v 6 My people have become like sheep that have gotten lost.
\q2 Their leaders have caused them to stop worshiping me,
\q1 leading them to worship one idol after another,
\q2 until my people forgot that I was the one who kept them safe.
\q1 \v 7 Everyone who found them attacked them and pillaged them.
\q2 Their enemies said that they were not guilty of doing anything wrong,
\q1 because my people had sinned against me, Yahweh,
\q2 the one who had always cared for them rightly,
\q2 the one whom their ancestors had trusted to help them.
\q1 \v 8 You Israelites, get away from the city of Babylon,
\q2 and go out from the land of the Chaldeans!
\q1 Lead the way out, just as male goats walk ahead of the flock.
\q1 \v 9 Listen! I am preparing a group of powerful nations in the north,
\q2 and I am bringing them against Babylon.
\q1 They will line up in battle formation against the city,
\q2 and they will capture it.
\q1 Their soldiers will shoot their arrows skillfully,
\q2 and every arrow will hit its target.
\q1 \v 10 Enemies will plunder the land of the Chaldeans,
\q2 and everyone who plunders it will take away all that they want.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 11 You people of Babylon are celebrating and shouting for joy
\q2 because you plundered the people who belong to me.
\q1 You prance about restlessly, like a young cow treading out grain,
\q2 and you whinny like strong stallions.
\q1 \v 12 However, enemies will greatly shame and disgrace Babylon, the city that is like a mother to you.
\q2 It will surely become the least important of all the nations,
\q2 instead it will be a dry and empty wilderness where nothing grows.
\q1 \v 13 Because I, Yahweh, am extremely angry with Babylon,
\q2 no one will live there; the whole city will be just ruins.
\q1 Everyone who passes by Babylon will shudder in horror
\q2 and will hiss to show their scorn when they see how badly enemies have ruined it.
\q1 \v 14 All you archers, take your positions around Babylon!
\q2 Shoot at the city; do not save any of your arrows,
\q2 because its people have sinned against me, Yahweh.
\q1 \v 15 Shout your battle cry against the city from every side!
\q2 The people of Babylon are surrendering;
\q1 the towers that support its walls are collapsing,
\q2 and enemy soldiers are tearing its walls down.
\q1 I, Yahweh, am punishing Babylon for what it did; punish it!
\q2 Do to its people exactly what they have done to others!
\q1 \v 16 Do not let anyone in Babylon plant crops
\q2 or harvest the grain with a sickle.
\q1 Because the enemy army will attack so cruelly,
\q2 all the foreigners living there will run back to their own people;
\q2 each one will flee to his own land.
\q1 \v 17 The people of Israel are like sheep that lions have chased and scattered.
\q2 The king of Assyria was the first one who attacked and plundered them,
\q1 and after him Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon
\q2 crushed them completely.”
\p
\v 18 So this is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelites worship, says: “Listen! I am about to punish the king of Babylon and his land, just as I punished the king of Assyria.
\q1 \v 19 I will bring the people of Israel back to their own land,
\q2 where they will have plenty of food from the fields of Carmel and Bashan.
\q1 In the hill country of Ephraim and in Gilead
\q2 they will eat all that they want.
\q1 \v 20 I, Yahweh, declare that in those days, at that time,
\q2 if anyone searches for any wrong thing that the people of Israel have done, he will find nothing,
\q1 and if anyone searches for any sin that the people of Judah have committed, he will find nothing,
\q2 because I will forgive the few people whom I allow to survive.
\q1 \v 21 Attack the land of Merathaim!
\q2 Attack the people who live in Pekod!
\q1 Chase after them, kill them, and destroy them completely,
\q2 doing everything that I have commanded you.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 22 People hear the noise of battle throughout the land;
\q2 they hear the noise of terrible destruction.
\q1 \v 23 Babylon used to crush all the other nations, the way a hammer smashes things,
\q2 but now Yahweh has shattered Babylon itself!
\q1 The other nations shudder in horror
\q2 at what has happened to Babylon!”
\q1 \v 24 {Yahweh says, “}I set a trap for the people of Babylon, and I caught them
\q2 before they realized what was happening.
\q1 I found them and seized them,
\q2 because they had fought against me.
\q1 \v 25 Yahweh has brought out the armies that he will use to show how angry he is,
\q2 just as a warrior opens his storehouse and takes out his weapons.
\q1 The Lord Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has work to do
\q2 in the land of the Chaldeans.
\q1 \v 26 You enemies, come against Babylon from far away!
\q2 Break open the buildings where its people store their grain!
\q1 Pile up the ruins of the city in heaps, the way people pile up grain,
\q2 and destroy the city completely.
\q1 Do not let anyone there survive.
\q1 \v 27 Kill all of Babylon’s strongest soldiers;
\q2 send them all down to the slaughter.
\q1 How terrible it will be for them!
\q2 The time has come when Yahweh will punish them.
\q1 \v 28 Listen! People who have escaped and fled from the land of Babylon
\q2 are announcing in Zion how Yahweh our God has punished Babylon,
\q1 how he has punished its people for destroying his temple.
\q1 \v 29 {Yahweh says, “}Summon the archers to attack Babylon!
\q2 All you who shoot arrows, set up your camps all around the city.
\q1 Do not let anyone escape from it.
\q2 Punish its people for what they have done;
\q1 do to them everything that they have done to others,
\q2 because they arrogantly defied me, Yahweh,
\q2 the holy God whom the Israelites worship.
\q1 \v 30 So its young men will fall dead in the city squares,
\q2 and on that day all its soldiers will die.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.”
\q1 \v 31 The Lord Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says,
\q2 “Listen, you arrogant people of Babylon! I am against you,
\q1 because the time has now come
\q2 when I will punish you.
\q1 \v 32 You arrogant people will stumble and fall,
\q2 and no one will help you get up again.
\q1 I will start a fire in your cities,
\q2 and it will burn up everything around you.”
\q1 \v 33 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says:
\q1 “Enemies are cruelly oppressing the people of Israel and the people of Judah.
\q2 Everyone who captured them is holding them tightly
\q2 and refusing to let them go.
\q1 \v 34 However, the one who will set them free is strong;
\q2 his name is Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q1 He will certainly defend them when they plead their case,
\q2 so that he may bring rest to their land
\q2 and make the people who live in Babylon tremble.”
\q1 \v 35 Yahweh declares, “Enemy soldiers will come with swords
\q2 to kill the Chaldeans and the people who live in Babylon,
\q2 and also its officials and its wise men.
\q1 \v 36 They will kill the false prophets of Babylon,
\q2 and everyone will see that those prophets were fools.
\q1 They will attack Babylon’s warriors,
\q2 and those warriors will tremble with terror.
\q1 \v 37 They will kill Babylon’s horses and destroy its chariots,
\q2 and they will kill the foreign soldiers who fight in its army,
\q1 and those soldiers will lose all their courage.
\q2 Enemies will break into Babylon’s storehouses and carry off everything valuable.
\q1 \v 38 I will send a drought on Babylon’s rivers,
\q2 and they will dry up,
\q1 because the people there have filled their land with idols,
\q2 and those terrifying idols have made the people act like madmen.
\q1 \v 39 So wild animals of the desert will live there along with hyenas,
\q2 and ostriches will make their homes in the city.
\q1 No people will ever live there again;
\q2 no one will settle there for all time to come.
\q1 \v 40 I will destroy Babylon just as I destroyed the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah and the towns near them.
\q2 No one will live there;
\q2 no human being will ever stay there again.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 41 Look! An army is coming from the north.
\q2 I am preparing a great nation and many kings
\q2 from lands far away.
\q1 \v 42 Those soldiers carry bows and curved swords;
\q2 they are cruel and will not act mercifully toward anyone.
\q1 The noise they make is as loud as the roaring sea.
\q2 They ride on horses,
\q1 and they line up in battle formation like one man
\q2 to attack you people of Babylon.
\q1 \v 43 When the king of Babylon hears the reports about that army,
\q2 he will lose all his strength and courage.
\q1 He will feel great distress and anguish,
\q2 like a woman who is about to give birth.
\q1 \v 44 Look! I will attack suddenly, like a lion that comes out of the thick bushes along the Jordan River
\q2 and springs on sheep in a well-watered pasture.
\q1 In a moment I will chase the people of Babylon out of their land,
\q2 and then I will appoint over them the ruler whom I choose.
\q1 No one is like me, and no one can punish me for my actions.
\q2 No ruler anywhere is able to stand and oppose me.”
\q1 \v 45 So listen to what Yahweh has decided to do to the city of Babylon
\q2 and to what he has planned to do to the land of the Chaldeans:
\q1 Enemies will certainly drag away even the Babylonians’ children,
\q2 and Yahweh will certainly turn their homeland into a wasteland.
\q1 \v 46 When enemies capture Babylon, the noise will be so loud
\q2 that the whole earth will shake,
\q1 and people in all the nations
\q2 will hear the Babylonians crying out.
\c 51
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says this:
\q1 “Listen! I am about to bring {an army} against the city of Babylon
\q2 and against the people who live in the land of the Babylonians
\q2 that will destroy them {as quickly as} a powerful wind.
\q1 \v 2 I will send foreigners to attack Babylon.
\q2 Like a strong wind, they will scatter its people in every direction and leave its land empty.
\q1 They will surround that city on every side
\q2 when the time comes for me to destroy it.
\q1 \v 3 Do not give the archers of Babylon time to string their bows,
\q2 and do not let any soldier there stand up wearing his armor.
\q1 Do not spare Babylon’s young men.
\q2 Destroy its whole army completely, as a sacrifice.
\q1 \v 4 Enemies will kill Babylon’s soldiers in their own land.
\q2 The enemies will stab them, and let their bodies fall in the streets of the city.”
\q1 \v 5 This will happen because Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has not abandoned the people of Israel or the people of Judah.
\q2 He is still their God,
\q1 even though the Israelites throughout their land have sinned again and again
\q2 against the holy God whose people they are.
\q1 \v 6 Run away from the city of Babylon!
\q2 Each of you, run away to save your life!
\q1 Do not stay there and die when Yahweh punishes its people for their sins,
\q2 because the time has come for Yahweh to punish them as they deserve.
\q2 He is paying them back for what they have done.
\q1 \v 7 {When Yahweh used Babylon to severely punish every nation on the earth, it was as though} Babylon was a golden cup in Yahweh’s hand
\q2 that he made all of the nations of the earth drink wine from until they became drunk.
\q1 As a result, the people of those nations now behave like crazy people.
\q1 \v 8 But suddenly enemies will conquer the city of Babylon and destroy it.
\q2 {When that happens, Babylon’s enemies will mock its people by saying,} “Cry out loudly because of what has happened to Babylon!
\q1 Bring healing salve for its wounds;
\q2 maybe someone can heal it with medicine!”
\q1 \v 9 {The foreigners living there will say,} “We tried to heal Babylon, but it did not get well.
\q2 Let us abandon it, and let each of us return to his own land,
\q1 because its people have sinned so much that {it is as though} their sins have piled up to the sky,
\q2 and God is punishing them an equal amount.”
\q1 \v 10 {The Israelites will say,} “Yahweh has shown everyone that we were the ones who were doing what was right.
\q2 Come, let us go to Zion and tell the people there everything that Yahweh our God has done {for us}.”
\q1 \v 11 {You soldiers of Media,} sharpen your arrows!
\q2 Fill your quivers {with them}!
\q1 Yahweh has made the kings of Media determined to fight {against Babylon},
\q2 because he has decided to destroy the city of Babylon.
\q1 Yahweh will do this to repay its people {for what they did};
\q2 he is punishing them for what they did to his temple.
\q1 \v 12 Raise a signal flag to attack the walls of the city of Babylon!
\q2 Post more guards! Station men to keep watch!
\q2 Set soldiers in place to attack without warning,
\q1 because Yahweh is now doing exactly what he planned to do to the people who live in Babylon,
\q2 which is what he also said that he would do to them.
\q1 \v 13 The city of Babylon sits beside the great river and its many canals, and its people have become very wealthy.
\q2 But now the time for that city to exist has ended.
\q2 {It is as though} Babylon’s life is over.
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has solemnly sworn by himself,
\q1 “City of Babylon, I will certainly fill you with enemy soldiers, as many of them as a swarm of locusts,
\q2 and they will shout {triumphantly} over you {when they defeat you}.”
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh is the one who made the earth because he is powerful;
\q2 he set the world firmly in place because he is wise,
\q2 and because he understands everything, he created the heavens.
\q1 \v 16 When he thunders, the waters in the sky roar,
\q2 and he causes clouds to rise from the most distant places on the earth.
\q1 He sends lightning along with the rain,
\q2 and he sends out the wind from where he keeps it.
\q1 \v 17 People tend to be stupid and ignorant.
\q2 Even craftsmen who work with gold should feel disgraced because of the idols that they carve,
\q1 because the idols that they cast deceive people;
\q2 they are not alive at all.
\q1 \v 18 Those idols are worthless, and people ought to laugh at them.
\q2 When the time comes for Yahweh to punish {the people who worship} them, he will destroy them all.
\q1 \v 19 The God whom the descendants of Jacob worship is not like those idols,
\q2 because he is the one who formed everything that exists,
\q2 including the tribe that he chose to belong to him.
\q1 His name is Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q1 \v 20 {To the army of Babylon, Yahweh would say,}
\q1 “I have used you as a soldier uses his war club to smash {his enemies};
\q2 by means of you I have destroyed {the armies of other} nations,
\q2 yes, by means of you I have conquered kingdoms.
\q1 \v 21 By means of you I have destroyed horses and the men who ride them;
\q2 by means of you I have destroyed chariots and the men who drive them.
\q1 \v 22 By means of you I have destroyed men and women,
\q2 old people and young people,
\q2 young men and young women.
\q1 \v 23 By means of you I have destroyed shepherds and their flocks,
\q2 farmers and the oxen that they plow with,
\q2 governors and the officials who serve under them.
\q1 \v 24 But now{, people of Israel,} I will punish the people of the city of Babylon and all of the people of that entire country for all of the terrible things that they did in Zion. I will do this in a way that you people of Israel will see it happen. That is what I, Yahweh, declare.”
\q1 \v 25 {To the people of Babylon,} Yahweh would say, “Listen, you people of Babylon! I am your enemy.
\q2 You have seemed as permanent and dominant as a mountain as you have destroyed nations all over the earth.
\q1 But now I will fight against you;
\q2 I will destroy you {as quickly} as if I had thrown you off of a cliff,
\q2 and I will leave your city as nothing but a heap of burned rubble.
\q1 \v 26 {I will destroy your city so thoroughly that} none of your stones will be good enough to use for a cornerstone
\q2 or for any part of the foundation of a new building.
\q1 No, your city will lie empty forever.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.”
\q1 \v 27 Raise flags in the land {to summon armies}!
\q2 Blow ram’s horns among the nations {to call them to war}!
\q1 Dedicate nations to fight against Babylon!
\q2 Summon the kingdoms of Ararat, Minni, and Ashkenaz to attack it!
\q1 Appoint an officer to lead the army against it!
\q2 Bring up so many horses that they look like a swarm of locusts!
\q1 \v 28 Dedicate {other} nations to fight against Babylon:
\q2 the kings of Media, their governors and all their officials,
\q2 and every land that they rule.
\q1 \v 29 {When those armies attack Babylon, it will be as though} the ground will shake violently,
\q2 because Yahweh will certainly do to the city of Babylon everything that he has planned.
\q1 He will make the land of Babylon completely desolate,
\q2 so that no one lives there at all.
\q1 \v 30 The mighty men of Babylon will stop fighting;
\q2 they will hide inside their fortresses.
\q1 They will become weak and {as helpless} as women.
\q2 Their enemies will set the buildings of the city on fire
\q1 and will break the bars that hold its gates shut.
\q1 \v 31 One messenger will run to give his message to another messenger,
\q2 and he will run to give his report to another messenger,
\q1 in order to tell the king of Babylon
\q2 that enemies have captured every part of his city.
\q1 \v 32 {He will also tell the king} that enemies now control the places where people cross the river,
\q2 that they have burned the marshland,
\q2 and that his soldiers are panicking.
\q1 \v 33 Indeed, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, the God whom the Israelites worship, says this:
\q1 “The city of Babylon is like a threshing floor
\q2 that people are preparing so that they can thresh wheat there.
\q1 Very soon the time will come
\q2 for me {to send an army} to kill the people there, as farmers cut down grain at harvest time and have their oxen trample it on the threshing floor.”
\q1 \v 34 {The people of Jerusalem say,} “{It is as though we are fruit and} Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon has devoured us and sucked the juice out of us,
\q1 swallowing us as greedily as a huge sea creature swallows its prey.
\q2 He took all of our finest possessions to satisfy himself, as though filling his belly with our delicious food.
\q2 He has taken everything that we owned, so that we are like an empty dish.
\q2 He left nothing for us, as though he rinsed the dish of every crumb.”
\q1 \v 35 The people who live in Zion say,
\q2 “May the people of Babylon suffer for the violence that they did to us and to our relatives!”
\q1 The people of Jerusalem say,
\q2 “May Yahweh hold the people who live in the land of the Chaldeans responsible for murdering our people!”
\q1 \v 36 So Yahweh says this to the people of Jerusalem:
\q1 “I myself am about to fight for you;
\q2 I will completely pay back your enemies for what they did to you.
\q1 I will dry up Babylon’s rivers
\q2 and make its springs stop flowing.
\q1 \v 37 The city of Babylon will become piles {of rubble}.
\q2 It will be a place where jackals make their dens.
\q1 Everyone who sees it will feel horrified {at what happened there} and will hiss {as they go by it}.
\q2 No one will live there.
\q1 \v 38 All the people of Babylon act fiercely {as they seek to exploit people of other nations},
\q2 like young lions {looking for prey to devour}.
\q1 \v 39 When they very greatly desire to exploit others, I will {make other nations available to them, as though I have} set out a great banquet for them.
\q2 {It will be as though} I will make them so drunk {from exploiting other nations} that they shout with glee,
\q1 and then {It will be as though} they will fall asleep and die;
\q2 they will never be a nation again.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.
\q1 \v 40 I will cause armies to kill them,
\q2 as easily as someone leads lambs and rams and male goats to the place where someone slaughters them.”
\q1 \v 41 {Then people will say,} “Enemies have captured the city of Babylon!
\q2 They have seized the city that everyone on the earth used to praise!
\q1 What a horrible sight Babylon is now
\q2 to everyone in the other nations!
\q1 \v 42 Enemy armies have swept over the city of Babylon
\q2 and have completely overwhelmed it, just as the roaring waves of the sea cover the land.
\q1 \v 43 The cities of Babylonia have become ruins,
\q2 a dry and empty wilderness.
\q1 No one lives in that land,
\q2 and no one even travels through those cities.
\q1 \v 44 Yahweh says, “I will punish Bel, the god whom the people of Babylon worship,
\q2 and I will force him to give back everything that he swallowed.
\q1 People from other nations will no longer crowd together to worship him.
\q2 The wall around the city of Babylon will fall down.
\q1 \v 45 My people, get out of that city!
\q2 Each of you must escape and save your own life,
\q2 because I, Yahweh, am extremely angry with the people there!
\q1 \v 46 Do not lose your courage, and do not be afraid
\q2 when you hear people reporting what is happening in that land.
\q1 People will bring one report this year
\q2 and another report the next year,
\q1 reports that people are attacking one another violently in the land
\q2 and that one ruler is fighting against another ruler.
\q1 \v 47 So listen! The time is coming soon
\q2 when I will punish the people of Babylon for the idols that they carved.
\q1 Everyone in their land will feel ashamed,
\q2 and enemies will kill its people, whose bodies will lie in the middle of the city.
\q1 \v 48 Then everyone in heaven and everyone on the earth will shout joyfully
\q2 because of what has happened to the city of Babylon,
\q1 because the armies that will destroy it will come from the north.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.”
\q1 \v 49 The people of Babylon must die because they killed the people of Israel,
\q2 just as they killed people in nations everywhere.
\q1 \v 50 You Israelites whom enemies did not kill in the war, leave that city and go!
\q2 Do not wait any longer!
\q1 Even though you are far away from your own land, keep remembering Yahweh and worshiping him,
\q2 and keep thinking about the city of Jerusalem.
\q1 \v 51 The Israelites say, “We feel ashamed, because we have heard people insult us;
\q2 we cannot lift up our faces, because we feel so much shame,
\q1 since foreigners entered the holy places
\q2 in the temple of Yahweh and defiled them.”
\q1 \v 52 Yahweh replies, “So listen! The time is coming
\q2 when I will punish the people of Babylon for the idols that they carved,
\q1 and all through their land
\q2 the people whom enemies wound will groan in pain.
\q1 \v 53 Even if the people of Babylon could build their city as high as the sky
\q2 and make its defenses impossible to reach,
\q1 I would still send armies to destroy it.
\q2 That is what I, Yahweh, declare.”
\q1 \v 54 Listen! People in the city of Babylon are crying out!
\q2 Enemies are destroying everything in the land of the Chaldeans!
\q1 \v 55 Yahweh is ruining the city of Babylon
\q2 and silencing the loud noise of its crowds.
\q1 The enemies advance with a roar like floodwaters;
\q2 they shout so loudly that everyone can hear them.
\q1 \v 56 An army has come to destroy the city of Babylon.
\q2 Enemies have captured its strongest soldiers
\q2 and have broken their bows to pieces.
\q1 Yahweh is a God who pays people back for what they have done;
\q2 he will certainly give the Babylonians what they deserve.
\q1 \v 57 Yahweh says, “I will make the officials and the wise men of Babylon drunk,
\q2 along with its governors, its other officials, and its warriors,
\q1 and they will fall asleep and die;
\q2 they will never wake up again .
\q1 That is what I, the King, declare;
\q2 my name is Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.”
\q1 \v 58 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says this:
\q1 “Enemies will completely tear down the thick wall around the city of Babylon,
\q2 and they will burn its high gates.
\q1 The peoples who built that city worked hard for nothing;
\q2 fire will consume everything that the nations made,
\q2 and all their work will only exhaust them.”
\p
\v 59 Jeremiah the prophet gave this message to Seraiah son of Neriah, son of Mahseiah, when Seraiah went to Babylon with Zedekiah the king of Judah. This happened in the fourth year that Zedekiah ruled Judah. Seraiah was the official who arranged where the king would stay each night.
\v 60 Jeremiah had written on one scroll all the disasters that would come on Babylon; he wrote down all these words about Babylon.
\p
\v 61 Jeremiah said to Seraiah, “When you arrive in Babylon, read all these words aloud so that the people there hear them,
\v 62 and then say, ‘Yahweh, you yourself said that you would destroy this place, so that nothing would live here anymore, neither people nor animals; you said that it would lie empty forever.’
\v 63 When you have finished reading this scroll, tie a stone to it and throw it into the middle of the Euphrates River.
\v 64 Then say, ‘Babylon will sink like this and will never rise again, because of the disaster that Yahweh will bring on it, and its people will collapse from exhaustion.’” That is the end of the messages that Jeremiah gave.
\c 52
\p
\v 1 Zedekiah was 21 years old when he became king, and he ruled in Jerusalem for eleven years. His mother’s name was Hamutal. She was the daughter of a man whose name was Jeremiah. She was from the city of Libnah.
\v 2 Zedekiah did many things that Yahweh considered to be evil, just as {his older brother} Jehoiakim had done.
\v 3 Yahweh made these things happen to the people who lived in Jerusalem and other parts of Judah because he was very angry with them. He finally allowed the Babylonians to force them to leave their country. That happened when Zedekiah rebelled against the king of Babylon.
\v 4 {Because Zedekiah had rebelled,} King Nebuchadnezzar led his whole army to attack Jerusalem. They arrived there after Zedekiah had been ruling for nine years, on the tenth day of the tenth month of that year. They surrounded the city and built earthen ramps against its walls to help them attack it.
\v 5 The Babylonians kept attacking the city {for the next two years,} until Zedekiah had been ruling for eleven years.
\p
\v 6 By the ninth day of the fourth month of that year, the famine in the city had become very severe. There was no more food for the people to eat.
\v 7 Then the Babylonian soldiers broke through the city wall and entered the city. {King Zedekiah and} the Judean soldiers tried to escape at night {when the Babylonians would not see them}. They used the gate near the king’s garden to go through the inner and outer walls of the city. Zedekiah {and the soldiers} tried to escape through the Jordan River valley. But the Babylonians had surrounded the city{, and some of their soldiers saw where they went}.
\v 8 So Babylonian soldiers chased after them. They caught up to King Zedekiah in the plains around Jericho. His soldiers ran away {instead of fighting to defend him}.
\v 9 So the Babylonian soldiers were able to capture King Zedekiah. They brought him to Nebuchadnezzar, who was in the city of Riblah in the region of Hamath. Nebuchadnezzar declared that Zedekiah was guilty of rebelling against him.
\v 10 There at Riblah, King Nebuchadnezzar forced Zedekiah to watch as his soldiers killed all of his sons. They also killed many Judean leaders whom they had captured.
\v 11 Then the soldiers gouged out Zedekiah’s eyes and put bronze chains on his hands and feet. King Nebuchanezzar brought him ]to the city of Babylon and put him in prison for the rest of his life.
\p
\v 12 On the seventh day of the fifth month of that year, after Nebuchadnezzar had been ruling for nineteen years, Nebuzaradan arrived in Jerusalem. He was one of King Nebuchadnezzar’s most important officials. He commanded the royal bodyguards.
\v 13 He ordered his soldiers to burn down Yahweh’s temple, the king’s palace, and all the important buildings in Jerusalem.
\v 14 Then the Babylonian soldiers who had come to Jerusalem with Nebuzaradan tore down the walls all around Jerusalem.
\v 15 After that, Nebuzaradan and his soldiers took to Babylon some poor people and the people who were still alive in Jerusalem. They also took other people who lived in the region of Judah. Some Judeans had previously surrendered to the Babylonian army, and they took them away too.
\v 16 But Nebuzaradan allowed some of the very poor people to stay in Judah to take care of the vineyards and to plant crops in the fields.
\p
\v 17 The Babylonian soldiers broke into pieces the bronze pillars, the carts, and the giant bronze water basin that people called the sea. Those things had been in the temple courtyard. They took the bronze to Babylon.
\v 18 They also took the pots, shovels, wick snuffers, bowls, incense dishes, and other bronze items that the priests used for their work in the temple.
\v 19 The soldiers also took away from the temple all the items that were pure gold or pure silver. These included the basins, the pans for carrying hot coals, the bowls, the pots, the lampstands, the incense dishes, and the cups.
\p
\v 20 There was too much bronze for the Babylonians to weigh in the items that King Solomon had commanded his workers to make for Yahweh’s temple. Those items included the two pillars, the giant water basin that people called the sea, the twelve bronze oxen under it, and the carts.
\v 21 Each pillar was eight and one quarter meters tall and five and one half meters around. They were hollow, and their sides were nearly eight centimeters thick.
\v 22 The bronze decorative top of each pillar was two and one quarter meters tall. Bronze strands in the shape of nets and bronze ornaments in the shape of pomegranates surrounded the pillar tops. Each pillar had the same decorations.
\v 23 There were 24 pomegranates on each side of each pillar, for a total of 96. {There was also a pomegranate on each corner, so} there was a total of 100 pomegranates on each net.
\p
\v 24 Nebuzaradan took some prisoners. These included the high priest Seraiah, Zephaniah, who was another leading priest, and the three Levites who had supervised the temple courtyard entrance.
\v 25 The prisoners also included the royal official responsible for the army, seven of the king’s advisors who had not fled, the commander who recorded the names of Judean men as they came to serve as soldiers, and 60 men who were serving as soldiers and were still in the city.
\v 26 Nebuzaradan took them all as prisoners to the king of Babylon at the city of Riblah.
\v 27 There at the city of Riblah in the province of Hamath, the king of Babylon had his soldiers kill all of the prisoners. That is what happened when the Babylonians forcefully took the people of Judah away from their land.
\v 28 This is how many people Nebuchadnezzar had his soldiers take away to Babylon. When he had been ruling for almost seven years, they took away 3,023 people from Judah.
\v 29 When he had been ruling for almost 18 years, his soldiers took away 832 more people from Jerusalem.
\v 30 When Nebuchadnezzar had been ruling for almost 23 years, Nebuzaradan, the commander of his royal bodyguards, took away 745 more people from Judah. In total, the Babylonians took away 4,600 people.
\p
\v 31 Later, when King Jehoiachin of Judah had been a prisoner in Babylon for 37 years, Nebuchadnezzar’s son Evil Merodach became the king of Babylon. During his first year as king, on the twenty-fifth day of the twelfth month, he treated Jehoiachin favorably. He released him from prison.
\v 32 He reassured Jehoiachin that he would treat him well, and he gave him a position of greater honor than the other kings whom the Babylonians had {conquered and} brought to their royal court.
\v 33 He gave Jehoiachin new clothes to replace the clothes that he had been wearing in prison, and he allowed Jehoiachin to eat meals with him every day for the rest of his life.
\v 34 The king of Babylon also gave Jehoiachin money to buy the things he needed. He gave him a regular amount that was sufficient for his needs. The king continued to do that until Jehoiachin died. 